tors and the denominators by the appropriate factors.", "That gives me $\\frac{2 + (\\cos 20^\\circ)^2 - (\\sin 20^\\circ)^2}{((\\cos 20^\\circ)^2 - (\\sin 20^\\circ)^2)(1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ)}$.", "I wonder if I can simplify this expression further.", "I notice that the numerator has a term that is the same as the first factor of the denominator, so maybe I can cancel them out.", "That gives me $\\frac{1}{1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ}$.", "But wait, this looks familiar.", "Isn't this the same as the second term in the original expression?", "Yes, it is! That means I've found a nice simplification of the expression.", "I can now use the identity $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$ to rewrite the denominator as $\\sin^2 20^\\circ$.", "That gives me the final answer.", "# Answer\n\n$\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ}$"], "pre_generated_answer": "$\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ}$", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.6969029380062555e-19}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks like it involves some trigonometric identities, so I'll start by reviewing the ones I know.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One useful identity is that $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$ for any angle $\\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Another one is that $\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "And a third one is that $\\cos 2\\theta = \\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can use these identities to simplify the expression in the problem.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Let me try to rewrite each term using the identity for $\\cos 2\\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the first term, I have $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{(\\cos 20^\\circ)^2 - (\\sin 20^\\circ)^2}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I have\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{(\\cos 20^\\circ)^2 - (\\sin 20^\\circ)^2}\\]\nand\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{(\\sin 20^\\circ)^2}\\]\nand\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{(2\\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ)^2}\\]\nby substituting $\\theta = 20^\\circ$ in the identity for $\\sin 2\\theta$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I let $\\theta = 10^\\circ$, then $\\cos 2\\theta = \\cos 20^\\circ$, so I can write\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin^2 10^\\circ}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I have $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}(1 + \\cos 20^\\circ)} = \\frac{2}{1 + \\cos 20^\\circ}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The first term is $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ}$, which I can write as $\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 20^\\circ}$ using the identity.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 355418, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2ae76b3f-eaf3-4a8e-901c-9ce4095c2f7c", "timestamp": "2022-11-16T16:06:33.200745", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Compute\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can write\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} &= \\frac{2}{1 + \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 (1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)}{(1 + \\cos 20^\\circ)(1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 (1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)}{1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ},\n\\end{align*}so\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= \\frac{2 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cos^2 20^\\circ (3 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ)}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ + 1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}.\n\\end{align*}By the triple angle formula,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\cos 60^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\cos (3 \\cdot 20^\\circ) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cos^3 20^\\circ - 3 \\cos 20^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}which means $8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ = 6 \\cos 20^\\circ + 1.$  Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ + 1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 6 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 6 \\cos 20^\\circ}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 (1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ) \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\cos 20^\\circ - 4 \\cos^3 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\cos 20^\\circ - 3 \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{12}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "12", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the angles are multiples of 10 degrees, and that there is a symmetry between cosine and sine, so I wonder if there is a trigonometric identity that can simplify this expression.", "I recall that $\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1$, and that $\\cos 2x = \\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x$.", "Maybe I can use these to rewrite the terms in the sum.", "For the first term, I have $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = \\frac{\\cos^2 10^\\circ + \\sin^2 10^\\circ}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} = 1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ$, using the fact that $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$.", "For the second term, I have $\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin^2 20^\\circ + \\cos^2 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} = 1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ$, using the fact that $\\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$.", "For the third term, I can use the angle-doubling identity to get $\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 (2 \\cdot 20^\\circ)} = \\frac{1}{(2\\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ)^2} = \\frac{1}{4\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ}$.", "Now I can add up the three terms and see if anything cancels out or simplifies.", "I get\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} = 1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ + 1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\frac{1}{4\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ}.\\]", "I notice that the last term can be rewritten as $\\frac{\\tan^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 20^\\circ}{4\\tan^2 20^\\circ \\cot^2 20^\\circ}$, using the fact that $\\tan x \\cot x = 1$.", "Then I can combine the second and fourth terms, and the third and fifth terms, to get\n\\[2 + \\frac{\\tan^2 10^\\circ + \\tan^2 20^\\circ}{\\tan^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{\\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 10^\\circ}{\\cot^2 10^\\circ}.\\]", "I see that this is almost the same as the original expression, except that the angles are swapped between the numerator and the denominator.", "This makes me think that maybe there is a symmetry in the expression that can be exploited.", "I try to rewrite the expression in terms of a single angle, say $\\theta = 10^\\circ$.", "Then I have\n\\[2 + \\frac{\\tan^2 \\theta + \\tan^2 (2\\theta)}{\\tan^2 (2\\theta)} + \\frac{\\cot^2 (2\\theta) + \\cot^2 \\theta}{\\cot^2 \\theta}.\\]", "I notice that if I swap $\\theta$ and $2\\theta$, the expression does not change, because $\\tan (2\\theta) = \\frac{2\\tan \\theta}{1 - \\tan^2 \\theta}$ and $\\cot (2\\theta) = \\frac{\\cot^2 \\theta - 1}{2\\cot \\theta}$.", "This means that the expression is equal to itself divided by 4, because I can write it as\n\\[\\frac{1}{4}\\left(2 + \\frac{\\tan^2 (2\\theta) + \\tan^2 \\theta}{\\tan^2 \\theta} + \\frac{\\cot^2 \\theta + \\cot^2 (2\\theta)}{\\cot^2 (2\\theta)}\\right).\\]", "Therefore, the expression must be equal to 4, because multiplying both sides by 4 gives the same expression.", "# Answer\n\n4"], "pre_generated_answer": "4", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 4.1337428828108285e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the angles are multiples of 10 degrees, and that there is a symmetry between cosine and sine, so I wonder if there is a trigonometric identity that can simplify this expression.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This problem involves trigonometric identities and angles. A good strategy is to look for ways to simplify the expression using identities or common factors.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To simplify this expression, I need to use some trigonometric identities.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the angles 10, 20, and 40 are all multiples of 10, so I wonder if there is some kind of pattern or identity that relates them.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the given expression involves cosines and sines of angles in multiples of 10 degrees. This makes me think of some identities that relate them, such as the double angle formula, the half angle formula, and the angle sum and difference formula.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 467303, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5b158ac4-13d1-40db-8987-3856a5926c39", "timestamp": "2022-11-16T16:02:39.356217", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Compute\n\\[\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can write\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} &= \\frac{2}{1 + \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 (1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)}{(1 + \\cos 20^\\circ)(1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 (1 - \\cos 20^\\circ)}{1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ},\n\\end{align*}so\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= \\frac{2 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cos^2 20^\\circ (3 - 2 \\cos 20^\\circ)}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ + 1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}.\n\\end{align*}By the triple angle formula,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\cos 60^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\cos (3 \\cdot 20^\\circ) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cos^3 20^\\circ - 3 \\cos 20^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}which means $8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ = 6 \\cos 20^\\circ + 1.$  Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 8 \\cos^3 20^\\circ + 1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 6 \\cos 20^\\circ}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos^2 20^\\circ - 6 \\cos 20^\\circ}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 (1 - \\cos^2 20^\\circ) \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\cos 20^\\circ - 4 \\cos^3 20^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{4 \\cos 20^\\circ - 3 \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12 \\cos 20^\\circ - 6}{\\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{12}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "12", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks like it involves some trigonometric identities, so I'm going to recall some of the basic ones that might be useful.", "One identity is that $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$, which can be rearranged to get $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta} = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\theta}$ or $\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\theta} = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta}$.", "Another identity is that $\\sin(2\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$, which can be used to find the values of $\\sin 20^\\circ$ and $\\sin 40^\\circ$ in terms of $\\sin 10^\\circ$ and $\\cos 10^\\circ$.", "Using these identities, I can rewrite the expression as follows:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ}\\right) + \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 (2 \\cdot 10^\\circ)}\\right) + \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 (2 \\cdot 20^\\circ)}\\right)\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\left(2\\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ\\right)^2} + \\frac{1}{\\left(2\\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ\\right)^2}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{4\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\cos^2 20^\\circ}\\\\\n\\end{align*}", "Now I notice that the last two terms have a common factor of $4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ$, which is actually equal to $\\sin^2 20^\\circ$ by the double angle identity.", "So I can simplify the expression further by combining those terms:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ} &= 3 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{\\sin^2 20^\\circ + \\cos^2 20^\\circ}{4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ}}{4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{\\frac{1 + \\sin^2 10^\\circ}{\\sin^2 10^\\circ}}{4\\sin^2 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ}\\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1 + \\sin^2 10^\\circ}{4\\sin^4 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ}\\\\\n\\end{align*}", "I don't think I can simplify this expression any further, so I'll leave it like this.", "However, if the problem asked for a numerical value, I would use a calculator to find the value of $\\sin 10^\\circ$ and then plug it into the expression.", "I would also round the answer to the appropriate number of significant figures or decimal places, depending on the context.", "# Answer\n\n\\[\\frac{1 + \\sin^2 10^\\circ}{4\\sin^4 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ} + 3\\]"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\[\\frac{1 + \\sin^2 10^\\circ}{4\\sin^4 10^\\circ \\cos^2 10^\\circ} + 3\\]", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00015099729762201562}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks like it involves some trigonometric identities, so I'm going to recall some of the basic ones that might be useful.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One identity is that $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$, which can be rearranged to get $\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta} = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\theta}$ or $\\frac{1}{\\sin^2 \\theta} = 1 + \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "One identity that I always remember is the Pythagorean identity, which says that for any angle $\\theta$, $\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One identity that comes to mind is the double angle formula for cosine: $\\cos 2\\theta = 1 - 2\\sin^2 \\theta$ or $\\cos 2\\theta = 2 \\cos^2 \\theta - 1$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I know that $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$ for any angle $\\theta$, and that $\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta$ for any angle $\\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I know that \\(\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\\) for any angle \\(\\theta\\), so I can use that to rewrite an expression like \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta}\\) as \\(\\frac{\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta}{\\cos^2 \\theta}\\) and then split it into two fractions: \\(\\frac{\\sin^2 \\theta}{\\cos^2 \\theta} + \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 \\theta}\\).", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 249325, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "e7dd34f2-fa17-4e7e-bc66-ac3d5ca993a4", "timestamp": "2022-11-16T17:00:20.722523", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can add 4 to both sides to get\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + 1 + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + 1 + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} + 1 = 2010x.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[\\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x.\\]We see that $x = 0$ is a solution (which does not affect our sum).  Otherwise, we can divide both sides by $x$:\n\\[\\frac{x}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 4} = 2010.\\]Clearing the denominators, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n&x(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) \\\\\n&\\quad = 2010(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\n\\end{align*}This expands to\n\\[4x^7 + \\dotsb = 2010x^8 + \\dotsb,\\]where only terms that have degree 7 or greater are shown.  Then\n\\[2010x^8 - 4x^7 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots is $\\frac{4}{2010} = \\boxed{\\frac{2}{1005}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{2}{1005}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the left-hand side of the equation is a rational function with four simple poles at x = 1, 2, 3, and 4.", "This suggests that I could use partial fraction decomposition to rewrite the left-hand side as a sum of terms of the form \\(\\frac{A}{x - a}\\) where A and a are constants.", "To find the constants A, I could multiply both sides of the equation by the denominator of the left-hand side, which is \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)\\).", "This would give me a polynomial equation of degree 8.", "However, before doing that, I notice that the right-hand side of the equation has a very large coefficient for the linear term, 2010x.", "This makes me wonder if there is a simpler way to approach this problem.", "I realize that if I want to find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation, I don't actually need to find the individual solutions.", "I can use the fact that the sum of the roots of a polynomial equation is equal to the negative of the coefficient of the second-highest degree term, divided by the leading coefficient.", "In this case, the leading coefficient of the polynomial equation that I would get after multiplying by the denominator is 1, and the second-highest degree term is 2010x.", "So the sum of the roots is -2010.", "Therefore, the sum of all complex solutions of the original equation is also -2010, since the equation is equivalent to the polynomial equation after multiplying by the denominator.", "# Answer\n\n-2010"], "pre_generated_answer": "-2010", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0001427875710081284}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the left-hand side of the equation is a rational function with four simple poles at x = 1, 2, 3, and 4.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This is a very complicated looking equation, but maybe I can simplify it by finding a common denominator for the fractions on the left side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This is a pretty intimidating equation, but I notice that the left side has a lot of fractions with x^2 in the denominator. Maybe I can combine them into a single fraction by finding a common denominator.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This is a very tricky equation, and it is not clear how to simplify it or solve it directly.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This is a pretty complicated-looking equation, but maybe I can start by simplifying the left-hand side. I see that every term has a denominator of the form $x^2 - k$ for some constant $k$, so maybe I can put them all over a common denominator by finding the least common multiple of these expressions.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 174461, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "36d8765f-fcf0-4a4c-a23e-9e57ed9bc074", "timestamp": "2022-11-16T07:28:25.237177", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The data in the table below shows the percent of bus riders in a survey of Central H.S. students; 300 males and 300 females in each grade were surveyed. For which grade is the number of its male bus riders closest to $135\\%$ of the number of its female bus riders? \\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}\\hline\nGrade & Males & Females \\\\ \\hline\n$9^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade & $41.1\\%$ & $39.4\\%$ \\\\ \\hline\n$10^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade & $34.4\\%$ & $33.1\\%$ \\\\ \\hline\n$11^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade & $20.6\\%$ & $13.8\\%$ \\\\ \\hline\n$12^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade & $11.6\\%$ & $8.6\\%$ \\\\ \\hline\n\\end{tabular}", "ground_truth_solution": "The grade level in which the number of male bus riders is closest to $135\\%$ of the number of female bus riders is the same grade level in which the percentage of male bus riders is closest to $135\\%$ of the percentage of female bus riders. To find what percentage is $135\\%$ of the percentage of female bus riders, we multiply each percentage by $1.35.$ In ninth grade, the percentage is $39.4\\cdot1.35=53.19$ percent. In tenth grade, the percentage is $33.1\\cdot1.35=44.685$ percent. In eleventh grade, the percentage is $13.8\\cdot1.35=18.63$ percent. In twelfth grade, the percentage is $8.6\\cdot1.35=11.61$ percent. From inspection, we see that $135\\%$ of the percentage of female bus riders in twelfth grade is closest to the percentage of male bus riders in twelfth grade. So the answer is $\\boxed{12}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "12", "pre_generated_steps": ["I want to find the grade where the number of male bus riders is about $1.35$ times the number of female bus riders.", "I could calculate the exact number of bus riders for each grade and gender, but that would take too long and I don't need to be precise.", "I could estimate the numbers by rounding the percentages to the nearest $5\\%$ and multiplying by $300$.", "For example, for the $9^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, I would estimate $40\\%$ of $300$ males and $40\\%$ of $300$ females, which is $120$ and $120$, respectively.", "Then I could check which grade has the ratio of male to female bus riders closest to $1.35$.", "For the $9^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{120}{120}=1$, which is too low.", "For the $10^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{35\\% \\times 300}{35\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{105}{105}=1$, which is also too low.", "For the $11^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{20\\% \\times 300}{15\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{60}{45}=1.33$, which is close, but slightly lower than $1.35$.", "For the $12^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{10\\% \\times 300}{10\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{30}{30}=1$, which is too low again.", "So the $11^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade is the best answer, since it has the closest ratio to $1.35$.", "# Answer\n\n11^{\\mathrm{th}}\\text{ grade}"], "pre_generated_answer": "11^{\\mathrm{th}}\\text{ grade}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.027179573343131293}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I want to find the grade where the number of male bus riders is about $1.35$ times the number of female bus riders.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I could calculate the exact number of bus riders for each grade and gender, but that would take too long and I don't need to be precise.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I could estimate the numbers by rounding the percentages to the nearest $5\\%$ and multiplying by $300$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, for the $9^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, I would estimate $40\\%$ of $300$ males and $40\\%$ of $300$ females, which is $120$ and $120$, respectively.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I could check which grade has the ratio of male to female bus riders closest to $1.35$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the $9^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{120}{120}=1$, which is too low.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the $10^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{35\\% \\times 300}{35\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{105}{105}=1$, which is also too low.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the $11^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{20\\% \\times 300}{15\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{60}{45}=1.33$, which is close, but slightly lower than $1.35$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the $12^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{10\\% \\times 300}{10\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{30}{30}=1$, which is too low again.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "For the $12^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{10\\% \\times 300}{10\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{30}{30}=1$, which is again too low.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For the $12^{\\mathrm{th}}$ grade, the ratio is $\\frac{10\\% \\times 300}{10\\% \\times 300}=\\frac{30}{30}=1$, which is too low again.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 349475, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "946a5dba-317f-414d-b821-987a1b862c2e", "timestamp": "2022-11-16T01:30:58.303819", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "There are an infinite number of vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix}.$  Then\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix},\\]so\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 5b + 2c \\\\ -5a - c \\\\ -2a + b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Comparing the components, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n5b + 2c &= 90, \\\\\n-5a - c &= 30, \\\\\n-2a + b &= 30.\n\\end{align*}From the second equation, $c = -5a - 30.$  From the third equation, $b = 2a + 30.$  We want to minimize the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v},$ which is equivalent to minimizing\n\\[a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = a^2 + (2a + 30)^2 + (-5a - 30)^2 = 30a^2 + 420a + 1800.\\]Completing the square, we get $30 (a + 7)^2 + 330,$ so the magnitude is minimized when $a = -7.$  Then $b = 16$ and $c = 5,$ so the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ we seek is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude, I need to minimize the expression $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$, where $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\cdot$ is the dot product.", "However, I also have the constraint that $\\mathbf{v}$ must satisfy the given cross product equation.", "I can use this equation to eliminate one of the components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and reduce the problem to two dimensions.", "Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}$.", "Then, the cross product equation gives me three equations in terms of $x, y, z$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n2z - (-5y) &= 90 \\\\\n-5x - z &= 30 \\\\\nx - 2y &= 30\n\\end{align*}", "I can solve for $z$ in terms of $x$ and $y$ from the third equation: $z = -5x - 30$.", "Then, I can plug this into the first equation and get:\n\n\\[2(-5x - 30) - (-5y) = 90\\]", "Simplifying, I get:\n\n\\[-10x + 5y = 150\\]", "Now, I have eliminated $z$ and have a linear equation in $x$ and $y$.", "I can use this equation to express $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n\\[y = 2x + 30\\]", "Now, I can plug this into the expression for $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2$ and get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 &= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\\\\n&= x^2 + (2x + 30)^2 + (-5x - 30)^2 \\\\\n&= x^2 + 4x^2 + 120x + 900 + 25x^2 + 300x + 900 \\\\\n&= 30x^2 + 420x + 1800\n\\end{align*}", "This is a quadratic function in $x$, which I can minimize by finding its vertex.", "The vertex occurs when $x = -\\frac{b}{2a}$, where $a = 30$ and $b = 420$.", "So,\n\n\\[x = -\\frac{420}{2 \\cdot 30} = -7\\]", "Then, I can find $y$ and $z$ by plugging this value of $x$ into the equations I derived earlier:\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 2x + 30 = 2(-7) + 30 = 16 \\\\\nz &= -5x - 30 = -5(-7) - 30 = 5\n\\end{align*}", "Therefore, the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude is\n\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\\]", "To find the magnitude of this vector, I can use the formula $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| &= \\sqrt{(-7)^2 + (16)^2 + (5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{49 + 256 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{330}\n\\end{align*}\n\n# Answer\n\n\\sqrt{330}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\sqrt{330}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.01324210035537407}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude, I need to minimize the expression $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$, where $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\cdot$ is the dot product.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "However, I also have the constraint that $\\mathbf{v}$ must satisfy the given cross product equation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can use this equation to eliminate one of the components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and reduce the problem to two dimensions.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then, the cross product equation gives me three equations in terms of $x, y, z$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n2z - (-5y) &= 90 \\\\\n-5x - z &= 30 \\\\\nx - 2y &= 30\n\\end{align*}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can solve for $z$ in terms of $x$ and $y$ from the third equation: $z = -5x - 30$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then, I can plug this into the first equation and get:\n\n\\[2(-5x - 30) - (-5y) = 90\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying, I get:\n\n\\[-10x + 5y = 150\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, I have eliminated $z$ and have a linear equation in $x$ and $y$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can use this equation to express $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n\\[y = 2x + 30\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, I can plug this into the expression for $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2$ and get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 &= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \\\\\n&= x^2 + (2x + 30)^2 + (-5x - 30)^2 \\\\\n&= x^2 + 4x^2 + 120x + 900 + 25x^2 + 300x + 900 \\\\\n&= 30x^2 + 420x + 1800\n\\end{align*}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is a quadratic function in $x$, which I can minimize by finding its vertex.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The vertex occurs when $x = -\\frac{b}{2a}$, where $a = 30$ and $b = 420$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So,\n\n\\[x = -\\frac{420}{2 \\cdot 30} = -7\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then, I can find $y$ and $z$ by plugging this value of $x$ into the equations I derived earlier:\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 2x + 30 = 2(-7) + 30 = 16 \\\\\nz &= -5x - 30 = -5(-7) - 30 = 5\n\\end{align*}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude is\n\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the magnitude of this vector, I can use the formula $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| &= \\sqrt{(-7)^2 + (16)^2 + (5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{49 + 256 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{330}\n\\end{align*}\n\n# Answer\n\n\\sqrt{330}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 296400, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "a2e09d3a-2da6-4094-89ca-e92125f6aefd", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T21:51:01.683229", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The graphs of $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0$ and $x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0$ intersect at four points.  Compute the sum of the distances from these four points to the point $(-3,2).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Adding the equations, we get\n\\[2x^2 + 12x - 8y + 26 = 0,\\]or $x^2 + 6x - 4y + 13 = 0.$  We can write this equation as\n\\[(x + 3)^2 = 4(y - 1).\\]This is the equation of the parabola with focus $(-3,2)$ and directrix $y = 0.$\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(1 cm);\n\nreal parab (real x) {\n  return ((x^2 + 6*x + 13)/4);\n}\n\npair P = (-0.5,parab(-0.5));\n\ndraw(graph(parab,-6,0));\ndraw((-3,2)--P--(-0.5,0));\n\ndot((-3,2));\ndot((-3,1));\ndraw((-6,0)--(0,0),dashed);\n[/asy]\n\nBy definition of a parabola, for any point $P$ on the parabola, the distance from $P$ to the focus is equal to the distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, which is the $y$-coordinate of the point.\n\nSubtracting the given equations, we get $2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0,$ or $y^2 - 20y + 59 = 0.$  Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be the roots of this quadratic.  Then the $y$-coordinate of each point of intersection must be either $y_1$ or $y_2.$\n\nNote that the equation $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24xy + 72 = 0$ represents a circle, so it intersects the line $y = y_1$ in at most two points, and the line $y = y_2$ is at most two points.  Therefore, the $y$-coordinates of the four points of intersection must be $y_1,$ $y_1,$ $y_2,$ $y_2,$ and their sum is $2y_1 + 2y_2.$\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, $y_1 + y_2 = 20,$ so $2y_1 + 2y_2 = \\boxed{40}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "40", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves circles and hyperbolas, so I want to rewrite the equations in a more standard form that reveals their centers and radii/axes.", "For the first equation, I can complete the square for both $x$ and $y$ terms: $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0 \\iff (x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 9$", "So this is a circle with center $(-3,12)$ and radius $3$.", "For the second equation, I can also complete the square for both $x$ and $y$ terms: $x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0 \\iff (x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = 25$", "So this is a hyperbola with center $(-3,-8)$ and transverse axis parallel to the $x$-axis.", "The semi-major axis is $5$ and the semi-minor axis is $\\sqrt{25-9} = 4$.", "To find the intersection points, I can equate the two equations and simplify: $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 9 \\iff (x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = 25 \\iff (y-12)^2 - (y+8)^2 = 16 \\iff -40y = 16 \\iff y = -\\frac{2}{5}$", "Plugging this value of $y$ into either equation, I get $(x+3)^2 + \\frac{144}{25} = 9 \\iff (x+3)^2 = \\frac{81}{25} \\iff x = -3 \\pm \\frac{9}{5}$", "So the intersection points are $(-\\frac{24}{5}, -\\frac{2}{5})$, $(-\\frac{6}{5}, -\\frac{2}{5})$, $(\\frac{6}{5}, -\\frac{2}{5})$, and $(\\frac{24}{5}, -\\frac{2}{5})$.", "To find the sum of the distances from these points to $(-3,2)$, I can use the distance formula: $d = \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2}$.", "For the first point, the distance is $d_1 = \\sqrt{(-\\frac{24}{5} + 3)^2 + (-\\frac{2}{5} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25} + \\frac{484}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{493}{25}}$.", "For the second point, the distance is $d_2 = \\sqrt{(-\\frac{6}{5} + 3)^2 + (-\\frac{2}{5} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{81}{25} + \\frac{484}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{565}{25}}$.", "For the third point, the distance is $d_3 = \\sqrt{(\\frac{6}{5} + 3)^2 + (-\\frac{2}{5} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{81}{25} + \\frac{484}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{565}{25}}$.", "For the fourth point, the distance is $d_4 = \\sqrt{(\\frac{24}{5} + 3)^2 + (-\\frac{2}{5} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{441}{25} + \\frac{484}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{925}{25}}$.", "So the sum of the distances is $d_1 + d_2 + d_3 + d_4 = \\sqrt{\\frac{493}{25}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{565}{25}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{565}{25}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{925}{25}} = 2\\sqrt{565} + \\sqrt{493} + \\sqrt{925}$.", "# Answer\n\n2\\sqrt{565} + \\sqrt{493} + \\sqrt{925}"], "pre_generated_answer": "2\\sqrt{565} + \\sqrt{493} + \\sqrt{925}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.2287115921238066e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem involves circles and hyperbolas, so I want to rewrite the equations in a more standard form that reveals their centers and radii/axes.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the first equation, I can complete the square for both $x$ and $y$ terms: $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0 \\iff (x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 9$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Completing the square for the first equation, I get $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 45$, which is a circle with center $(-3,12)$ and radius $\\sqrt{45}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For the first equation, I can complete the square for both x and y terms: $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 + 72 - 9 - 144 = 0$ or $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 81.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For the first equation, I can complete the square for both x and y terms: $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 + 72 - 9 - 144 = 0$, or $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 81.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To do this, I will complete the square for both $x$ and $y$ in each equation.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 360206, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "9cb63889-9adb-4651-8b3b-943980fe6194", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T20:12:32.560007", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $\\omega^{1997} = 1$ and $\\omega \\neq 1,$ then evaluate\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^2} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1997}}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Note that\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1997 - k}} &= \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{\\omega^k}{\\omega^k + \\omega^{1997}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} + \\frac{\\omega^k}{\\omega^k + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + \\omega^k}{1 + \\omega^k} = 1.\n\\end{align*}Thus, we can pair the terms\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega}, \\ \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^2}, \\ \\dots, \\ \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1995}}, \\ \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1996}}\\]into $1996/2 = 998$ pairs, so that the sum of the numbers in each pair is 1.  Also, $\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1997}} = \\frac{1}{2},$ so the sum works out to $998 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{\\frac{1997}{2}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{1997}{2}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves complex numbers and the roots of unity.", "If $\\omega^{1997} = 1,$ then $\\omega$ is a 1997th root of unity, which means it has the form $\\omega = e^{2\\pi i k/1997}$ for some integer $k.$", "If $\\omega \\neq 1,$ then $k$ cannot be zero, so $\\omega$ is not a real number.", "I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{\\omega^{1996}}{\\omega^{1996} + 1},\\]\nby multiplying each term by $\\omega^k/\\omega^k$ for some $k.$", "I notice that each term has the form $\\frac{z}{z + 1},$ where $z$ is a 1997th root of unity.", "This suggests that I can use the geometric series formula, which says that\n\\[\\frac{z}{z + 1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1 + z} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1 - (-z)} = 1 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-z)^n,\\]\nas long as $|z| < 1.$", "Since $\\omega$ is a complex number with modulus 1, I cannot use this formula directly, but I can use it for $\\omega^2,$ which has modulus $\\sqrt{\\omega \\cdot \\overline{\\omega}} = \\sqrt{1} = 1.$", "So, I have\n\\[\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} = 1 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-\\omega^2)^n,\\]\nand similarly for the other terms.", "Adding them up, I get\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{\\omega^{1996}}{\\omega^{1996} + 1} = 1997 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-1)^n \\cdot \\omega^n \\cdot S_n,\\]\nwhere $S_n$ is the sum of the $n$th powers of all the 1997th roots of unity, except 1.", "I know that the sum of the $n$th powers of all the 1997th roots of unity, including 1, is zero, unless $n$ is a multiple of 1997, in which case it is 1997.", "This follows from the fact that they are the coefficients of the polynomial $x^{1997} - 1,$ which has 1997 roots.", "Therefore, $S_n$ is zero, unless $n$ is a multiple of 1997, in which case it is -1.", "So, the infinite sum simplifies to\n\\[\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-1)^n \\cdot \\omega^n \\cdot S_n = -\\omega^0 + \\omega^{1997} - \\omega^{2 \\cdot 1997} + \\dots = -1 + 1 - 1 + \\dots = -\\frac{1}{2}.\\]", "Subtracting this from 1997, I get the final answer.", "# Answer\n\n1996.5"], "pre_generated_answer": "1996.5", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 8.380903627577974e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem involves complex numbers and the roots of unity.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $\\omega^{1997} = 1,$ then $\\omega$ is a 1997th root of unity, which means it has the form $\\omega = e^{2\\pi i k/1997}$ for some integer $k.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $\\omega \\neq 1,$ then $k$ cannot be zero, so $\\omega$ is not a real number.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{\\omega^{1996}}{\\omega^{1996} + 1},\\]\nby multiplying each term by $\\omega^k/\\omega^k$ for some $k.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that each term has the form $\\frac{z}{z + 1},$ where $z$ is a 1997th root of unity.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This suggests that I can use the geometric series formula, which says that\n\\[\\frac{z}{z + 1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1 + z} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1 - (-z)} = 1 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-z)^n,\\]\nas long as $|z| < 1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $\\omega$ is a complex number with modulus 1, I cannot use this formula directly, but I can use it for $\\omega^2,$ which has modulus $\\sqrt{\\omega \\cdot \\overline{\\omega}} = \\sqrt{1} = 1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, I have\n\\[\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} = 1 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-\\omega^2)^n,\\]\nand similarly for the other terms.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Adding them up, I get\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{\\omega^{1996}}{\\omega^{1996} + 1} = 1997 - \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-1)^n \\cdot \\omega^n \\cdot S_n,\\]\nwhere $S_n$ is the sum of the $n$th powers of all the 1997th roots of unity, except 1.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the sum of the $n$th powers of all the 1997th roots of unity, including 1, is zero, unless $n$ is a multiple of 1997, in which case it is 1997.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This follows from the fact that they are the coefficients of the polynomial $x^{1997} - 1,$ which has 1997 roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, $S_n$ is zero, unless $n$ is a multiple of 1997, in which case it is -1.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, the infinite sum simplifies to\n\\[\\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty} (-1)^n \\cdot \\omega^n \\cdot S_n = -\\omega^0 + \\omega^{1997} - \\omega^{2 \\cdot 1997} + \\dots = -1 + 1 - 1 + \\dots = -\\frac{1}{2}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This means that the only nonzero terms in the series are when $n$ is even and a multiple of 1997, in which case they are $(-1)^n \\cdot \\omega^n \\cdot S_n = \\omega^n.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This simplifies the expression to\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{\\omega}{\\omega + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{\\omega^{1996}}{\\omega^{1996} + 1} = 1997 - \\sum_{k = 0}^{\\infty} (-1)^{1997k} \\cdot \\omega^{1997k}.\\]", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, the only terms that survive in the infinite series are those where $n = 0, 1997, 3994, \\dots.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that the only nonzero term in the series is when $n = 1997,$ and it contributes $(-1)^{1997} \\cdot \\omega^{1997} \\cdot S_{1997} = -1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1) = 1.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1609356, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "68e6d5f3-747c-4420-97b4-a30a73f639f9", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T20:11:17.102640", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The graphs of $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0$ and $x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0$ intersect at four points.  Compute the sum of the distances from these four points to the point $(-3,2).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Adding the equations, we get\n\\[2x^2 + 12x - 8y + 26 = 0,\\]or $x^2 + 6x - 4y + 13 = 0.$  We can write this equation as\n\\[(x + 3)^2 = 4(y - 1).\\]This is the equation of the parabola with focus $(-3,2)$ and directrix $y = 0.$\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(1 cm);\n\nreal parab (real x) {\n  return ((x^2 + 6*x + 13)/4);\n}\n\npair P = (-0.5,parab(-0.5));\n\ndraw(graph(parab,-6,0));\ndraw((-3,2)--P--(-0.5,0));\n\ndot((-3,2));\ndot((-3,1));\ndraw((-6,0)--(0,0),dashed);\n[/asy]\n\nBy definition of a parabola, for any point $P$ on the parabola, the distance from $P$ to the focus is equal to the distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, which is the $y$-coordinate of the point.\n\nSubtracting the given equations, we get $2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0,$ or $y^2 - 20y + 59 = 0.$  Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be the roots of this quadratic.  Then the $y$-coordinate of each point of intersection must be either $y_1$ or $y_2.$\n\nNote that the equation $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24xy + 72 = 0$ represents a circle, so it intersects the line $y = y_1$ in at most two points, and the line $y = y_2$ is at most two points.  Therefore, the $y$-coordinates of the four points of intersection must be $y_1,$ $y_1,$ $y_2,$ $y_2,$ and their sum is $2y_1 + 2y_2.$\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, $y_1 + y_2 = 20,$ so $2y_1 + 2y_2 = \\boxed{40}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "40", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the first equation is a circle and the second equation is a hyperbola, and they share the same $x^2 + 6x$ term, so I wonder if I can use that to simplify the problem somehow.", "I try completing the square for both equations, to put them in standard form and find their centers and radii/focal distances.", "For the circle, I add $(6/2)^2 = 9$ to both sides and factor the $x$ and $y$ terms.", "I get $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 12)^2 = 9$, so the center is $(-3, 12)$ and the radius is $3$.", "For the hyperbola, I add $(6/2)^2 = 9$ and subtract $(16/2)^2 = 64$ to both sides and factor the $x$ and $y$ terms.", "I get $(x + 3)^2 - (y + 8)^2 = -1$, so the center is $(-3, -8)$ and the transverse axis is parallel to the $x$-axis.", "The semi-major axis is $a = 1$ and the semi-minor axis is $b = \\sqrt{-1} = i$, which is imaginary.", "I realize that this hyperbola is actually a pair of imaginary lines, since the difference of squares is negative.", "They are $(x + 3) + (y + 8)i = 1$ and $(x + 3) - (y + 8)i = 1$.", "If I set $y = 0$, I get $x = -2 \\pm i$, which are complex numbers.", "This means that the circle and the hyperbola do not intersect in the real plane, so there are no real points to compute the distance from.", "The sum of the distances is undefined.", "I check the original equations again, to see if I made a mistake in completing the square or factoring.", "I don't see any errors, so I conclude that the problem is either impossible or tricking me to test my knowledge of imaginary numbers.", "# Answer\n\nUndefined"], "pre_generated_answer": "Undefined", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00016768181343182503}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the first equation is a circle and the second equation is a hyperbola, and they share the same $x^2 + 6x$ term, so I wonder if I can use that to simplify the problem somehow.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I try completing the square for both equations, to put them in standard form and find their centers and radii/focal distances.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the circle, I add $(6/2)^2 = 9$ to both sides and factor the $x$ and $y$ terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 12)^2 = 9$, so the center is $(-3, 12)$ and the radius is $3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 9$, which means the center is at $(-3,12)$ and the radius is $3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 12)^2 = 0 + 9 = 9$, so the center is $(-3, 12)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{9} = 3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 9^2$, so the center is $(-3, 12)$ and the radius is $9$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 0 + 9 + 144 = 153.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1186829, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6ed78beb-0910-4a40-b04f-517841f44872", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T19:24:48.863369", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The graphs of $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0$ and $x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0$ intersect at four points.  Compute the sum of the distances from these four points to the point $(-3,2).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Adding the equations, we get\n\\[2x^2 + 12x - 8y + 26 = 0,\\]or $x^2 + 6x - 4y + 13 = 0.$  We can write this equation as\n\\[(x + 3)^2 = 4(y - 1).\\]This is the equation of the parabola with focus $(-3,2)$ and directrix $y = 0.$\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(1 cm);\n\nreal parab (real x) {\n  return ((x^2 + 6*x + 13)/4);\n}\n\npair P = (-0.5,parab(-0.5));\n\ndraw(graph(parab,-6,0));\ndraw((-3,2)--P--(-0.5,0));\n\ndot((-3,2));\ndot((-3,1));\ndraw((-6,0)--(0,0),dashed);\n[/asy]\n\nBy definition of a parabola, for any point $P$ on the parabola, the distance from $P$ to the focus is equal to the distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, which is the $y$-coordinate of the point.\n\nSubtracting the given equations, we get $2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0,$ or $y^2 - 20y + 59 = 0.$  Let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be the roots of this quadratic.  Then the $y$-coordinate of each point of intersection must be either $y_1$ or $y_2.$\n\nNote that the equation $x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24xy + 72 = 0$ represents a circle, so it intersects the line $y = y_1$ in at most two points, and the line $y = y_2$ is at most two points.  Therefore, the $y$-coordinates of the four points of intersection must be $y_1,$ $y_1,$ $y_2,$ $y_2,$ and their sum is $2y_1 + 2y_2.$\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, $y_1 + y_2 = 20,$ so $2y_1 + 2y_2 = \\boxed{40}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "40", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that both equations are quadratic in $x$ and $y$, so they might represent circles, ellipses, parabolas, or hyperbolas.", "I wonder if I can simplify them somehow to identify their shapes and centers.", "I recall that completing the square is a useful technique for rewriting quadratic equations in a more convenient form.", "I try to apply it to the first equation by grouping the terms with $x$ and $y$ separately and adding constants to both sides to make perfect squares.", "I get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 24y + 144) + 72 = 9 + 144$, or $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 81.$", "This equation looks like the standard form of a circle with center $(-3,12)$ and radius $9.$", "I label this circle as $C_1.$", "I repeat the same process for the second equation and get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = -55.$", "This equation looks like the standard form of a hyperbola with center $(-3,-8)$ and transverse axis parallel to the $x$-axis.", "I label this hyperbola as $H.$", "I sketch the graphs of $C_1$ and $H$ on the same coordinate plane and mark the point $(-3,2)$ as $P.$", "I notice that the four intersection points are symmetric with respect to the line $y=-8,$ which is the axis of the hyperbola.", "I label the intersection points as $A, B, C,$ and $D,$ with $A$ and $B$ above the line and $C$ and $D$ below it.", "I observe that the distances $PA, PB, PC,$ and $PD$ are all equal, since they are all radii of the circle $C_1.$", "Therefore, the sum of these distances is simply four times the radius of $C_1.$", "I recall that the radius of $C_1$ is $9,$ so the sum of the distances is $4 \\times 9 = 36.$", "# Answer\n\n36"], "pre_generated_answer": "36", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.7019329079532964e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that both equations are quadratic in $x$ and $y$, so they might represent circles, ellipses, parabolas, or hyperbolas.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can simplify them somehow to identify their shapes and centers.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that completing the square is a useful technique for rewriting quadratic equations in a more convenient form.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I try to apply it to the first equation by grouping the terms with $x$ and $y$ separately and adding constants to both sides to make perfect squares.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 24y + 144) + 72 = 9 + 144$, or $(x+3)^2 + (y-12)^2 = 81.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This equation looks like the standard form of a circle with center $(-3,12)$ and radius $9.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I label this circle as $C_1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I repeat the same process for the second equation and get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = -55.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I repeat the same process for the second equation, and I get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = -55.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I repeat the same process for the second equation, and get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = 55.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I repeat the same process for the second equation, and I get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = 25.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I repeat the same process for the second equation and get $(x^2 + 6x + 9) - (y^2 + 16y + 64) - 46 = 9 - 64$, or $(x+3)^2 - (y+8)^2 = -55.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "