{"labeler": "a2e09d3a-2da6-4094-89ca-e92125f6aefd", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T17:41:15.789865", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $F(z)=\\frac{z+i}{z-i}$ for all complex numbers $z\\not= i,$ and let $z_n=F(z_{n-1})$ for all positive integers $n.$ Given that $z_0=\\frac 1{137}+i,$ find $z_{2002}.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Iterating $F$ a few times, we get \\[\\begin{aligned} F(F(z)) &= \\frac{\\frac{z+i}{z-i}+i}{\\frac{z+i}{z-i}-i} = \\frac{(z+i)+i(z-i)}{(z+i)-i(z-i)}= \\frac{z+i+zi+1}{z+i-zi-1}= \\frac{(z+1)(i+1)}{(z-1)(1-i)}\\\\\n&= \\frac{(z+1)(i+1)^2}{(z-1) \\cdot 2}= \\frac{(z+1)(2i)}{(z-1) \\cdot 2} = \\frac{z+1}{z-1}i,\\\\\nF(F(F(z))) &= \\frac{\\frac{z+1}{z-1}i+i}{\\frac{z+1}{z-1}i-i} = \\frac{\\frac{z+1}{z-1}+1}{\\frac{z+1}{z-1}-1} = \\frac{(z+1)+(z-1)}{(z+1)-(z-1)}= z. \\end{aligned}\\]Thus, $z_{k+3} = z_k$ for all $k.$ Since $2002 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3},$ we then have \\[z_{2002} = z_1 = \\frac{z_0+i}{z_0-i} = \\frac{1/137 + 2i}{1/137} = \\boxed{1+274i}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "1+274i", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves iterating a complex function many times and finding the result after 2002 steps.", "It seems daunting, but maybe there is some pattern or symmetry that simplifies the process.", "I notice that the function $F$ maps $i$ to $\\infty$ and $\\infty$ to $i.$", "That means that if $z_n=i$ for some $n,$ then $z_{n+1}=\\infty$ and $z_{n+2}=i$ again, and so on.", "So the sequence would repeat $i,\\infty,i,\\infty,\\dots$ forever.", "I wonder if there is any other value of $z_0$ that leads to such a repetition.", "Maybe if $z_0$ is on the unit circle, centered at the origin.", "Then $F(z_0)$ would also be on the unit circle, since $|F(z)|=|z+i|/|z-i|=1$ for any $z$ on the unit circle.", "And then $F(F(z_0))=F(z_1)=z_0$ again, by the inverse property of $F.$", "So the sequence would repeat $z_0,z_1,z_0,z_1,\\dots$ forever.", "In fact, I can generalize this idea.", "If $z_0$ is on any circle centered at $i,$ then $F(z_0)$ is also on that circle, and $F(F(z_0))=z_0.$", "To see this, let $r$ be the radius of the circle, and write $z_0=i+re^{i\\theta}$ for some angle $\\theta.$", "Then $F(z_0)=i+r^{-1}e^{i\\theta},$ which is also on the same circle, but with the reciprocal radius.", "And then $F(F(z_0))=i+re^{i\\theta}=z_0.$", "This means that if $z_0$ is on any circle centered at $i,$ the sequence will repeat with period $2.$", "So I want to find out if $z_0=\\frac 1{137}+i$ is on such a circle.", "To do this, I need to find the distance from $z_0$ to $i,$ and see if it is a rational number.", "The distance from $z_0$ to $i$ is $|z_0-i|=\\sqrt{(\\frac 1{137})^2+0^2}=\\frac 1{137}.$", "This is a rational number, so $z_0$ is on a circle centered at $i$ with radius $\\frac 1{137}.$", "That means $F(z_0)$ is on the same circle, but with radius $137.$", "And then $F(F(z_0))=z_0$ again, and the sequence repeats.", "Therefore, the sequence alternates between two values: $z_0$ and $F(z_0).$", "To find $F(z_0),$ I just plug in $z_0$ into the formula for $F.$", "I get $F(z_0)=\\frac{z_0+i}{z_0-i}=\\frac{\\frac 1{137}+2i}{\\frac 1{137}}=137+274i.$", "So $F(z_0)=137+274i.$", "Now I just need to determine whether $z_{2002}$ is equal to $z_0$ or $F(z_0).$", "Since the sequence repeats with period $2,$ I just need to look at the remainder of $2002$ divided by $2.$", "This is $0,$ so $z_{2002}$ is equal to $z_0.$", "# Answer\n\n\\frac 1{137}+i"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac 1{137}+i", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.1824349393343695e-06}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem involves iterating a complex function many times and finding the result after 2002 steps.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "It seems daunting, but maybe there is some pattern or symmetry that simplifies the process.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the function $F$ maps $i$ to $\\infty$ and $\\infty$ to $i.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Let me first try to understand what the function $F$ does to a given complex number $z.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I start by computing the first few values of $z_n$ to see if I can spot any pattern.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "A useful trick for complex functions is to use the polar form $z=re^{i\\theta},$ where $r$ is the modulus and $\\theta$ is the argument of $z.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let me try to visualize the function F on the complex plane. F maps any complex number z to another complex number F(z) by dividing z+i by z-i.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 338765, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "80cac07a-618b-4d1c-a9f9-6af9ef621fde", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T12:01:58.237064", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can add 4 to both sides to get\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + 1 + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + 1 + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} + 1 = 2010x.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[\\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x.\\]We see that $x = 0$ is a solution (which does not affect our sum).  Otherwise, we can divide both sides by $x$:\n\\[\\frac{x}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 4} = 2010.\\]Clearing the denominators, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n&x(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) \\\\\n&\\quad = 2010(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\n\\end{align*}This expands to\n\\[4x^7 + \\dotsb = 2010x^8 + \\dotsb,\\]where only terms that have degree 7 or greater are shown.  Then\n\\[2010x^8 - 4x^7 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots is $\\frac{4}{2010} = \\boxed{\\frac{2}{1005}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{2}{1005}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a tricky equation to solve, but maybe I can simplify it by multiplying both sides by a common denominator.", "The denominator on the left side is the product of all the factors $(x^2 - n)$ for $n = 1, 2, 3, 4$, so it is $x^4 - 10x^2 + 9$.", "The denominator on the right side is just $1$, so I can multiply both sides by $x^4 - 10x^2 + 9$ and get rid of the fractions.", "Doing that, I get\n\\[ 4(x^2 - 3) + 3(x^2 - 4) + 2(x^2 - 2) + (x^2 - 1) = 2010x(x^4 - 10x^2 + 9) - 4(x^4 - 10x^2 + 9).\\]", "Now I can expand and simplify this by collecting like terms.", "I get\n\\[ 10x^2 - 30 = 2010x^5 - 20,100x^3 + 18,090x - 4x^4 + 40x^2 - 36.\\]", "This is a fifth-degree polynomial equation, so it might have up to five complex solutions.", "But I don't need to find them individually, I just need to find their sum.", "One way to do that is to use Vieta's formulas, which relate the coefficients of a polynomial to the sums and products of its roots.", "Specifically, for a polynomial of the form $a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_1x + a_0 = 0$, the sum of the roots is $-a_{n-1}/a_n$.", "In this case, the leading coefficient is $2010x$, and the coefficient of the fourth-degree term is $-4$, so the sum of the roots is $-(-4)/(2010x) = 2/(505x)$.", "# Answer\n\n2/(505x)"], "pre_generated_answer": "2/(505x)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.2947861810772833e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a tricky equation to solve, but maybe I can simplify it by multiplying both sides by a common denominator.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The denominator on the left side is the product of all the factors $(x^2 - n)$ for $n = 1, 2, 3, 4$, so it is $x^4 - 10x^2 + 9$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The common denominator of the left side is the product of the factors \\(x^2 - 1\\), \\(x^2 - 2\\), \\(x^2 - 3\\), and \\(x^2 - 4\\). This is also the lowest common multiple of the denominators on the right side, since \\(2010x - 4\\) has a factor of \\(x - 2\\).", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The common denominator of the left side is \\[(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4),\\] so I'll multiply both sides by that expression.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The common denominator would be the product of all the factors in the denominator, namely \\[(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\\] This is a quartic polynomial, which makes sense since we expect four complex solutions.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I look at the denominators on the left-hand side, they are all factors of \\[(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2).\\] This is a degree-8 polynomial, so it would be hard to solve for x directly, but maybe I can reduce the degree by canceling out some terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 3129516, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "dcd9c8b6-6390-45ac-beb6-75e0de7f4413", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T10:53:34.564863", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can add 4 to both sides to get\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + 1 + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + 1 + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} + 1 = 2010x.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[\\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x.\\]We see that $x = 0$ is a solution (which does not affect our sum).  Otherwise, we can divide both sides by $x$:\n\\[\\frac{x}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 4} = 2010.\\]Clearing the denominators, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n&x(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) \\\\\n&\\quad = 2010(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\n\\end{align*}This expands to\n\\[4x^7 + \\dotsb = 2010x^8 + \\dotsb,\\]where only terms that have degree 7 or greater are shown.  Then\n\\[2010x^8 - 4x^7 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots is $\\frac{4}{2010} = \\boxed{\\frac{2}{1005}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{2}{1005}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a very complicated equation, so I need to look for some way to simplify it or make it more manageable.", "One thing I notice is that the left-hand side is a sum of four fractions, each with a quadratic denominator of the form \\(x^2 - k\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer from 1 to 4.", "I also notice that the right-hand side is a linear expression in \\(x\\).", "This suggests to me that I might be able to rewrite the left-hand side as a single fraction with a common denominator, and then compare the numerators and denominators of both sides.", "To do that, I need to find the least common multiple of the four quadratic factors on the left-hand side.", "A quick way to do that is to use the fact that any quadratic polynomial can be factored into linear factors, if it has complex roots.", "For example, \\(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\\), \\(x^2 - 2 = (x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})\\), and so on.", "So the least common multiple of the four quadratic factors is just the product of all the distinct linear factors, which are \\((x - 1), (x + 1), (x - \\sqrt{2}), (x + \\sqrt{2}), (x - \\sqrt{3}), (x + \\sqrt{3}), (x - 2), (x + 2)\\).", "This means that the common denominator of the left-hand side is \n\\[(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2).\\]", "To find the numerator, I need to multiply each fraction by the appropriate factor to make the denominators equal.", "For example, for the first fraction, I need to multiply by \n\\[\\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)} = 1,\\] and then simplify.", "I won't show all the details here, but after doing this for all four fractions and adding them up, I get the numerator to be\n\\[12x^7 - 152x^5 + 760x^3 - 1236x + 24.\\]", "So the left-hand side of the equation becomes\n\\[\\frac{12x^7 - 152x^5 + 760x^3 - 1236x + 24}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}.\\]", "Now I can compare this to the right-hand side, which is \\(2010x - 4\\).", "Since the equation is supposed to hold for all values of \\(x\\), except the ones that make the denominator zero, I can equate the numerators and the denominators separately.", "Equating the denominators gives me\n\\[(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2) = 1,\\] which is impossible, since the left-hand side is a polynomial of degree 8, and the right-hand side is a constant.", "This means that the equation has no solutions, complex or otherwise.", "So the sum of all complex solutions is zero.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.8508689875778404e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a very complicated equation, so I need to look for some way to simplify it or make it more manageable.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One thing I notice is that the left-hand side is a sum of four fractions, each with a quadratic denominator of the form \\(x^2 - k\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer from 1 to 4.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also notice that the right-hand side is a linear expression in \\(x\\).", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This suggests to me that I might be able to rewrite the left-hand side as a single fraction with a common denominator, and then compare the numerators and denominators of both sides.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To do that, I need to find the least common multiple of the four quadratic factors on the left-hand side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "A quick way to do that is to use the fact that any quadratic polynomial can be factored into linear factors, if it has complex roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, \\(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\\), \\(x^2 - 2 = (x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})\\), and so on.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the least common multiple of the four quadratic factors is just the product of all the distinct linear factors, which are \\((x - 1), (x + 1), (x - \\sqrt{2}), (x + \\sqrt{2}), (x - \\sqrt{3}), (x + \\sqrt{3}), (x - 2), (x + 2)\\).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the common denominator of the left-hand side is \n\\[(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2).\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the numerator, I need to multiply each fraction by the appropriate factor to make the denominators equal.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, for the first fraction, I need to multiply by \n\\[\\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)} = 1,\\] and then simplify.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I won't show all the details here, but after doing this for all four fractions and adding them up, I get the numerator to be\n\\[12x^7 - 152x^5 + 760x^3 - 1236x + 24.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Doing this for each fraction and adding them up, I obtain the numerator as\n\\[10x^6 - 20x^5 - 36x^4 + 80x^3 - 32x^2 - 40x + 24.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This gives me \n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot \\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)} = \\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Doing this for all four fractions, I get \n\\[\\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})}{x^2 - 4}\\]\n\\[= \\frac{4x^5 - 26x^3 + 24x}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Doing this for all four fractions and adding them up, I get \n\\[\\frac{x^6 - 11x^4 + 32x^2 - 55}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 457285, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "50251386-3892-4b2e-b616-b534cef9aa5c", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T10:51:25.537667", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can add 4 to both sides to get\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + 1 + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + 1 + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} + 1 = 2010x.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[\\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x.\\]We see that $x = 0$ is a solution (which does not affect our sum).  Otherwise, we can divide both sides by $x$:\n\\[\\frac{x}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 4} = 2010.\\]Clearing the denominators, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n&x(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) \\\\\n&\\quad = 2010(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\n\\end{align*}This expands to\n\\[4x^7 + \\dotsb = 2010x^8 + \\dotsb,\\]where only terms that have degree 7 or greater are shown.  Then\n\\[2010x^8 - 4x^7 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots is $\\frac{4}{2010} = \\boxed{\\frac{2}{1005}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{2}{1005}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a daunting equation, but maybe we can simplify it by finding a common denominator for the left-hand side.", "Multiplying the first term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 2}{x^2 - 2}\\), the second term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 - 1}\\), the third term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 4}{x^2 - 4}\\), and the fourth term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 3}{x^2 - 3}\\), we get\n\\[\\frac{(x^2 - 2) + 2(x^2 - 1) + 3(x^2 - 4) + 4(x^2 - 3)}{(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "Simplifying the numerator, we have\n\\[\\frac{10x^2 - 24}{(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "Now we can cross-multiply and rearrange to get\n\\[(10x^2 - 24)(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) = (2010x - 4)(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\\]", "Notice that both sides have the same factor \\((x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)\\), so we can cancel it out and get\n\\[10x^2 - 24 = 2010x - 4.\\]", "This is much simpler to solve.", "Expanding and collecting terms, we have\n\\[10x^2 - 2010x - 20 = 0.\\]", "Dividing by 10, we have\n\\[x^2 - 201x - 2 = 0.\\]", "This looks like a quadratic equation, so we can try to use the quadratic formula.", "The discriminant is\n\\[b^2 - 4ac = (201)^2 - 4(-2) = 40409.\\]", "This is not a perfect square, so the solutions will be irrational.", "Using the quadratic formula, we have\n\\[x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} = \\frac{201 \\pm \\sqrt{40409}}{2}.\\]", "These are the two complex solutions of the original equation.", "To find their sum, we just add them up and simplify.", "\\[x_1 + x_2 = \\frac{201 + \\sqrt{40409}}{2} + \\frac{201 - \\sqrt{40409}}{2} = \\frac{201 + 201}{2} = 201.\\]", "# Answer\n\n201"], "pre_generated_answer": "201", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0006931509986569231}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a daunting equation, but maybe we can simplify it by finding a common denominator for the left-hand side.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Multiplying the first term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 2}{x^2 - 2}\\), the second term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 - 1}\\), the third term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 4}{x^2 - 4}\\), and the fourth term by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 3}{x^2 - 3}\\), we get\n\\[\\frac{(x^2 - 2) + 2(x^2 - 1) + 3(x^2 - 4) + 4(x^2 - 3)}{(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The common denominator would be the product of all the quadratic factors in the denominators, so that would be \\[(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To do that, we need to multiply each fraction by the product of the other denominators. For example, the first fraction becomes\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} \\cdot \\frac{(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)}{(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)} = \\frac{(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)}{(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To do that, we can multiply the first fraction by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 2}{x^2 - 2}\\), the second fraction by \\(\\frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 - 1}\\), and so on, until we have fractions with the same denominator.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To do this, we need to multiply each fraction by the product of all the other denominators. For example, the first fraction becomes\n\\[\\frac{(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)}{(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)} = \\frac{x^6 - 9x^4 + 22x^2 - 24}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 351960, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "80cac07a-618b-4d1c-a9f9-6af9ef621fde", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T10:48:18.267113", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of all complex solutions of the equation\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can add 4 to both sides to get\n\\[\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + 1 + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + 1 + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + 1 + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} + 1 = 2010x.\\]This simplifies to\n\\[\\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x^2}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x.\\]We see that $x = 0$ is a solution (which does not affect our sum).  Otherwise, we can divide both sides by $x$:\n\\[\\frac{x}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{x}{x^2 - 4} = 2010.\\]Clearing the denominators, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n&x(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + x(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) \\\\\n&\\quad = 2010(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\n\\end{align*}This expands to\n\\[4x^7 + \\dotsb = 2010x^8 + \\dotsb,\\]where only terms that have degree 7 or greater are shown.  Then\n\\[2010x^8 - 4x^7 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots is $\\frac{4}{2010} = \\boxed{\\frac{2}{1005}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{2}{1005}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This equation looks complicated, but I notice that the left-hand side has a lot of fractions with similar denominators.", "Maybe I can simplify it by finding a common denominator.", "To do that, I need to factor each denominator as a difference of squares.", "For example, \\(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\\).", "Then I can multiply both sides of the equation by the product of all the factors, which is \\((x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)\\).", "On the left-hand side, most of the factors will cancel out in each fraction, leaving only one factor in each numerator.", "For example, \\(\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1}\\) will become \\(\\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)} = \\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)}\\).", "On the right-hand side, I will have a very large polynomial with 16 terms, but I don't need to expand it all out.", "I just need to find the coefficients of the highest and lowest degree terms, which are the easiest to get.", "The highest degree term will be the product of all the x's in each factor, which is \\(x^8\\).", "The coefficient of \\(x^8\\) will be 2010.", "The lowest degree term will be the product of all the constant terms in each factor, which is \\(-4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}) \\cdot (\\sqrt{2}) \\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot 2 = -96\\).", "The coefficient of the constant term will be \\(-4 \\cdot (-96) = 384\\).", "So the right-hand side will look something like \\(2010x^8 + \\cdots + 384\\).", "Now I have a much simpler equation to work with, which is\n\\[\\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)} + \\frac{2}{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})} + \\frac{3}{(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})} + \\frac{4}{(x - 2)(x + 2)} = 2010x^8 + \\cdots + 384.\\]", "I notice that the left-hand side has four fractions that are symmetric around the y-axis, meaning that if I replace x with -x, nothing changes.", "This means that the left-hand side is an even function of x.", "On the other hand, the right-hand side has an odd function of x, namely \\(2010x^8\\), plus some even functions of x. This means that the right-hand side is not symmetric around the y-axis.", "This gives me a contradiction, because an even function can never equal a non-even function, except at the origin.", "But x = 0 is not a solution of the equation, because it makes the denominators on the left-hand side zero.", "Therefore, this equation has no solutions, complex or otherwise.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 5.5959218283016454e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This equation looks complicated, but I notice that the left-hand side has a lot of fractions with similar denominators.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Maybe I can simplify it by finding a common denominator.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To do that, I need to factor each denominator as a difference of squares.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, \\(x^2 - 1 = (x - 1)(x + 1)\\).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I can multiply both sides of the equation by the product of all the factors, which is \\((x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)\\).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "On the left-hand side, most of the factors will cancel out in each fraction, leaving only one factor in each numerator.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, \\(\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1}\\) will become \\(\\frac{(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)}{(x - 1)(x + 1)(x - \\sqrt{2})(x + \\sqrt{2})(x - \\sqrt{3})(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - 2)(x + 2)} = \\frac{1}{(x - 1)(x + 1)}\\).", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "On the right-hand side, I can expand the binomial and distribute the product to get a degree-eight polynomial.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "On the right-hand side, I will get a large polynomial with degree 8, but I don't need to 