{"labeler": "30f0b980-2587-4ec3-957a-0ca2bf958c40", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T03:12:29.677315", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $T$ be the set of all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers for which there exist triangles with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c.$  Compute\n\\[\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "For a triangle with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ let $s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2},$ and let\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= s - a = \\frac{-a + b + c}{2}, \\\\\ny &= s - b = \\frac{a - b + c}{2}, \\\\\nz &= s - c = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}.\n\\end{align*}By the Triangle Inequality, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive.  (This technique is often referred to as the Ravi Substitution.)  Note that\n\\begin{align*}\na &= y + z, \\\\\nb &= x + z, \\\\\nc &= x + y.\n\\end{align*}If $s$ is even, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive integers.  So, we can set $x = i,$ $y = j,$ and $z = k,$ which gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k, i + k, i + j).$\n\nIf $s$ is odd, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all of the form $n - \\frac{1}{2},$ where $n$ is a positive integer.  So, we can set $x = i - \\frac{1}{2},$ $y = j - \\frac{1}{2},$ and $z = k - \\frac{1}{2}.$  This gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k - 1, i + k - 1, i + j - 1).$\n\nThus, our sum is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{2^{j + k - 1}}{3^{i + k - 1} 5^{i + j - 1}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{15^i} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^j \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^k \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/15}{1 - 1/15} \\cdot \\frac{2/5}{1 - 2/5} \\cdot \\frac{2/3}{1 - 2/3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{17}{21}}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{17}{21}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a tricky problem, but one possible strategy is to use the triangle inequality, which says that the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.", "This gives us three conditions for $(a,b,c)$ to be in $T$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na + b &> c\\\\\na + c &> b\\\\\nb + c &> a\n\\end{align*}", "We can rewrite these inequalities in terms of the exponents of $2,$ $3,$ and $5$ in the denominators of the fractions:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\log_2 3^b + \\log_2 5^c &< \\log_2 2^a\\\\\n\\log_2 3^b + \\log_2 2^a &< \\log_2 5^c\\\\\n\\log_2 5^c + \\log_2 2^a &< \\log_2 3^b\n\\end{align*}", "We can simplify these expressions by using the properties of logarithms and the fact that $\\log_2 3 \\approx 1.585$ and $\\log_2 5 \\approx 2.322$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nb \\cdot 1.585 + c \\cdot 2.322 &< a\\\\\nb \\cdot 1.585 + a &< c \\cdot 2.322\\\\\nc \\cdot 2.322 + a &< b \\cdot 1.585\n\\end{align*}", "Now, we can try to find some upper bounds for $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ based on these inequalities.", "For example, if we fix $a = 1,$ then we get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nb \\cdot 1.585 + c \\cdot 2.322 &< 1\\\\\nb \\cdot 1.585 + 1 &< c \\cdot 2.322\\\\\nc \\cdot 2.322 + 1 &< b \\cdot 1.585\n\\end{align*}", "The first inequality implies that $b = 0$ and $c = 0,$ which contradicts the second and third inequalities.", "So there is no solution with $a = 1.$", "Similarly, if we fix $a = 2,$ then we get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nb \\cdot 1.585 + c \\cdot 2.322 &< 2\\\\\nb \\cdot 1.585 + 2 &< c \\cdot 2.322\\\\\nc \\cdot 2.322 + 2 &< b \\cdot 1.585\n\\end{align*}", "The first inequality implies that $b = 0$ and $c \\leq 0.63,$ which means that $c = 0.$", "The second inequality then implies that $b \\geq 1.27,$ which means that $b = 1.$", "The third inequality then implies that $c \\geq -0.14,$ which is true.", "So the only solution with $a = 2$ is $(a,b,c) = (2,1,0).$", "Continuing this way, we can find that the only solutions with $a = 3$ are $(a,b,c) = (3,1,1)$ and $(3,2,0),$ and the only solution with $a = 4$ is $(a,b,c) = (4,2,1).$", "For $a \\geq 5,$ there are no solutions, because the first inequality becomes impossible to satisfy.", "So we have found all the possible values of $a,$ and for each one, the corresponding values of $b$ and $c.$", "Now we can plug them into the sum and compute it:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \\frac{2^2}{3^1 5^0} + \\frac{2^3}{3^1 5^1} + \\frac{2^3}{3^2 5^0} + \\frac{2^4}{3^2 5^1}\\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{8}{15} + \\frac{8}{9} + \\frac{16}{45}\\\\\n&= \\frac{80}{45} + \\frac{48}{45} + \\frac{40}{45} + \\frac{16}{45}\\\\\n&= \\frac{184}{45}\n\\end{align*}\n\n# Answer\n\n\\frac{184}{45}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{184}{45}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00010723548240692007}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a tricky problem, but one possible strategy is to use the triangle inequality, which says that the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This gives us three conditions for $(a,b,c)$ to be in $T$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na + b &> c\\\\\na + c &> b\\\\\nb + c &> a\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "We can rewrite these inequalities in terms of the exponents of $2,$ $3,$ and $5$ in the denominators of the fractions:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\log_2 3^b + \\log_2 5^c &< \\log_2 2^a\\\\\n\\log_2 3^b + \\log_2 2^a &< \\log_2 5^c\\\\\n\\log_2 5^c + \\log_2 2^a &< \\log_2 3^b\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Now, because $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are positive integers, we can rearrange these inequalities to get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\na - c &> -b\\\\\nb - c &> -a\\\\\na - b &> -c\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These inequalities can be rewritten as\n\n\\begin{align*}\na - c &> -b\\\\\na - b &> -c\\\\\nb - a &> -c\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, we can try to partition $T$ into smaller sets based on the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$ For example, let $T_1$ be the set of triples where $a = b = c,$ and let $T_2$ be the set of triples where $a < b = c.$ We can also let $T_3$ be the set of triples where $a < b < c,$ and so on.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "We can rewrite these as $a > c - b, b > c - a, c > a - b.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 721159, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
