{15^i} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^j \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^k \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/15}{1 - 1/15} \\cdot \\frac{2/5}{1 - 2/5} \\cdot \\frac{2/3}{1 - 2/3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{17}{21}}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{17}{21}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a tricky problem that requires some knowledge of triangle inequalities and divisibility properties.", "A first observation is that the sum is finite, because the side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c$ must satisfy $a+b>c,$ $b+c>a,$ and $c+a>b,$ which imply that $a<\\frac{b+c}{2},$ $b<\\frac{c+a}{2},$ and $c<\\frac{a+b}{2}.$", "Therefore, the maximum possible value of any side length is $2,$ since if it were $3$ or more, it would be impossible to satisfy the triangle inequalities.", "This also means that the only possible exponents of $5$ are $0$ and $1,$ since if $c\\geq 2,$ then $a$ and $b$ must be $1,$ and then $5^c$ would not divide $2^a 3^b.$", "So, the problem reduces to finding all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers less than or equal to $2$ such that $2^a$ divides $3^b 5^c$ and the triangle inequalities hold.", "I will make a table of all such triples and their corresponding fractions, and then add them up to get the final answer.", "| a | b | c | 2^a/3^b 5^c |\n| - | - | - | ----------- |\n| 1 | 1 | 1 | 4/15 |\n| 1 | 1 | 2 | 4/75 |\n| 1 | 2 | 1 | 2/45 |\n| 1 | 2 | 2 | 2/225 |\n| 2 | 1 | 1 | 8/15 |\n| 2 | 1 | 2 | 8/75 |\n| 2 | 2 | 1 | 4/45 |\n| 2 | 2 | 2 | 4/225 |", "Adding up these fractions, I get\n\\[\\frac{4}{15}+\\frac{4}{75}+\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{2}{225}+\\frac{8}{15}+\\frac{8}{75}+\\frac{4}{45}+\\frac{4}{225}\\]\n\\[\\frac{60}{225}+\\frac{12}{225}+\\frac{10}{225}+\\frac{2}{225}+\\frac{120}{225}+\\frac{24}{225}+\\frac{20}{225}+\\frac{4}{225}\\]\n\\[\\frac{252}{225}\\]\n\\[\\frac{28}{25}.\\]", "# Answer\n\n28/25"], "pre_generated_answer": "28/25", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0004934961291380224}, "label": {"steps":