{"labeler": "5a1ab09c-9262-405a-aa6f-b44212a6a84b", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T03:07:42.690737", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $T$ be the set of all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers for which there exist triangles with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c.$  Compute\n\\[\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "For a triangle with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ let $s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2},$ and let\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= s - a = \\frac{-a + b + c}{2}, \\\\\ny &= s - b = \\frac{a - b + c}{2}, \\\\\nz &= s - c = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}.\n\\end{align*}By the Triangle Inequality, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive.  (This technique is often referred to as the Ravi Substitution.)  Note that\n\\begin{align*}\na &= y + z, \\\\\nb &= x + z, \\\\\nc &= x + y.\n\\end{align*}If $s$ is even, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive integers.  So, we can set $x = i,$ $y = j,$ and $z = k,$ which gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k, i + k, i + j).$\n\nIf $s$ is odd, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all of the form $n - \\frac{1}{2},$ where $n$ is a positive integer.  So, we can set $x = i - \\frac{1}{2},$ $y = j - \\frac{1}{2},$ and $z = k - \\frac{1}{2}.$  This gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k - 1, i + k - 1, i + j - 1).$\n\nThus, our sum is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{2^{j + k - 1}}{3^{i + k - 1} 5^{i + j - 1}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{15^i} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^j \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^k \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/15}{1 - 1/15} \\cdot \\frac{2/5}{1 - 2/5} \\cdot \\frac{2/3}{1 - 2/3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{17}{21}}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{17}{21}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the sum is a product of sums, because I can factor out the powers of 2, 3, and 5 in each term.", "That is, the sum is equal to\n\\[\\left(\\sum_{a \\in S} 2^a\\right) \\left(\\sum_{b \\in S} \\frac{1}{3^b}\\right) \\left(\\sum_{c \\in S} \\frac{1}{5^c}\\right),\\]\nwhere $S$ is the set of positive integers that can be side lengths of a triangle.", "To find $S,$ I recall the triangle inequality, which says that the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.", "This means that $S$ is the set of positive integers that are less than or equal to $n-1,$ where $n$ is the smallest positive integer that cannot be a side length of any triangle.", "To find $n,$ I consider the worst case scenario, where the two smaller sides of a triangle are both $1.$", "Then the third side must be less than $1 + 1 = 2,$ which means that $n = 2.$", "Therefore, $S = \\{1\\},$ and the sums in the product are easy to compute:\n\\[\\sum_{a \\in S} 2^a = 2,\\]\n\\[\\sum_{b \\in S} \\frac{1}{3^b} = \\frac{1}{3},\\]\n\\[\\sum_{c \\in S} \\frac{1}{5^c} = \\frac{1}{5}.\\]", "Multiplying these sums together gives the final answer.", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{2}{15}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{2}{15}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0005931115681705862}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the sum is a product of sums, because I can factor out the powers of 2, 3, and 5 in each term.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Wow, this is a pretty unusual problem. I wonder how to approach it.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem is about counting the number of triangles with different side lengths and computing a sum involving their lengths.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the fraction $\\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}$ is only defined for positive integers $a,b,c$, so I can ignore any triples that have a zero or negative component.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem looks pretty hard, but maybe there is some pattern or symmetry that I can exploit to simplify the computation.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 543928, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "cd63d345-bd43-4cbe-aa3f-3b2cd57f0a75", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T03:06:42.180856", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $T$ be the set of all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers for which there exist triangles with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c.$  Compute\n\\[\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "For a triangle with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ let $s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2},$ and let\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= s - a = \\frac{-a + b + c}{2}, \\\\\ny &= s - b = \\frac{a - b + c}{2}, \\\\\nz &= s - c = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}.\n\\end{align*}By the Triangle Inequality, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive.  (This technique is often referred to as the Ravi Substitution.)  Note that\n\\begin{align*}\na &= y + z, \\\\\nb &= x + z, \\\\\nc &= x + y.\n\\end{align*}If $s$ is even, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive integers.  So, we can set $x = i,$ $y = j,$ and $z = k,$ which gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k, i + k, i + j).$\n\nIf $s$ is odd, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all of the form $n - \\frac{1}{2},$ where $n$ is a positive integer.  So, we can set $x = i - \\frac{1}{2},$ $y = j - \\frac{1}{2},$ and $z = k - \\frac{1}{2}.$  This gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k - 1, i + k - 1, i + j - 1).$\n\nThus, our sum is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{2^{j + k - 1}}{3^{i + k - 1} 5^{i + j - 1}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{15^i} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^j \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^k \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/15}{1 - 1/15} \\cdot \\frac{2/5}{1 - 2/5} \\cdot \\frac{2/3}{1 - 2/3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{17}{21}}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{17}{21}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the sum has the form $\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} 2^a q^{b+c},$ where $q = \\frac{1}{15}.$", "I also notice that the condition for $(a,b,c)$ to be in $T$ is that $a,b,c$ satisfy the triangle inequality: $a + b > c, a + c > b, b + c > a.$", "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the condition or the sum by using some symmetry or transformation.", "I see that swapping any two elements of $(a,b,c)$ does not change the value of the summand, since $q^{b+c} = q^{c+b}.$", "I also see that adding a constant to all three elements of $(a,b,c)$ does not change the value of the summand, since $2^{a+k} q^{b+k+c+k} = 2^a q^{b+c}.$", "So, I can assume without loss of generality that $a \\leq b \\leq c$ and that $a = 1.$", "Now, the sum becomes\n\\[\\sum_{\\substack{1 \\leq b \\leq c \\\\ 1 + b > c, 1 + c > b, b + c > 1}} \\frac{2}{3^b 5^c}.\\]", "I try to find a bound for $b$ and $c.$", "I observe that $b + c > 1$ implies that $b \\geq 1$ and $c \\geq 1.$", "I also observe that $1 + c > b$ implies that $c < b + 1.$", "So, I have $1 \\leq b \\leq c < b + 1.$", "This means that there is only one possible value for $c$ given $b,$ namely $c = b.$", "So, the sum becomes\n\\[\\sum_{b = 1}^\\infty \\frac{2}{3^b 5^b}.\\]", "I recognize this as a geometric series with first term $\\frac{2}{15}$ and common ratio $\\frac{1}{15}.$", "I recall the formula for the sum of a geometric series: $S = \\frac{a}{1-r},$ where $a$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.", "I apply the formula to get the sum:\n\\[S = \\frac{\\frac{2}{15}}{1 - \\frac{1}{15}} = \\frac{2}{15 - 1} = \\frac{1}{7}.\\]", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{1}{7}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{1}{7}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 5.1590379968268125e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the sum has the form $\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} 2^a q^{b+c},$ where $q = \\frac{1}{15}.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This is a tricky problem, and I don't see any obvious shortcuts or clever tricks. So I'll start by trying some small values of $a,b,c$ and see if I can find a pattern or a formula.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the sum has a fractional expression with different bases in the denominator. This makes me think of using the prime factorizations of $a,$ $b,$ $c$ and seeing how they relate to the existence of a triangle.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that this sum involves fractions with powers of 2, 3, and 5 in the denominator. This suggests that it might be easier to work with the reciprocals of these fractions, which are just products of powers of 2, 3, and 5. So I will rewrite the sum as\n\\[\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} = \\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{1}{2^a 3^b 5^c}.\\]", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem looks hard, but maybe there is a way to simplify it somehow.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 295365, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "fc08c519-6e30-4ca6-b223-37cc3bcb5879", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T03:05:09.828592", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $T$ be the set of all triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers for which there exist triangles with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c.$  Compute\n\\[\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "For a triangle with side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ let $s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2},$ and let\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= s - a = \\frac{-a + b + c}{2}, \\\\\ny &= s - b = \\frac{a - b + c}{2}, \\\\\nz &= s - c = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}.\n\\end{align*}By the Triangle Inequality, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive.  (This technique is often referred to as the Ravi Substitution.)  Note that\n\\begin{align*}\na &= y + z, \\\\\nb &= x + z, \\\\\nc &= x + y.\n\\end{align*}If $s$ is even, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all positive integers.  So, we can set $x = i,$ $y = j,$ and $z = k,$ which gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k, i + k, i + j).$\n\nIf $s$ is odd, then $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are all of the form $n - \\frac{1}{2},$ where $n$ is a positive integer.  So, we can set $x = i - \\frac{1}{2},$ $y = j - \\frac{1}{2},$ and $z = k - \\frac{1}{2}.$  This gives us the parameterization $(a,b,c) = (j + k - 1, i + k - 1, i + j - 1).$\n\nThus, our sum is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{(a,b,c) \\in T} \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} &= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{2^{j + k - 1}}{3^{i + k - 1} 5^{i + j - 1}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} + \\frac{15}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{2^{j + k}}{3^{i + k} 5^{i + j}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\sum_{i = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{15^i} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{5} \\right)^j \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^k \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/15}{1 - 1/15} \\cdot \\frac{2/5}{1 - 2/5} \\cdot \\frac{2/3}{1 - 2/3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{\\frac{17}{21}}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{17}{21}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks tricky, because there are infinitely many triples $(a,b,c)$ in $T,$ and the sum might not even converge.", "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the summand $\\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.$", "One idea is to rewrite it as $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^b \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^c 2^a.$", "This suggests that the sum might be easier to deal with if I group the triples by their value of $a.$", "For a fixed $a,$ how many values of $b$ and $c$ are possible?", "Well, the triangle inequality tells me that $a < b + c,$ so I need to find the number of positive integer solutions to $b + c = k,$ where $k$ ranges from $a + 1$ to $2a.$", "For each $k,$ there are $k - 1$ solutions, since $b$ can be any integer from $1$ to $k - 1,$ and then $c = k - b.$", "So the total number of solutions for a fixed $a$ is\n\\[\\sum_{k = a + 1}^{2a} (k - 1) = \\sum_{k = 1}^{2a} k - \\sum_{k = 1}^a k = (2a)(2a + 1)/2 - a(a + 1)/2 = a^2.\\]", "Therefore, the contribution to the sum from the triples with $a = n$ is\n\\[\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^b \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^c 2^n \\cdot n^2.\\]", "To find the total sum, I need to sum this expression over all positive integers $n.$", "This looks like a geometric series with a variable ratio, so I'm not sure how to evaluate it exactly.", "Maybe I can use some approximation or bounding techniques instead.", "For example, I notice that the ratio of consecutive terms is\n\\[\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^b \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^c 2^{n+1} \\cdot (n+1)^2}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^b \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^c 2^n \\cdot n^2} = \\frac{2(n+1)^2}{n^2}.\\]", "This ratio is always greater than $2,$ so the series is divergent.", "That means the sum does not have a finite value.", "Alternatively, I can try to compare the series with another series that I know how to sum.", "For example, I know that the series $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 2^n$ diverges, and it is smaller than the given series, since $n^2 \\geq 1$ for all $n.$", "This also shows that the sum does not have a finite value.", "Therefore, the answer is undefined.", "# Answer\n\nUndefined"], "pre_generated_answer": "Undefined", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.287063731925029e-09}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks tricky, because there are infinitely many triples $(a,b,c)$ in $T,$ and the sum might not even converge.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the summand $\\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One idea is to rewrite it as $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^b \\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)^c 2^a.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "One observation is that $\\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}$ is a rational number with denominator $3^b 5^c,$ which is relatively prime to $2.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that if I take the base-$2$ logarithm of both sides, I get \\[\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c} \\right) = a - b \\log_2 3 - c \\log_2 5.\\]", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that if $(a,b,c)$ is in $T,$ then so is $(ka,kb,kc)$ for any positive integer $k.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One thing I notice is that $\\frac{2^a}{3^b 5^c}$ is the product of $\\frac{2}{3}^b$ and $\\frac{2}{5}^c,$ both of which are less than $1.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 220365, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5a1ab09c-9262-405a-aa6f-b44212a6a84b", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T02:41:11.421674", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the largest value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $|5x-1|=x+3$.", "ground_truth_solution": "We can split the expression $|5x-1|=x+3$ into two separate cases. In the first case, \\begin{align*} 5x-1&=x+3\n\\\\\\Rightarrow \\qquad 4x&=4\n\\\\\\Rightarrow \\qquad x&=1\n\\end{align*}If we plug this value of $x$ back into the original equation in order to check our answer, we see that $|5(1)-1|=1+3$ or $4=4$. Since this is true, we can accept $x=1$ as a valid solution.\n\nIn the second case, \\begin{align*} 5x-1&=-(x+3)\n\\\\ 5x-1&=-x-3\n\\\\\\Rightarrow \\qquad 6x&=-2\n\\\\\\Rightarrow \\qquad x&=-\\frac13.\n\\end{align*}If we plug $-\\frac13$ back into the initial equation, we get that $\\left|5\\left(-\\frac13\\right)-1\\right|=-\\frac13+3$, which simplifies to $\\left|-\\frac{8}{3}\\right|=\\frac{8}{3}$, or $\\frac{8}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}$. Since this is also a true statement, we can accept $x=-\\frac13$ as a valid solution as well. Since both $x=1$ and $x=-\\frac13$ are possible values of $x$, the final answer is the larger of the two: $x=\\boxed{1}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "1", "pre_generated_steps": ["To solve an equation involving absolute value, I need to consider two cases: one where the expression inside the absolute value is positive, and one where it is negative.", "If $5x-1$ is positive, then $|5x-1|=5x-1$ and I can solve the equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides: $4x-1=x+3$, which simplifies to $3x=4$ and $x=\\frac{4}{3}$.", "If $5x-1$ is negative, then $|5x-1|=-(5x-1)$ and I can solve the equation by adding $x$ to both sides: $-4x+1=x+3$, which simplifies to $-5x=2$ and $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$.", "However, I need to check that these values of $x$ actually satisfy the original equation, and not just the simplified ones.", "For $x=\\frac{4}{3}$, I plug it in and get $|5(\\frac{4}{3})-1|=\\frac{4}{3}+3$, which simplifies to $|5-1|=4+3$ and $|4|=7$, which is true.", "So this is a valid solution.", "For $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$, I plug it in and get $|5(-\\frac{2}{5})-1|=-\\frac{2}{5}+3$, which simplifies to $|-2-1|=-\\frac{2}{5}+3$ and $|-3|=-\\frac{2}{5}+3$, which is false.", "So this is not a valid solution.", "Therefore, the only solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}$, which is also the largest value of $x$ that satisfies the equation.", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{4}{3}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{4}{3}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 4.3268334280740736e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To solve an equation involving absolute value, I need to consider two cases: one where the expression inside the absolute value is positive, and one where it is negative.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $5x-1$ is positive, then $|5x-1|=5x-1$ and I can solve the equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides: $4x-1=x+3$, which simplifies to $3x=4$ and $x=\\frac{4}{3}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "If $5x-1$ is positive, then $|5x-1|=5x-1$, and the equation becomes $5x-1=x+3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If $5x-1$ is positive, then I can drop the absolute value bars and get $5x-1=x+3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If $5x-1$ is positive, then I can drop the absolute value and solve for $x$ as usual.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If $5x-1$ is positive, then the equation becomes $5x-1=x+3$, which I can solve by subtracting $x$ from both sides and adding $1$ to both sides, getting $4x=4$ and then dividing by $4$ to get $x=1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 214157, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "30e5a26d-9170-40da-a162-9dbd4b96bbca", "timestamp": "2022-11-15T02:27:41.587219", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $G$ and $H$ denote the centroid and orthocenter of triangle $ABC,$ respectively.   Let $F$ be the midpoint of $\\overline{GH}.$  Express $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2$ in terms of the side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c$ and circumradius $R$ of triangle $ABC.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let the circumcenter $O$ of triangle $ABC$ be the origin.  Then\n\\[\\overrightarrow{G} = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}}{3}\\]and $\\overrightarrow{H} = \\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C},$ so\n\\[\\overrightarrow{F} = \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}).\\]Then\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 &= \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - \\overrightarrow{F}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}) \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\frac{1}{3} \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{B} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{C} \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{A} + 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 4 \\overrightarrow{C} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}  - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Similarly,\n\\begin{align*}\nBF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}), \\\\\nCF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Thus, $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\boxed{3R^2}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "3R^2", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a very geometric problem, so I will start by drawing a diagram of the situation.", "I know that the centroid $G$ is the intersection of the medians of the triangle, and that the orthocenter $H$ is the intersection of the altitudes of the triangle.", "I also know that the circumcenter $O$ is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides, and that it lies on the circumcircle of the triangle with radius $R.$", "I will label the lengths of the medians as $m_a,$ $m_b,$ and $m_c,$ and the lengths of the altitudes as $h_a,$ $h_b,$ and $h_c.$", "I will also label the midpoint of $BC$ as $D,$ the midpoint of $AC$ as $E,$ and the midpoint of $AB$ as $F.$", "I recall a theorem that states that $G$ divides each median in the ratio $2:1,$ so I have $GD = \\frac{2}{3} m_a,$ $GE = \\frac{2}{3} m_b,$ and $GF = \\frac{2}{3} m_c.$", "Similarly, I recall another theorem that states that $H$ divides each altitude in the ratio $n:1,$ where $n = OA \\cot A = OB \\cot B = OC \\cot C.$", "This means that $HD = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_a,$ $HE = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_b,$ and $HF = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_c.$", "I want to find an expression for $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2,$ so I will use the Pythagorean theorem on each of the right triangles $AFD,$ $BFE,$ and $CFG.$", "I get $AF^2 = AD^2 + DF^2,$ $BF^2 = BE^2 + EF^2,$ and $CF^2 = CD^2 + DG^2.$", "I will substitute the expressions for $AD,$ $BE,$ and $CD$ in terms of $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ and the expressions for $DF,$ $EF,$ and $DG$ in terms of $m_a,$ $m_b,$ and $m_c.$", "I get $AF^2 = \\frac{a^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_c\\right)^2,$ $BF^2 = \\frac{b^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_a\\right)^2,$ and $CF^2 = \\frac{c^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_b\\right)^2.$", "Adding these up, I get $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{4} + \\frac{4}{9} (m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2).$", "This is almost the expression I want, but I still need to replace $m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2$ with something in terms of $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $R.$", "I recall another theorem that relates the medians and the circumradius of a triangle, which states that $m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2 = \\frac{3}{4} (a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - 9R^2.$", "I will substitute this into the previous expression and simplify.", "I get $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{4} + \\frac{4}{9} \\left(\\frac{3}{4} (a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - 9R^2\\right).$", "This simplifies to $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{3} - 4R^2.$", "This is the expression I want, so I am done.", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{3} - 4R^2"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{3} - 4R^2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.04720310094199e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a very geometric problem, so I will start by drawing a diagram of the situation.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the centroid $G$ is the intersection of the medians of the triangle, and that the orthocenter $H$ is the intersection of the altitudes of the triangle.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also know that the circumcenter $O$ is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides, and that it lies on the circumcircle of the triangle with radius $R.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will label the lengths of the medians as $m_a,$ $m_b,$ and $m_c,$ and the lengths of the altitudes as $h_a,$ $h_b,$ and $h_c.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will also label the midpoint of $BC$ as $D,$ the midpoint of $AC$ as $E,$ and the midpoint of $AB$ as $F.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall a theorem that states that $G$ divides each median in the ratio $2:1,$ so I have $GD = \\frac{2}{3} m_a,$ $GE = \\frac{2}{3} m_b,$ and $GF = \\frac{2}{3} m_c.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, I recall another theorem that states that $H$ divides each altitude in the ratio $n:1,$ where $n = OA \\cot A = OB \\cot B = OC \\cot C.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $HD = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_a,$ $HE = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_b,$ and $HF = \\frac{n}{n+1} h_c.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to find an expression for $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2,$ so I will use the Pythagorean theorem on each of the right triangles $AFD,$ $BFE,$ and $CFG.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $AF^2 = AD^2 + DF^2,$ $BF^2 = BE^2 + EF^2,$ and $CF^2 = CD^2 + DG^2.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will substitute the expressions for $AD,$ $BE,$ and $CD$ in terms of $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ and the expressions for $DF,$ $EF,$ and $DG$ in terms of $m_a,$ $m_b,$ and $m_c.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $AF^2 = \\frac{a^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_c\\right)^2,$ $BF^2 = \\frac{b^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_a\\right)^2,$ and $CF^2 = \\frac{c^2}{4} + \\left(\\frac{2}{3} m_b\\right)^2.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Adding these up, I get $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{4} + \\frac{4}{9} (m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is almost the expression I want, but I still need to replace $m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2$ with something in terms of $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $R.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall another theorem that relates the medians and the circumradius of a triangle, which states that $m_a^2 + m_b^2 + m_c^2 = \\frac{3}{4} (a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - 9R^2.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will substitute this into the previous expression and simplify.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{4} + \\frac{4}{9} \\left(\\frac{3}{4} (a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - 9R^2\\right).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This simplifies to $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{3} - 4R^2.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is the expression I want, so I am done.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n\\frac{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}{3} - 4R^2", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 53979, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "74a6401e-dfd1-4a50-a999-0d13a74970ac", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T22:25:35.531541", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Four points, $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, are chosen randomly and independently on the circumference of a circle. What is the probability that segments $AB$ and $CD$ intersect?", "ground_truth_solution": "Consider the four random points before they are labeled $A$, $B$, $C$, or $D$. In the general case, they will be distinct, forming a convex quadrilateral. Suppose $A$ is labeled. If $B$ is labeled as the vertex opposite $A$, segments $AB$ and $CD$ will intersect; otherwise, they will not. Since there are 3 points to label as $B$, the probability these segments intersect is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}$. [asy]\ndraw((0,1)..(1,0)..(0,-1)..(-1,0)..cycle);\ndot((0,1)); dot((-5/13,-12/13)); dot((-1,0)); dot((4/5,3/5));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(0,1),N); label(\"$B$\",(-5/13,-12/13),SSW); label(\"$C$\",(-1,0),W); label(\"$D$\",(4/5,3/5),NE);\ndraw((0,1)--(-5/13,-12/13),green); draw((-1,0)--(4/5,3/5),green);\ndraw((0,1)--(4/5,3/5),blue); draw((-1,0)--(-5/13,-12/13),blue);\ndraw((0,1)--(-1,0),red); draw((-5/13,-12/13)--(4/5,3/5),red);\n[/asy] In this diagram, the green edges represent the labeling where $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, and the blue and red edges represent the equally likely labelings where $AB$ and $CD$ do not intersect.", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{1}{3}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves geometry and probability, two topics that I enjoy a lot.", "I will start by drawing a circle and labeling the four points as $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$.", "<insert image of a circle with four points>", "I want to find the probability that $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, so I will draw the two segments as well.", "<insert image of a circle with four points and two segments>", "I notice that there are two cases where the segments do not intersect: either they are both on the same side of a diameter, or they are both parallel to a diameter.", "For example, if I draw a diameter through $A$ and $B$, then $C$ and $D$ must be on the opposite side of the diameter for the segments to intersect.", "<insert image of a circle with four points, two segments, and a diameter through $A$ and $B$>", "Similarly, if I draw a diameter perpendicular to $AB$, then $C$ and $D$ must not be on the same line as the diameter for the segments to intersect.", "<insert image of a circle with four points, two segments, and a diameter perpendicular to $AB$>", "These two cases are mutually exclusive, meaning that they cannot happen at the same time.", "Therefore, I can add the probabilities of these two cases to find the probability that the segments do not intersect, and then subtract that from 1 to find the probability that they do intersect.", "To find the probability of each case, I need to use the fact that the points are chosen randomly and independently on the circumference of the circle.", "This means that the probability of any point being in a given arc is proportional to the measure of that arc in degrees.", "For the first case, where $C$ and $D$ are on the same side of the diameter through $A$ and $B$, the probability is equal to the probability of $C$ being in the arc opposite to $A$ and $B$ times the probability of $D$ being in the same arc.", "Since the arc opposite to $A$ and $B$ has a measure of 180 degrees, the probability of $C$ being in that arc is $\\frac{180}{360} = \\frac{1}{2}$.", "Similarly, the probability of $D$ being in that arc is also $\\frac{1}{2}$.", "Therefore, the probability of this case is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$.", "For the second case, where $C$ and $D$ are on the same line as the diameter perpendicular to $AB$, the probability is equal to the probability of $C$ being on the diameter times the probability of $D$ being on the diameter.", "Since the diameter has a measure of 0 degrees, the probability of $C$ being on the diameter is $\\frac{0}{360} = 0$.", "Similarly, the probability of $D$ being on the diameter is also 0.", "Therefore, the probability of this case is $0 \\times 0 = 0$.", "Now, I can add the probabilities of these two cases to find the probability that the segments do not intersect: $\\frac{1}{4} + 0 = \\frac{1}{4}$.", "Finally, I can subtract this from 1 to find the probability that the segments do intersect: $1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}$.", "# Answer\n\n$\\frac{3}{4}$"], "pre_generated_answer": "$\\frac{3}{4}$", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.8794983927841082e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem involves geometry and probability, two topics that I enjoy a lot.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will start by drawing a circle and labeling the four points as $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "<insert image of a circle with four points>", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to find the probability that $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, so I will draw the two segments as well.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "<insert image of a circle with four points and two segments>", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that there are two cases where the segments do not intersect: either they are both on the same side of a diameter, or they are both parallel to a diameter.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Now, I notice that the two segments intersect if and only if points $A$ and $C$ are on opposite sides of segment $BD$, and points $B$ and $D$ are on opposite sides of segment $AC$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the segments intersect if and only if $C$ is in the smaller arc between $A$ and $B$ and $D$ is in the larger arc between $A$ and $B$ (or vice versa).", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now I can see that there are two possible cases: either the segments intersect or they do not.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the segments intersect if and only if $C$ is between $A$ and $B$ on the circle, or $D$ is between $A$ and $B$ on the circle.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 351272, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "80b82593-ad18-415f-af99-f85db7ef6aab", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T22:22:59.034266", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If\n\\[f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\\]for $n \\ge 1,$ and $f(1) = f(1986),$ compute\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985).\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can list the equations\n\\begin{align*}\nf(2) &= 1 - 2f(1), \\\\\nf(3) &= -2 - 2f(2), \\\\\nf(4) &= 3 - 2f(3), \\\\\nf(5) &= -4 - 2f(4), \\\\\n&\\dots, \\\\\nf(1985) &= -1984 - 2f(1984), \\\\\nf(1986) &= 1985 - 2f(1985).\n\\end{align*}Adding these equations, we get\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = (1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985) - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]To find $1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985,$ we can pair the terms\n\\begin{align*}\n1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985 &= (1 - 2) + (3 - 4) + \\dots + (1983 - 1984) + 1985 \\\\\n&= (-1) + (-1) + \\dots + (-1) + 1985 \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1984}{2} + 1985 \\\\\n&= 993.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = 993 - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]Then\n\\[2f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) + f(1986) = 993.\\]Since $f(1986) = f(1),$\n\\[3f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) = 993.\\]Therefore, $f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) = \\boxed{331}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "331", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is an interesting recursive function, where each term depends on the previous one.", "I notice that the sign of the function alternates depending on whether $n$ is odd or even, because of the $(-1)^{n + 1}$ factor.", "I also notice that the function decreases by $2f(n)$ each time, which means it will eventually become negative and then positive again, depending on the starting value of $f(1).$", "I wonder what the value of $f(1)$ is, and why it is equal to $f(1986).$", "Maybe I can find a pattern by computing some terms of the function.", "If $f(1) = f(1986) = a,$ then $f(2) = -a - 2a = -3a,$ and $f(3) = 2a - 2(-3a) = 8a.$", "I see that the absolute value of the function increases by a factor of 4 each time, and the sign alternates.", "So, I can guess that $f(4) = -8a - 2(8a) = -24a,$ and $f(5) = 24a - 2(-24a) = 72a,$ and so on.", "This means that $f(6) = -72a - 2(72a) = -216a,$ and $f(7) = 216a - 2(-216a) = 648a.$", "This looks like a geometric sequence, where the common ratio is $-4.$", "I can write a general formula for the function in terms of $a$ and $n,$ using the fact that $f(1) = a.$", "It is $f(n) = (-4)^{n - 1} a.$", "Now, I can use this formula to find the value of $a.$", "Since $f(1) = f(1986),$ I have $a = (-4)^{1985} a.$", "This means that $(-4)^{1985} = 1,$ which is only possible if $a = 0.$", "So, the function is actually zero for all values of $n.$", "This makes sense, because if I start with zero, then I always subtract or add zero, and the sign doesn't matter.", "Therefore, the sum of the function from $n = 1$ to $n = 1985$ is simply zero.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 4.3791861373091393e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is an interesting recursive function, where each term depends on the previous one.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the sign of the function alternates depending on whether $n$ is odd or even, because of the $(-1)^{n + 1}$ factor.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also notice that the function decreases by $2f(n)$ each time, which means it will eventually become negative and then positive again, depending on the starting value of $f(1).$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder what the value of $f(1)$ is, and why it is equal to $f(1986).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": n