numbers.", "I also notice that f(1) and f(2) have opposite signs, and so do f(3) and f(4), and so on.", "This means that if I pair up consecutive terms of the sequence, they will cancel out the f(n) part and leave only the constant part.", "For example, f(1) + f(2) = -1, f(3) + f(4) = -1, f(5) + f(6) = -1, and so on.", "In general, f(2k - 1) + f(2k) = -1 for any positive integer k.", "This suggests a strategy to simplify the sum f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + ... + f(1985) by grouping the terms in pairs of two, except for the last term f(1985) which is left alone.", "So, the sum becomes (f(1) + f(2)) + (f(3) + f(4)) + ... + (f(1983) + f(1984)) + f(1985).", "Using the identity I found, each pair of two terms is equal to -1, so there are 992 pairs of -1 in the sum.", "The last term f(1985) is equal to 1984 - 2f(1984), which I can compute using the formula for f(1984).", "I get f(1984) = -1983 - 2f(1983), and f(1983) = 1982 - 2f(1982), and so on, until I reach f(1), which is given to be equal to f(1986).", "So, f(1984) = -1983 - 2(1982 - 2f(1986)), and f(1985) = 1984 - 2(-1983 - 2(1982 - 2f(1986))).", "Simplifying, I get f(1985) = 4f(1986) + 9925.", "Now, I can plug in f(1985) into the sum and get the final answer.", "The sum is -992 + 4f(1986) + 9925, which simplifies to 4f(1986) + 8933.", "# Answer\n\n4f(1986) + 8933"], "pre_generated_answer": "4f(1986) + 8933", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 8.19807881864427e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this is a recursive formula for f, and that it involves alternating signs based on the parity of n.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the expression by grouping terms or using some identity.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I observe that f(n + 1) depends on f(n), so I try to write out a few terms of the sequence