{"labeler": "e7dd34f2-fa17-4e7e-bc66-ac3d5ca993a4", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T22:00:56.691389", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If\n\\[f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\\]for $n \\ge 1,$ and $f(1) = f(1986),$ compute\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985).\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can list the equations\n\\begin{align*}\nf(2) &= 1 - 2f(1), \\\\\nf(3) &= -2 - 2f(2), \\\\\nf(4) &= 3 - 2f(3), \\\\\nf(5) &= -4 - 2f(4), \\\\\n&\\dots, \\\\\nf(1985) &= -1984 - 2f(1984), \\\\\nf(1986) &= 1985 - 2f(1985).\n\\end{align*}Adding these equations, we get\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = (1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985) - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]To find $1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985,$ we can pair the terms\n\\begin{align*}\n1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985 &= (1 - 2) + (3 - 4) + \\dots + (1983 - 1984) + 1985 \\\\\n&= (-1) + (-1) + \\dots + (-1) + 1985 \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1984}{2} + 1985 \\\\\n&= 993.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = 993 - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]Then\n\\[2f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) + f(1986) = 993.\\]Since $f(1986) = f(1),$\n\\[3f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) = 993.\\]Therefore, $f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) = \\boxed{331}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "331", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is an interesting problem involving a recursive function.", "I want to find a way to simplify the expression for the sum of f(1) to f(1985).", "One thing I notice is that f(n) and f(n+1) have opposite signs, because of the (-1)^(n+1) factor.", "That means that if I pair up consecutive terms, they will cancel out, except for the 2f(n) term.", "For example, f(1) + f(2) = (-1)^2 1 - 2f(1) + (-1)^3 2 - 2f(2) = -2f(1) - 2f(2).", "Similarly, f(3) + f(4) = -2f(3) - 2f(4), and so on.", "So, if I group the sum into pairs, I get\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1984) + f(1985) = (-2f(1) - 2f(2)) + (-2f(3) - 2f(4)) + \\dots + (-2f(1984) - 2f(1985)).\\]", "I can factor out -2 and get\n\\[-2(f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1984) + f(1985)) = -2(f(1) + f(2) + \\dots + f(1985)).\\]", "Now, I can divide both sides by -2 and get\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1984) + f(1985) = f(1) + f(2) + \\dots + f(1985).\\]", "This is a bit surprising, but it makes sense, because the left and right sides are the same sum.", "It also tells me that this sum is equal to zero, because any nonzero number times -2 cannot be equal to itself.", "So, the final answer is zero, and I don't even need to know what f(1) or f(1986) are.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.4463831892816865e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is an interesting problem involving a recursive function.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to find a way to simplify the expression for the sum of f(1) to f(1985).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One thing I notice is that f(n) and f(n+1) have opposite signs, because of the (-1)^(n+1) factor.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "One thing I notice is that f(1) and f(1986) are equal, so they will cancel out when I add them.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One thing I notice is that f(1) and f(1986) are equal, by the given condition. So I can write\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) = f(1) + f(1986) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) - f(1986).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that f(n + 1) depends on f(n) and the parity of n, that is, whether n is odd or even.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One thing I notice is that f(1) = f(1986), which means f(2) = f(1987) by applying the recursion formula once.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 160439, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T21:58:58.130274", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If\n\\[f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\\]for $n \\ge 1,$ and $f(1) = f(1986),$ compute\n\\[f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985).\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "We can list the equations\n\\begin{align*}\nf(2) &= 1 - 2f(1), \\\\\nf(3) &= -2 - 2f(2), \\\\\nf(4) &= 3 - 2f(3), \\\\\nf(5) &= -4 - 2f(4), \\\\\n&\\dots, \\\\\nf(1985) &= -1984 - 2f(1984), \\\\\nf(1986) &= 1985 - 2f(1985).\n\\end{align*}Adding these equations, we get\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = (1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985) - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]To find $1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985,$ we can pair the terms\n\\begin{align*}\n1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \\dots + 1983 - 1984 + 1985 &= (1 - 2) + (3 - 4) + \\dots + (1983 - 1984) + 1985 \\\\\n&= (-1) + (-1) + \\dots + (-1) + 1985 \\\\\n&= -\\frac{1984}{2} + 1985 \\\\\n&= 993.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\[f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1986) = 993 - 2f(1) - 2f(2) - \\dots - 2f(1985).\\]Then\n\\[2f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) + f(1986) = 993.\\]Since $f(1986) = f(1),$\n\\[3f(1) + 3f(2) + 3f(3) + \\dots + 3f(1985) = 993.\\]Therefore, $f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) = \\boxed{331}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "331", "pre_generated_steps": ["The first thing I notice is that f(1) = f(1986), so I wonder if there is some kind of periodicity or symmetry in the function.", "To test this idea, I look at some small values of f(n).", "Using the given formula, I get:\n\n\\[f(1) = (-1)^{1 + 1} \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = -2f(1)\\]\n\\[f(2) = (-1)^{2 + 1} \\cdot 2 - 2f(1) = -2 - 2f(1)\\]\n\\[f(3) = (-1)^{3 + 1} \\cdot 3 - 2f(2) = 3 + 4f(1) + 4\\]\n\\[f(4) = (-1)^{4 + 1} \\cdot 4 - 2f(3) = -4 - 6f(1) - 8\\]\n\\[f(5) = (-1)^{5 + 1} \\cdot 5 - 2f(4) = 5 + 8f(1) + 16\\]", "I see a pattern emerging: the sign of f(n) alternates, and the coefficient of f(1) increases by 2 each time.", "The constant term also seems to follow a pattern: it is $(-2)^n$ times the nth triangular number, or $(-2)^n \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2}$.", "Based on this, I conjecture a general formula for f(n):\n\n\\[f(n) = (-1)^{n + 1} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{n(n - 1)}{2} f(1) + (-2)^n \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\right)\\]", "To check if this formula is correct, I plug in n + 1 and see if it satisfies the original recurrence relation:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf(n + 1) &= (-1)^{n + 2} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{(n + 1)n}{2} f(1) + (-2)^{n + 1} \\cdot \\frac{(n + 1)(n + 2)}{2} \\right) \\\\\n&= (-1)^{n + 1} \\cdot \\left( -n f(1) - 2(-2)^n \\cdot \\frac{(n + 1)(n + 2)}{2} \\right) \\\\\n&= (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\n\\end{align*}", "So the formula is indeed correct.", "Now I can use it to compute the sum that I want.", "I notice that the sum has 1985 terms, which is odd, so the sign of the last term is the same as the sign of the first term, which is negative.", "This means that the sum of the coefficients of f(1) is zero, since they cancel out in pairs.", "Therefore, the only thing that matters is the sum of the constant terms.", "I can use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic-geometric series to get:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{n = 1}^{1985} f(n) &= \\sum_{n = 1}^{1985} (-1)^{n + 1} \\cdot (-2)^n \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\\\\n&= -2 \\sum_{n = 1}^{1985} (-2)^{n - 1} \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} \\\\\n&= -2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\frac{(-2)^{1985} \\cdot (1985 + 1)}{(-2) - 1} - \\frac{1 \\cdot (1 + 1)}{(-2) - 1} \\right] \\\\\n&= -2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\frac{(-2)^{1986} - 2}{-3} - \\frac{2}{-3} \\right] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\left[ (-2)^{1986} - 2 + 2 \\right] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} (-2)^{1986}\n\\end{align*}\n\n# Answer\n\n\\frac{1}{3} (-2)^{1986}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{1}{3} (-2)^{1986}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0014931460469543392}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "The first thing I notice is that f(1) = f(1986), so I wonder if there is some kind of periodicity or symmetry in the function.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To test this idea, I look at some small values of f(n).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Using the given formula, I get:\n\n\\[f(1) = (-1)^{1 + 1} \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = -2f(1)\\]\n\\[f(2) = (-1)^{2 + 1} \\cdot 2 - 2f(1) = -2 - 2f(1)\\]\n\\[f(3) = (-1)^{3 + 1} \\cdot 3 - 2f(2) = 3 + 4f(1) + 4\\]\n\\[f(4) = (-1)^{4 + 1} \\cdot 4 - 2f(3) = -4 - 6f(1) - 8\\]\n\\[f(5) = (-1)^{5 + 1} \\cdot 5 - 2f(4) = 5 + 8f(1) + 16\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I know that f(1) = f(1986), so I can choose any value for f(1) and it will be consistent. Let me choose f(1) = 1 for simplicity.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "When n = 1, f(2) = -1 - 2f(1) = -1 - f(1986), using the given condition.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "f(1) = f(1986) by given, so let's call this value a.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If n = 1, f(2) = -1 - 2f(1) = -1 - 2f(1986), so f(2) and f(1987) are related by the same equation.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 277697, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "d2e87ba0-0318-4fc8-ab0d-e8a3d082fff8", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T23:41:28.298315", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $P(x)$ be a monic polynomial of degree 3.  Suppose that $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ and remainder $2R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3).$  Given that $P(0) = 5,$ find $P(5).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 5.$  The remainder $R(x)$ has degree at most 1, so let $R(x) = cx + d.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ the quotient is of the form $x + p,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + cx + d.\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = p - 5.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ the quotient is of the form $x + q,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2(cx + d).\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = q - 5.$  Hence, $p = q.$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x$ in both equations, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nb &= c - 5p + 4, \\\\\nb &= 2c - 5p + 6.\n\\end{align*}Subtracting these equations, we get $c + 2 = 0,$ so $c = -2.$\n\nComparing the constant coefficients in the first equation, we get $5 = 4p + d.$  Therefore,\n\\[P(5) = (5 + p)(4)(1) - 10 + d = 10 + 4p + d = \\boxed{15}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["To solve this problem, I will use the fact that the remainder of a polynomial division is a linear combination of the values of the polynomial at the roots of the divisor.", "For example, if $Q(x)$ is the quotient of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ then $P(x) = Q(x)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x),$ and $R(x) = P(1) + P(4)(x - 1).$", "Similarly, if $S(x)$ is the quotient of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ then $P(x) = S(x)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x),$ and $2R(x) = 2P(2) + 2P(3)(x - 2).$", "Now, I can equate the coefficients of $x$ and $x^0$ in the two expressions for $2R(x),$ and get a system of equations involving $P(1), P(2), P(3),$ and $P(4).$", "The system is:\n\n$2P(2) + 2P(3) = P(1) + P(4)$\n\n$2P(2) + 4P(3) = P(1) + 4P(4)$", "Subtracting the first equation from the second, I get:\n\n$2P(3) = 3P(4)$", "This implies that $P(4)$ is a multiple of $P(3),$ and since $P(x)$ is monic, it must have a repeated root at $x = 3$ or $x = 4.$", "If $P(3) = 0,$ then $P(4) = 0$ as well, and $R(x) = 0,$ which contradicts the fact that $P(0) = 5.$", "Therefore, $P(4) = 0$ and $P(3) \\neq 0,$ and $R(x) = P(1)(x - 4).$", "Plugging in $x = 0,$ I get $R(0) = -4P(1) = 5,$ so $P(1) = -\\frac{5}{4}.$", "Now, I can use the fact that $P(x)$ is monic of degree 3, and has roots at $x = 4$ and $x = 3,$ to write $P(x) = (x - 4)(x - 3)(x - a),$ where $a$ is the third root.", "To find $a,$ I can plug in $x = 0$ and use the fact that $P(0) = 5,$ and get $-12a = 5,$ so $a = -\\frac{5}{12}.$", "Therefore, $P(x) = (x - 4)(x - 3)(x + \\frac{5}{12}).$", "To find $P(5),$ I can simply plug in $x = 5$ and simplify, and get $P(5) = (5 - 4)(5 - 3)(5 + \\frac{5}{12}) = 10 \\cdot \\frac{65}{12} = \\frac{325}{6}.$", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{325}{6}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{325}{6}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 7.829890120927699e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To solve this problem, I will use the fact that the remainder of a polynomial division is a linear combination of the values of the polynomial at the roots of the divisor.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, if $Q(x)$ is the quotient of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ then $P(x) = Q(x)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x),$ and $R(x) = P(1) + P(4)(x - 1).$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, if $S(x)$ is the quotient of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ then $P(x) = S(x)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x),$ and $2R(x) = 2P(2) + 2P(3)(x - 2).$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, I can equate the coefficients of $x$ and $x^0$ in the two expressions for $2R(x),$ and get a system of equations involving $P(1), P(2), P(3),$ and $P(4).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The system is:\n\n$2P(2) + 2P(3) = P(1) + P(4)$\n\n$2P(2) + 4P(3) = P(1) + 4P(4)$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The system is:\n\n$2P(1) + 2P(4) = 4P(2) + 4P(3),$\n$P(1) = P(2) + P(3).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Coefficient of $x$: $2P(4) = 2P(3)$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The system is:\n\n$P(1) + P(4) = 2P(2) + 2P(3)$\n\n$P(1) + 4P(4) = 4P(2) + 6P(3)$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The coefficient of $x$ is $P(4) - 2P(3),$ and the coefficient of $x^0$ is $2P(2) - P(1).$ So, I have\n$P(4) - 2P(3) = 2P(3) - 2P(2)$ and\n$2P(2) - P(1) = P(1) + P(4).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 3014360, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "e20e861c-11d7-4a17-ac55-818f71d72641", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T23:14:05.900237", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $G$ and $H$ denote the centroid and orthocenter of triangle $ABC,$ respectively.   Let $F$ be the midpoint of $\\overline{GH}.$  Express $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2$ in terms of the side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c$ and circumradius $R$ of triangle $ABC.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let the circumcenter $O$ of triangle $ABC$ be the origin.  Then\n\\[\\overrightarrow{G} = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}}{3}\\]and $\\overrightarrow{H} = \\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C},$ so\n\\[\\overrightarrow{F} = \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}).\\]Then\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 &= \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - \\overrightarrow{F}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}) \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\frac{1}{3} \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{B} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{C} \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{A} + 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 4 \\overrightarrow{C} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}  - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Similarly,\n\\begin{align*}\nBF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}), \\\\\nCF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Thus, $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\boxed{3R^2}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "3R^2", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a geometry problem that involves a lot of concepts: centroid, orthocenter, midpoint, circumradius, and the Pythagorean theorem.", "I recall that the centroid is the point of intersection of the medians of a triangle, and that it divides each median in a 2:1 ratio.", "The orthocenter is the point of intersection of the altitudes of a triangle, and it can lie inside, outside, or on the triangle depending on the angle types.", "The circumradius is the radius of the circumcircle, which is the circle that passes through all the vertices of the triangle.", "The circumcenter, which is the center of the circumcircle, is the point of intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle.", "I wonder if there is a relationship between the centroid, orthocenter, and circumcenter that could help me solve this problem.", "I remember that there is a theorem called the Euler line, which states that the centroid, orthocenter, and circumcenter are collinear, and that the centroid divides the segment from the orthocenter to the circumcenter in a 2:1 ratio.", "That means that $F$ is actually the circumcenter of triangle $ABC.$", "Now I have a way to relate $F$ to the side lengths and the circumradius of the triangle.", "I know that the distance from $F$ to any vertex of the triangle is equal to the circumradius $R.$", "So I can write $AF = BF = CF = R.$", "Then, by the Pythagorean theorem, I have\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 &= R^2 + R^2 + R^2\\\\\n&= 3R^2.\n\\end{align*}", "But I still need to express $R$ in terms of $a,$ $b,$ $c.$", "I recall that there is a formula for the circumradius of a triangle in terms of its side lengths and its area.", "It is given by\n\\[R = \\frac{abc}{4A},\\]\nwhere $A$ is the area of the triangle.", "I also know that the area of the triangle can be computed using Heron's formula, which is\n\\[A = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)},\\]\nwhere $s$ is the semiperimeter of the triangle, given by\n\\[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2}.\\]", "So, substituting these formulas into the expression for $R,$ I get\n\\begin{align*}\nR &= \\frac{abc}{4\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}\\\\\n&= \\frac{abc}{4\\sqrt{\\frac{a+b+c}{2}\\left(\\frac{a+b+c}{2}-a\\right)\\left(\\frac{a+b+c}{2}-b\\right)\\left(\\frac{a+b+c}{2}-c\\right)}}\\\\\n&= \\frac{2abc}{\\sqrt{(a+b+c)(b+c-a)(c+a-b)(a+b-c)}}.\n\\end{align*}", "Now I can square this expression and plug it into the previous one to get\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 &= 3R^2\\\\\n&= 3\\left(\\frac{2abc}{\\sqrt{(a+b+c)(b+c-a)(c+a-b)(a+b-c)}}\\right)^2\\\\\n&= \\frac{36a^2b^2c^2}{(a+b+c)(b+c-a)(c+a-b)(a+b-c)}.\n\\end{align*}", "This is my final answer.", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{36a^2b^2c^2}{(a+b+c)(b+c-a)(c+a-b)(a+b-c)}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{36a^2b^2c^2}{(a+b+c)(b+c-a)(c+a-b)(a+b-c)}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.6837769906634395e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a geometry problem that involves a lot of concepts: centroid, orthocenter, midpoint, circumradius, and the Pythagorean theorem.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that the centroid is the point of intersection of the medians of a triangle, and that it divides each median in a 2:1 ratio.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The orthocenter is the point of intersection of the altitudes of a triangle, and it can lie inside, outside, or on the triangle depending on the angle types.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The circumradius is the radius of the circumcircle, which is the circle that passes through all the vertices of the triangle.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The circumcenter, which is the center of the circumcircle, is the point of intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the triangle.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if there is a relationship between the centroid, orthocenter, and circumcenter that could help me solve this problem.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I remember that there is a theorem called the Euler line, which states that the centroid, orthocenter, and circumcenter are collinear, and that the centroid divides the segment from the orthocenter to the circumcenter in a 2:1 ratio.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That means that $F$ is actually the circumcenter of triangle $ABC.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "That means that if I let $O$ be the circumcenter, then $G$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HO}$, and $F$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{GO}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that if I let $O$ be the circumcenter, then $OG = 2GH = 2GF$ and $OH = 3GH = 3GF$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that I can label the circumcenter as $O$ and use the fact that $GO = 2GF$ and $FO = 2GH$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that if I let $O$ be the circumcenter, then $F$ is also the midpoint of $\\overline{OH}$, and $OF = 2FH = \\frac{1}{3}OH$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion