{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T23:01:21.662947", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $G$ and $H$ denote the centroid and orthocenter of triangle $ABC,$ respectively.   Let $F$ be the midpoint of $\\overline{GH}.$  Express $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2$ in terms of the side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c$ and circumradius $R$ of triangle $ABC.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let the circumcenter $O$ of triangle $ABC$ be the origin.  Then\n\\[\\overrightarrow{G} = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}}{3}\\]and $\\overrightarrow{H} = \\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C},$ so\n\\[\\overrightarrow{F} = \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}).\\]Then\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 &= \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - \\overrightarrow{F}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}) \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\frac{1}{3} \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{B} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{C} \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{A} + 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 4 \\overrightarrow{C} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}  - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Similarly,\n\\begin{align*}\nBF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}), \\\\\nCF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Thus, $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\boxed{3R^2}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "3R^2", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the centroid is the intersection of the medians, and the orthocenter is the intersection of the altitudes of a triangle.", "I also recall that the centroid divides each median in a 2:1 ratio, and that the nine-point circle passes through the midpoints of the segments joining the orthocenter and the vertices.", "This means that F is the center of the nine-point circle, and the distances AF, BF, CF are equal to the radius of the nine-point circle.", "So, I need to find an expression for the radius of the nine-point circle in terms of the given quantities.", "I remember that the radius of the nine-point circle is half the radius of the circumcircle, so $AF = BF = CF = \\frac{R}{2}.$", "Therefore, $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = 3(\\frac{R}{2})^2 = \\frac{3R^2}{4}.$", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{3R^2}{4}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{3R^2}{4}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0003134443999243636}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the centroid is the intersection of the medians, and the orthocenter is the intersection of the altitudes of a triangle.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also recall that the centroid divides each median in a 2:1 ratio, and that the nine-point circle passes through the midpoints of the segments joining the orthocenter and the vertices.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that F is the center of the nine-point circle, and the distances AF, BF, CF are equal to the radius of the nine-point circle.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "To express $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2$ in terms of the given quantities, I might try to use the law of cosines or some other relation that involves angles and sides of the triangle.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So $F$ is the center of the nine-point circle, and $GF = GH/2$ and $HF = GH/2.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, the points $G$ and $H$ are both on the nine-point circle, and $F$ is their midpoint. This means that $F$ is the center of the nine-point circle.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that $F$ is the center of the nine-point circle, and $FA$, $FB$, $FC$ are radii of this circle.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 259962, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "32b4241c-8316-48f0-8bdc-30c3ba23ee96", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T23:00:46.792020", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $G$ and $H$ denote the centroid and orthocenter of triangle $ABC,$ respectively.   Let $F$ be the midpoint of $\\overline{GH}.$  Express $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2$ in terms of the side lengths $a,$ $b,$ $c$ and circumradius $R$ of triangle $ABC.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let the circumcenter $O$ of triangle $ABC$ be the origin.  Then\n\\[\\overrightarrow{G} = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}}{3}\\]and $\\overrightarrow{H} = \\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C},$ so\n\\[\\overrightarrow{F} = \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}).\\]Then\n\\begin{align*}\nAF^2 &= \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - \\overrightarrow{F}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} (\\overrightarrow{A} + \\overrightarrow{B} + \\overrightarrow{C}) \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\left\\| \\frac{1}{3} \\overrightarrow{A} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{B} - \\frac{2}{3} \\overrightarrow{C} \\right\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\|\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}\\|^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\cdot (\\overrightarrow{A} - 2 \\overrightarrow{B} - 2 \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (\\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{A} + 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 4 \\overrightarrow{C} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}  - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} + 8 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Similarly,\n\\begin{align*}\nBF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}), \\\\\nCF^2 &= \\frac{1}{9} (9R^2 + 8 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{B} - 4 \\overrightarrow{A} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C} - 4 \\overrightarrow{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{C}).\n\\end{align*}Thus, $AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = \\boxed{3R^2}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "3R^2", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a challenging geometry problem that involves some special points and properties of triangles.", "I will start by drawing a diagram to help me visualize the problem.", "I know that the centroid $G$ is the intersection of the medians of the triangle, which are the segments that connect each vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.", "I also know that the orthocenter $H$ is the intersection of the altitudes of the triangle, which are the perpendicular segments from each vertex to the line containing the opposite side.", "I also know that the midpoint $F$ of $\\overline{GH}$ has some special properties.", "In fact, it is the center of the nine-point circle of the triangle, which is the circle that passes through the midpoints of the sides, the feet of the altitudes, and the midpoints of the segments connecting the orthocenter to the vertices.", "I can use this property to relate $F$ to the circumcenter $O$ of the triangle, which is the center of the circumscribed circle that passes through the vertices.", "It turns out that $O, H, G, F$ are collinear, and $O$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{HF}.$", "This means that $\\overline{OF} = \\frac{1}{2} \\overline{HF}$ and $\\overline{OG} = \\frac{1}{2} \\overline{OH}.$", "Now I can use the Pythagorean theorem to relate the lengths of $\\overline{AF}, \\overline{BF}, \\overline{CF}$ to the lengths of $\\overline{AO}, \\overline{BO}, \\overline{CO},$ which are the radii of the circumcircle, and thus equal to $R.$", "I have\n\n\\[AF^2 = AO^2 - OF^2 = R^2 - \\frac{1}{4} HF^2\\]\n\\[BF^2 = BO^2 - OF^2 = R^2 - \\frac{1}{4} HF^2\\]\n\\[CF^2 = CO^2 - OF^2 = R^2 - \\frac{1}{4} HF^2\\]", "Adding these equations, I get\n\n\\[AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = 3R^2 - \\frac{3}{4} HF^2\\]", "So I have reduced the problem to finding $HF^2$ in terms of the side lengths and $R.$", "To do this, I will use another property of the orthocenter and the circumcenter.", "It turns out that $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle HAO$ are similar, since they share the angle at $A$ and the right angles at $H$ and $O.$", "This means that the ratio of their corresponding sides is constant, and I can write\n\n\\[\\frac{AH}{AO} = \\frac{HO}{AC} = \\frac{AO}{AB}\\]", "Simplifying this, I get\n\n\\[AH \\cdot AB = AO^2 = R^2\\]\n\\[HO \\cdot AC = AO^2 = R^2\\]", "Adding these equations, I get\n\n\\[AH \\cdot AB + HO \\cdot AC = 2R^2\\]", "Now I can use the Pythagorean theorem again, this time on $\\triangle AHF$ and $\\triangle HOF,$ to get\n\n\\[AH^2 + HF^2 = AF^2\\]\n\\[HO^2 + HF^2 = OF^2\\]", "Adding these equations, I get\n\n\\[AH^2 + HO^2 + 2HF^2 = AF^2 + OF^2\\]", "Substituting the values I found earlier, I get\n\n\\[2R^2 + 2HF^2 = 3R^2 - \\frac{1}{4} HF^2 + \\frac{1}{4} HF^2\\]", "Simplifying this, I get\n\n\\[HF^2 = R^2\\]", "Finally, I can plug this into the expression I wanted to find, and get\n\n\\[AF^2 + BF^2 + CF^2 = 3R^2 - \\frac{3}{4} R^2 = \\frac{9}{4} R^2\\]\n\n# Answer\n\n\\frac{9}{4} R^2"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{9}{4} R^2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.528963865550549e-09}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a challenging geometry problem that involves some special points and properties of triangles.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will start by drawing a diagram to help me visualize the problem.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completion