add them up to get the final answer.", "# Answer\n\n11 + \\sqrt{532}"], "pre_generated_answer": "11 + \\sqrt{532}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0008918189044864862}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the given condition for $P$ is the definition of a hyperbola with foci $F_1$ and $F_2$ and a constant difference of $24$ between the distances to the foci.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also recall that the center of a hyperbola is the midpoint of the segment joining the foci, so I can find the coordinates of the center $(h,k)$ by averaging the coordinates of $F_1$ and $F_2$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I calculate that $h = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3$ and $k = \\frac{2 + 2}{2} = 2$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the distance between the foci of a hyperbola is $2c$, where $c^2 = a^2 + b^2$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can find $c$ by using the distance formula on $F_1$ and $F_2$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get that $c = \\sqrt{(10 - (-16))^2 + (2 - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{676} = 26$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also remember that the constant difference of $24$ between the distances to the foci is equal to $2a$, where $a$ is the distance from the center to a vertex of the hyperbola.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I solve for $a$ and get that $a = \\frac{24}{2} = 12$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I substitute $a$ and $c$ into the equation $c^2 = a^2 + b^2$ and solve for $b$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get that $b^2 = c^2 - a^2 = 676 - 144 = 532$ and $b = \\sqrt{532}$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I have found all the values I need, so I add them up to get the final answer.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n11 + \\sqrt{532}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 202399, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "fbc13b41-a7f0-4f72-b9b9-bb66101e39f3", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T04:23:02.123729", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $P(x)$ be a monic polynomial of degree 3.  Suppose that $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ and remainder $2R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3).$  Given that $P(0) = 5,$ find $P(5).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 5.$  The remainder $R(x)$ has degree at most 1, so let $R(x) = cx + d.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ the quotient is of the form $x + p,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + cx + d.\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = p - 5.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ the quotient is of the form $x + q,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2(cx + d).\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = q - 5.$  Hence, $p = q.$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x$ in both equations, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nb &= c - 5p + 4, \\\\\nb &= 2c - 5p + 6.\n\\end{align*}Subtracting these equations, we get $c + 2 = 0,$ so $c = -2.$\n\nComparing the constant coefficients in the first equation, we get $5 = 4p + d.$  Therefore,\n\\[P(5) = (5 + p)(4)(1) - 10 + d = 10 + 4p + d = \\boxed{15}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["A monic polynomial means that the leading coefficient is 1, so $P(x)$ has the form $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c.$", "The remainder theorem says that the remainder of dividing a polynomial by a linear factor $(x - k)$ is equal to the value of the polynomial at $x = k.$", "So, if $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ then we must have $R(1) = P(1)$ and $R(4) = P(4).$", "Similarly, if $P(x)$ has remainder $2R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ then we must have $2R(2) = P(2)$ and $2R(3) = P(3).$", "Since $R(x)$ is a polynomial of degree less than 2, it must have the form $R(x) = dx + e.$", "Substituting the values of $R(1), R(2), R(3),$ and $R(4),$ we get a system of four equations in four unknowns:\n\\begin{align*}\nd + e &= P(1)\\\\\n2d + e &= P(4)\\\\\n4d + e &= P(2)\\\\\n6d + e &= P(3)\n\\end{align*}", "Subtracting the first equation from the second, we get $d = P(4) - P(1).$", "Substituting this into the third equation, we get $e = P(2) - 3P(4) + 3P(1).$", "Now we can use the fact that $P(0) = 5$ to find $c,$ the constant term of $P(x).$", "Since $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial, we have\n\\[P(0) = 0^3 + a(0)^2 + b(0) + c = c.\\]", "So, $c = 5.$", "Next, we can use synthetic division to find the other coefficients of $P(x).$", "Since $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ we can write\n\\[P(x) = (x - 1)(x - 4)Q(x) + R(x),\\]\nwhere $Q(x)$ is a quotient polynomial of degree 1.", "Performing synthetic division with $x = 1,$ we get\n\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n1 & 1 & a & b & c\\\\\n &  & 1 & a + 1 & b + a + 1\\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & a + 1 & b + a + 1 & c + b + a + 1\n\\end{array}\\]\nThe last entry in the bottom row is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 1,$ which is equal to $R(1).$", "Therefore, we have\n\\[R(1) = c + b + a + 1 = 5 + b + a + 1.\\]", "Similarly, performing synthetic division with $x = 4,$ we get\n\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n4 & 1 & a & b & c\\\\\n &  & 4 & 4a + 16 & 4b + 16a + 64\\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & a + 4 & b + 4a + 16 & c + 4b + 16a + 64\n\\end{array}\\]\nThe last entry in the bottom row is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 4,$ which is equal to $R(4).$ Therefore, we have\n\\[R(4) = c + 4b + 16a + 64 = 5 + 4b + 16a + 64.\\]", "Now we have two equations in two unknowns $a$ and $b.$", "Solving for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\\[a = -5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = -11.\\]", "Therefore, we have found that\n\\[P(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 - 11x + 5.\\]", "To find $P(5),$ we simply plug in $x = 5$ and evaluate:\n\\[P(5) = 5^3 - 5(5)^2 - 11(5) + 5 = 125 - 125 - 55 + 5 = -50.\\]", "# Answer\n\n-"], "pre_generated_answer": "-", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.8158572125217675e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "A monic polynomial means that the leading coefficient is 1, so $P(x)$ has the form $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The remainder theorem says that the remainder of dividing a polynomial by a linear factor $(x - k)$ is equal to the value of the polynomial at $x = k.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, if $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ then we must have $R(1) = P(1)$ and $R(4) = P(4).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, if $P(x)$ has remainder $2R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ then we must have $2R(2) = P(2)$ and $2R(3) = P(3).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $R(x)$ is a polynomial of degree less than 2, it must have the form $R(x) = dx + e.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Substituting the values of $R(1), R(2), R(3),$ and $R(4),$ we get a system of four equations in four unknowns:\n\\begin{align*}\nd + e &= P(1)\\\\\n2d + e &= P(4)\\\\\n4d + e &= P(2)\\\\\n6d + e &= P(3)\n\\end{align*}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Subtracting the first equation from the second, we get $d = P(4) - P(1).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Substituting this into the third equation, we get $e = P(2) - 3P(4) + 3P(1).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now we can use the fact that $P(0) = 5$ to find $c,$ the constant term of $P(x).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial, we have\n\\[P(0) = 0^3 + a(0)^2 + b(0) + c = c.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, $c = 5.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Next, we can use synthetic division to find the other coefficients of $P(x).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ we can write\n\\[P(x) = (x - 1)(x - 4)Q(x) + R(x),\\]\nwhere $Q(x)$ is a quotient polynomial of degree 1.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Performing synthetic division with $x = 1,$ we get\n\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n1 & 1 & a & b & c\\\\\n &  & 1 & a + 1 & b + a + 1\\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & a + 1 & b + a + 1 & c + b + a + 1\n\\end{array}\\]\nThe last entry in the bottom row is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 1,$ which is equal to $R(1).$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, we have\n\\[R(1) = c + b + a + 1 = 5 + b + a + 1.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, performing synthetic division with $x = 4,$ we get\n\\[\\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n4 & 1 & a & b & c\\\\\n &  & 4 & 4a + 16 & 4b + 16a + 64\\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & a + 4 & b + 4a + 16 & c + 4b + 16a + 64\n\\end{array}\\]\nThe last entry in the bottom row is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $x - 4,$ which is equal to $R(4).$ Therefore, we have\n\\[R(4) = c + 4b + 16a + 64 = 5 + 4b + 16a + 64.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now we have two equations in two unknowns $a$ and $b.$", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Solving for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\\[a = -5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = -11.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, we have found that\n\\[P(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 - 11x + 5.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find $P(5),$ we simply plug in $x = 5$ and evaluate:\n\\[P(5) = 5^3 - 5(5)^2 - 11(5) + 5 = 125 - 125 - 55 + 5 = -50.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n-", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 354351, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T04:19:28.557017", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $P(x)$ be a monic polynomial of degree 3.  Suppose that $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ and remainder $2R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3).$  Given that $P(0) = 5,$ find $P(5).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 5.$  The remainder $R(x)$ has degree at most 1, so let $R(x) = cx + d.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ the quotient is of the form $x + p,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + cx + d.\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = p - 5.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ the quotient is of the form $x + q,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2(cx + d).\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = q - 5.$  Hence, $p = q.$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x$ in both equations, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nb &= c - 5p + 4, \\\\\nb &= 2c - 5p + 6.\n\\end{align*}Subtracting these equations, we get $c + 2 = 0,$ so $c = -2.$\n\nComparing the constant coefficients in the first equation, we get $5 = 4p + d.$  Therefore,\n\\[P(5) = (5 + p)(4)(1) - 10 + d = 10 + 4p + d = \\boxed{15}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["A monic polynomial of degree 3 means that the leading coefficient is 1, so $P(x)$ has the form $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c$ for some constants $a, b, c.$", "Since $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ that means $P(x) = Q(x)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x)$ for some polynomial $Q(x).$", "Similarly, since $P(x)$ has remainder $2R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ that means $P(x) = S(x)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x)$ for some polynomial $S(x).$", "I want to find a way to relate $Q(x)$ and $S(x),$ and also to find $R(x).$", "One way to do that is to use the fact that $P(0) = 5.$", "Plugging in $x = 0$ into both equations, I get\n\n$5 = Q(0)(-1)(-4) + R(0)$\n\nand\n\n$5 = S(0)(-2)(-3) + 2R(0).$", "I can solve for $R(0)$ from the first equation, and get $R(0) = 5 + 4Q(0).$", "Then I can substitute that into the second equation, and get\n\n$5 = S(0)(-2)(-3) + 2(5 + 4Q(0))$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$S(0) = 2Q(0) + \\frac{5}{3}.$", "This gives me a relationship between $Q(0)$ and $S(0),$ but I still don't know their values.", "Maybe I can find another value of $x$ that makes $P(x)$ easy to compute, and use that to get another equation.", "Since $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial, it has at most three roots, and I already know two of them: $x = 1$ and $x = 4.$", "So the third root, if it exists, must be the other factor of $P(x),$ and it must be different from 1, 2, 3, and 4.", "Let me call the third root $r,$ and write $P(x) = (x - 1)(x - 4)(x - r).$", "Then I can expand $P(x)$ and compare it to the original form, and get\n\n$P(x) = x^3 - (5 + r)x^2 + (4 + 5r)x - 4r = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c.$", "This means that $a = -(5 + r),$ $b = 4 + 5r,$ and $c = -4r.$", "Since I know that $P(0) = 5,$ I can plug in $x = 0$ and get\n\n$5 = P(0) = -4r,$\n\nwhich implies that $r = -\\frac{5}{4}.$", "Now I have found the third root of $P(x),$ and I can also find the other coefficients: $a = -\\frac{15}{4},$ $b = \\frac{45}{4},$ and $c = 5.$", "So $P(x) = x^3 - \\frac{15}{4}x^2 + \\frac{45}{4}x + 5.$", "To find $P(5),$ I just need to plug in $x = 5$ and simplify.", "# Answer\n\n30"], "pre_generated_answer": "30", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 7.906570950004884e-09}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "A monic polynomial of degree 3 means that the leading coefficient is 1, so $P(x)$ has the form $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c$ for some constants $a, b, c.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ that means $P(x) = Q(x)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x)$ for some polynomial $Q(x).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, since $P(x)$ has remainder $2R(x)$ when divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ that means $P(x) = S(x)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x)$ for some polynomial $S(x).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to find a way to relate $Q(x)$ and $S(x),$ and also to find $R(x).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to do that is to use the fact that $P(0) = 5.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in $x = 0$ into both equations, I get\n\n$5 = Q(0)(-1)(-4) + R(0)$\n\nand\n\n$5 = S(0)(-2)(-3) + 2R(0).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can solve for $R(0)$ from the first equation, and get $R(0) = 5 + 4Q(0).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Solving for $Q(0)$ and $S(0),$ I get\n\n$Q(0) = \\frac{5 - R(0)}{4}$\n\nand\n\n$S(0) = \\frac{5 - 2R(0)}{6}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\n$5 = 4Q(0) + R(0)$\n\nand\n\n$5 = 6S(0) + 2R(0).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\n$R(0) = 5 + 4Q(0)$\n\nand\n\n$R(0) = \\frac{5}{2} + 3S(0).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Solving for $R(0),$ I get $R(0) = -4Q(0)$ and $R(0) = -\\frac{5}{2} + 3S(0).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 253451, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "fbc13b41-a7f0-4f72-b9b9-bb66101e39f3", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T04:16:58.950805", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $P(x)$ be a monic polynomial of degree 3.  Suppose that $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ and remainder $2R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3).$  Given that $P(0) = 5,$ find $P(5).$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + 5.$  The remainder $R(x)$ has degree at most 1, so let $R(x) = cx + d.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ the quotient is of the form $x + p,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + R(x) = (x + p)(x - 1)(x - 4) + cx + d.\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = p - 5.$\n\nWhen $P(x)$ is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ the quotient is of the form $x + q,$ so write\n\\[P(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2R(x) = (x + q)(x - 2)(x - 3) + 2(cx + d).\\]Comparing the coefficients of $x^2,$ we get $a = q - 5.$  Hence, $p = q.$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x$ in both equations, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nb &= c - 5p + 4, \\\\\nb &= 2c - 5p + 6.\n\\end{align*}Subtracting these equations, we get $c + 2 = 0,$ so $c = -2.$\n\nComparing the constant coefficients in the first equation, we get $5 = 4p + d.$  Therefore,\n\\[P(5) = (5 + p)(4)(1) - 10 + d = 10 + 4p + d = \\boxed{15}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["I see that this problem involves the remainder theorem, which says that the remainder of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - a)$ is equal to $P(a).$", "So, for example, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 1),$ I get a remainder of $R(1),$ which is also equal to $P(1).$", "Similarly, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 4),$ I get a remainder of $R(4),$ which is also equal to $P(4).$", "And if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 2),$ I get a remainder of $2R(2),$ which is also equal to $P(2).$", "And so on.", "Now, since $P(x)$ is a monic polynomial of degree 3, I can write it in the form $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c,$ where $a, b,$ and $c$ are some constants that I need to find.", "I can use the given information to set up a system of equations involving these constants.", "For example, from $P(0) = 5,$ I get $c = 5.$", "From $P(1) = R(1),$ I get $1 + a + b + c = R(1).$", "From $P(4) = R(4),$ I get $64 + 16a + 4b + c = R(4).$", "From $P(2) = 2R(2),$ I get $8 + 4a + 2b + c = 2R(2).$", "And from $P(3) = 2R(3),$ I get $27 + 9a + 3b + c = 2R(3).$", "Now, I have five equations and six unknowns, which means I have one degree of freedom.", "This makes sense, because I can choose any value for $R(x),$ as long as it is a polynomial of degree 1, and then find the corresponding values of $a, b,$ and $c$ that make the system consistent.", "For simplicity, I will choose $R(x) = x,$ but any other linear polynomial would work as well.", "Plugging in $R(x) = x,$ I get the following system:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nc &= 5 \\\\\n1 + a + b + c &= 1 \\\\\n64 + 16a + 4b + c &= 4 \\\\\n8 + 4a + 2b + c &= 4 \\\\\n27 + 9a + 3b + c &= 6\n\\end{align*}", "I can subtract the first equation from the others to eliminate $c,$ and then use elimination or substitution to solve for $a$ and $b.$", "Doing so, I get $a = -3$ and $b = 4.$", "Therefore, the polynomial $P(x)$ is $P(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x + 5.$", "To find $P(5),$ I just plug in $x = 5$ and simplify.", "I get $P(5) = 5^3 - 3(5^2) + 4(5) + 5 = 125 - 75 + 20 + 5 = 75.$", "# Answer\n\n75"], "pre_generated_answer": "75", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0004804866355077343}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I see that this problem involves the remainder theorem, which says that the remainder of dividing $P(x)$ by $(x - a)$ is equal to $P(a).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, for example, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 1),$ I get a remainder of $R(1),$ which is also equal to $P(1).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 4),$ I get a remainder of $R(4),$ which is also equal to $P(4).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "And if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 2),$ I get a remainder of $2R(2),$ which is also equal to $P(2).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Likewise, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 2),$ I get a remainder of $2R(2),$ which is also equal to $P(2).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, I notice that $(x - 1)(x - 4)$ and $(x - 2)(x - 3)$ have a common factor of $(x - 1).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ the remainder is not a constant, but a linear polynomial $R(x).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "And if I divide $P(x)$ by $(x - 2),$ I get a remainder of $2R(2),$ which is also equal to $P(2).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 129413, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "78d2023a-0bfc-486e-bd72-a1785e777ccb", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T04:01:08.088650", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Steve says to Jon, \"I am thinking of a polynomial whose roots are all positive integers. The polynomial has the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ for some positive integers $a$ and $c$. Can you tell me the values of $a$ and $c$?\"\n\nAfter some calculations, Jon says, \"There is more than one such polynomial.\"\n\nSteve says, \"You're right. Here is the value of $a$.\" He writes down a positive integer and asks, \"Can you tell me the value of $c$?\"\n\nJon says, \"There are still two possible values of $c$.\"\n\nFind the sum of the two possible values of $c$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $r, s, t$ be the three positive integer roots of $P(x).$ Then by Vieta's formulas, \\[\\begin{aligned} r+s+t &= a, \\\\ rs+st+rt &= \\frac{a^2-81}{2}, \\\\ rst &= \\frac{c}{2}. \\end{aligned}\\]Substituting the first equation into the second to eliminate $a,$ we have \\[rs+st+rt = \\frac{(r+s+t)^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{(r^2+s^2+t^2) + 2(rs+st+rt) - 81}{2}.\\]This simplifies to \\[r^2 + s^2 + t^2 = 81.\\]Therefore, each of $r, s, t$ lies in the set $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}.$ Assuming without loss of generality that $r \\le s \\le t,$ we have $81=r^2+s^2+t^2 \\le 3t^2,$ so $t^2 \\ge 27,$ and $t \\ge 6.$ We take cases:\n\nIf $t = 6,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81 - 6^2 = 45;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 6$ is $(r, s) = (3, 6).$\nIf $t = 7,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-7^2 = 32;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 7$ is $(r, s) = (4, 4).$\nIf $t = 8,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-8^2 = 17;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 8$ is $(r, s) = (1, 4).$\n\nTherefore, the possible sets of roots of such a polynomial are $(3, 6, 6), (4, 4, 7),$ and $(1, 4, 8).$ Calculating $a = r+s+t$ and $c=2rst$ for each set, we have $(a, c) = (15, 216), (15, 224), (13, 64).$\n\nSince, given the value of $a,$ there are still two possible values of $c,$ it must be that $a = 15,$ since two of the pairs $(a, c)$ have $a = 15,$ but only one has $a = 13.$ Then the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is \\[216 + 224 = \\boxed{440}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "440", "pre_generated_steps": ["I want to find a polynomial of the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ whose roots are all positive integers.", "One way to approach this is to use the rational root theorem, which says that if a polynomial with integer coefficients has a rational root, then it must be of the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient.", "In this case, the leading coefficient is $2$ and the constant term is $-c$, so any rational root must be of the form $\\frac{\\pm c}{2}$, where $c$ is a positive integer.", "But since the roots are all positive integers, I can simplify this and say that any root must be of the form $\\frac{c}{2}$, where $c$ is a positive even integer.", "Now, I can try to find some values of $c$ that work.", "For example, if $c = 2$, then the possible roots are $1$ and $2$.", "To see if these are indeed roots, I can plug them into the polynomial and see if they make it zero.", "If $x = 1$, then $P(1) = 2 - 2a + (a^2-81) - c = a^2 - 2a - 81 - c$.", "I want this to be zero, so I can solve for $a$ in terms of $c$: $a^2 - 2a - 81 - c = 0 \\implies a = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 4(81 + c)}}{2} = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 81 + c}$.", "Since $a$ has to be a positive integer, I can only choose the positive sign: $a = 1 + \\sqrt{82 + c}$.", "But this is only an integer if $82 + c$ is a perfect square.", "So I need to find some values of $c$ that make $82 + c$ a perfect square.", "For example, if $c = 2$, then $82 + c = 84 = 9^2$, so $a = 1 + 9 = 10$.", "This means that $P(x) = 2x^3 - 20x^2 + 19x - 2$ is a polynomial that satisfies the conditions, with roots $1$ and $2$.", "But is this the only one?", "No, because there might be other values of $c$ that make $82 + c$ a perfect square.", "For example, if $c = 18$, then $82 + c = 100 = 10^2$, so $a = 1 + 10 = 11$.", "This means that $P(x) = 2x^3 - 22x^2 + 41x - 18$ is another polynomial that satisfies the conditions, with roots $1$ and $2$.", "So I have found two different polynomials with the same roots, which means that Steve's question is not well-defined.", "He needs to tell me the value of $a$ before I can determine the value of $c$.", "Suppose he tells me that $a = 10$.", "Then I know that $c = 2$, because that is the only value of $c$ that makes $a = 1 + \\sqrt{82 + c}$.", "But suppose he tells me that $a = 11$.", "Then I have two possible values of $c$, either $c = 2$ or $c = 18$, because both of them make $a = 1 + \\sqrt{82 + c}$.", "In that case, the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is $2 + 18 = 20$.", "# Answer\n\n20"], "pre_generated_answer": "20", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.1412979748990736e-13}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I want to find a polynomial of the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ whose roots are all positive integers.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to approach this is to use the rational root theorem, which says that if a polynomial with integer coefficients has a rational root, then it must be of the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In this case, the leading coefficient is $2$ and the constant term is $-c$, so any rational root must be of the form $\\frac{\\pm c}{2}$, where $c$ is a positive integer.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But since the roots are all positive integers, I can simplify this and say that any root must be of the fo