a,$ there are still two possible values of $c,$ it must be that $a = 15,$ since two of the pairs $(a, c)$ have $a = 15,$ but only one has $a = 13.$ Then the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is \\[216 + 224 = \\boxed{440}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "440", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the roots of the polynomial, I need to factor it.", "If the roots are all positive integers, then the polynomial must have integer coefficients and be divisible by $x$.", "So I can write $P(x) = x(2x^2-2ax+a^2-81)-c$ and then use the quadratic formula to find the other two roots.", "The quadratic formula gives $x = \\frac{2a \\pm \\sqrt{4a^2-4(2)(a^2-81)}}{4} = \\frac{a \\pm \\sqrt{8a^2-648}}{2}$.", "For this to be a positive integer, I need $8a^2-648$ to be a perfect square and $a \\pm \\sqrt{8a^2-648}$ to be even.", "The smallest positive integer that satisfies both conditions is $a=9$, which gives $x = \\frac{9 \\pm \\sqrt{72}}{2} = 6$ or $1.5$.", "But $1.5$ is not an integer, so I can discard it.", "That means the roots of the polynomial are $x=1, 6$ and some other positive integer $r$.", "To find $r$, I can use the fact that the sum of the roots is equal to the negative coefficient of $x^2$, which is $2a$.", "So $1+6+r = 2a$.", "Plugging in $a=9$, I get $r = 2a-7 = 11$.", "So the polynomial is $P(x) = 2x^3-18x^2+18x-c$.", "To find $c$, I can plug in any of the roots and set the polynomial equal to zero.", "For example, if I plug in $x=1$, I get $P(1) = 2-18+18-c = 0$, so $c=2$.", "If I plug in $x=6$, I get $P(6) = 432-648+108-c = 0$, so $c=-108$.", "But $c$ is supposed to be positive, so I can discard this value.", "The only other possibility is $x=11$, which gives $P(11) = 2662-2178+198-c = 0$, so $c=682$.", "So the two possible values of $c$ are $2$ and $682$.", "# Answer\n\n684"], "pre_generated_answer": "684", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.9836798374301143e-13}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the roots of the polynomial, I need to factor it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If the roots are all positive integers, then the polynomial must have integer coefficients and be divisible by $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I can write $P(x) = x(2x^2-2ax+a^2-81)-c$ and then use the quadratic formula to find the other two roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The quadratic formula gives $x = \\frac{2a \\pm \\sqrt{4a^2-4(2)(a^2-81)}}{4} = \\frac{a \\pm \\sqrt{8a^2-648}}{2}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The discriminant of the quadratic equation is $(-2a)^2-4\\cdot2\\cdot(a^2-81) = -8a^2+656$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The discriminant of the quadratic is $(-2a)^2-4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (a^2-81) = -8a^2 + 656$ and it must be a perfect square for the roots to be rational.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For the quadratic formula to give me integer roots, the discriminant must be a perfect square. That means $(-2a)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (a^2-81)=4a^2 - 8a^2 + 324=324-4a^2$ must be a perfect square.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Alternatively, I can try to guess one of the roots and use synthetic division to find the other two.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 549862, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "423ec391-7def-48bd-821a-5e231333d6ce", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T03:54:44.495429", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Steve says to Jon, \"I am thinking of a polynomial whose roots are all positive integers. The polynomial has the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ for some positive integers $a$ and $c$. Can you tell me the values of $a$ and $c$?\"\n\nAfter some calculations, Jon says, \"There is more than one such polynomial.\"\n\nSteve says, \"You're right. Here is the value of $a$.\" He writes down a positive integer and asks, \"Can you tell me the value of $c$?\"\n\nJon says, \"There are still two possible values of $c$.\"\n\nFind the sum of the two possible values of $c$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $r, s, t$ be the three positive integer roots of $P(x).$ Then by Vieta's formulas, \\[\\begin{aligned} r+s+t &= a, \\\\ rs+st+rt &= \\frac{a^2-81}{2}, \\\\ rst &= \\frac{c}{2}. \\end{aligned}\\]Substituting the first equation into the second to eliminate $a,$ we have \\[rs+st+rt = \\frac{(r+s+t)^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{(r^2+s^2+t^2) + 2(rs+st+rt) - 81}{2}.\\]This simplifies to \\[r^2 + s^2 + t^2 = 81.\\]Therefore, each of $r, s, t$ lies in the set $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}.$ Assuming without loss of generality that $r \\le s \\le t,$ we have $81=r^2+s^2+t^2 \\le 3t^2,$ so $t^2 \\ge 27,$ and $t \\ge 6.$ We take cases:\n\nIf $t = 6,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81 - 6^2 = 45;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 6$ is $(r, s) = (3, 6).$\nIf $t = 7,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-7^2 = 32;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 7$ is $(r, s) = (4, 4).$\nIf $t = 8,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-8^2 = 17;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 8$ is $(r, s) = (1, 4).$\n\nTherefore, the possible sets of roots of such a polynomial are $(3, 6, 6), (4, 4, 7),$ and $(1, 4, 8).$ Calculating $a = r+s+t$ and $c=2rst$ for each set, we have $(a, c) = (15, 216), (15, 224), (13, 64).$\n\nSince, given the value of $a,$ there are still two possible values of $c,$ it must be that $a = 15,$ since two of the pairs $(a, c)$ have $a = 15,$ but only one has $a = 13.$ Then the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is \\[216 + 224 = \\boxed{440}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "440", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find a polynomial of degree 3 with positive integer roots, coefficients, and constant term.", "I notice that the coefficient of $x^3$ is 2, so if the roots are $r_1, r_2, r_3$, then I must have $2 = r_1r_2r_3$.", "The only way to write 2 as a product of three positive integers is $2 = 1 \\times 1 \\times 2$.", "This means that the polynomial has either one root of 2 and two roots of 1, or one root of 1 and two roots of 2.", "I can use the fact that the sum of the roots is equal to the opposite of the coefficient of $x^2$ divided by the coefficient of $x^3$.", "This gives me $r_1+r_2+r_3 = -(-2a)/2 = a$.", "If the roots are 1, 1, and 2, then $a = 1+1+2 = 4$.", "If the roots are 1, 2, and 2, then $a = 1+2+2 = 5$.", "So the possible values of $a$ are 4 and 5.", "I can use the fact that the product of the roots is equal to the constant term divided by the coefficient of $x^3$.", "This gives me $r_1r_2r_3 = c/2$.", "If the roots are 1, 1, and 2, then $c/2 = 1 \\times 1 \\times 2 = 2$, so $c = 4$.", "If the roots are 1, 2, and 2, then $c/2 = 1 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 4$, so $c = 8$.", "So the possible values of $c$ are 4 and 8.", "The sum of the two possible values of $c$ is 4 + 8 = 12.", "# Answer\n\n12"], "pre_generated_answer": "12", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.178419385084779e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find a polynomial of degree 3 with positive integer roots, coefficients, and constant term.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I know that roots are the values of $x$ that make $P(x)=0$. So I need to find some positive integers $x$ that satisfy the equation $2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c=0$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the polynomial has degree 3, so it should have 3 roots. Since the roots are all positive integers, they must divide the constant term $-c$, which is also a positive integer.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I need to find the values of $a$ and $c$ that make the polynomial $P(x)$ have all positive integer roots.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I need to find a polynomial $P(x)$ of the given form that has three positive integer roots.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 459432, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "fbc13b41-a7f0-4f72-b9b9-bb66101e39f3", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T03:53:52.163535", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Steve says to Jon, \"I am thinking of a polynomial whose roots are all positive integers. The polynomial has the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ for some positive integers $a$ and $c$. Can you tell me the values of $a$ and $c$?\"\n\nAfter some calculations, Jon says, \"There is more than one such polynomial.\"\n\nSteve says, \"You're right. Here is the value of $a$.\" He writes down a positive integer and asks, \"Can you tell me the value of $c$?\"\n\nJon says, \"There are still two possible values of $c$.\"\n\nFind the sum of the two possible values of $c$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $r, s, t$ be the three positive integer roots of $P(x).$ Then by Vieta's formulas, \\[\\begin{aligned} r+s+t &= a, \\\\ rs+st+rt &= \\frac{a^2-81}{2}, \\\\ rst &= \\frac{c}{2}. \\end{aligned}\\]Substituting the first equation into the second to eliminate $a,$ we have \\[rs+st+rt = \\frac{(r+s+t)^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{(r^2+s^2+t^2) + 2(rs+st+rt) - 81}{2}.\\]This simplifies to \\[r^2 + s^2 + t^2 = 81.\\]Therefore, each of $r, s, t$ lies in the set $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}.$ Assuming without loss of generality that $r \\le s \\le t,$ we have $81=r^2+s^2+t^2 \\le 3t^2,$ so $t^2 \\ge 27,$ and $t \\ge 6.$ We take cases:\n\nIf $t = 6,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81 - 6^2 = 45;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 6$ is $(r, s) = (3, 6).$\nIf $t = 7,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-7^2 = 32;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 7$ is $(r, s) = (4, 4).$\nIf $t = 8,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-8^2 = 17;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 8$ is $(r, s) = (1, 4).$\n\nTherefore, the possible sets of roots of such a polynomial are $(3, 6, 6), (4, 4, 7),$ and $(1, 4, 8).$ Calculating $a = r+s+t$ and $c=2rst$ for each set, we have $(a, c) = (15, 216), (15, 224), (13, 64).$\n\nSince, given the value of $a,$ there are still two possible values of $c,$ it must be that $a = 15,$ since two of the pairs $(a, c)$ have $a = 15,$ but only one has $a = 13.$ Then the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is \\[216 + 224 = \\boxed{440}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "440", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the roots of the polynomial, I need to factor it somehow.", "I notice that the coefficients of $x^3$ and $x$ are both even, so I can try to factor out a $2x$.", "I get $P(x) = 2x(x^2-ax+(a^2-81)/2)-c$.", "Now I need to find the values of $a$ and $c$ that make the quadratic factor have positive integer roots.", "I recall that a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ has integer roots if and only if its discriminant $b^2-4ac$ is a perfect square and $b$ and $c$ are divisible by $a$.", "Applying this to the quadratic factor, I see that I need $(a^2-81)/2$ to be a perfect square and $a$ to be divisible by $2$.", "So $a$ must be even, and $a^2-81$ must be a multiple of $4$.", "The simplest way to make $a^2-81$ a multiple of $4$ is to have $a^2-81=4$, which gives $a=\\pm 9$.", "But $a$ has to be positive, so $a=9$.", "This is the value that Steve wrote down.", "Now I know that $P(x) = 2x(x^2-9x+36)-c$, and I need to find the values of $c$ that make $x^2-9x+36$ have positive integer roots.", "Again, I use the discriminant condition.", "I need $(-9)^2-4(1)(36)$ to be a perfect square.", "This simplifies to $9-4(36)$, which is $-135$.", "Uh oh, this is a negative number, so it can't be a perfect square.", "That means there are no integer roots for this quadratic.", "I must have made a mistake somewhere.", "Let me go back and check my work.", "I see my error.", "I assumed that $a^2-81=4$ was the only way to make $a^2-81$ a multiple of $4$.", "But I forgot that $a^2-81$ could also be $0$, which gives $a=\\pm 9\\sqrt{2}$.", "But $a$ has to be an integer, so this doesn't work either.", "That means there are no values of $a$ and $c$ that make the polynomial have positive integer roots.", "Steve lied to Jon! There is no such polynomial, and the question is invalid.", "# Answer\n\nNo answer."], "pre_generated_answer": "No answer.", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.3651955746892756e-14}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the roots of the polynomial, I need to factor it somehow.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the coefficients of $x^3$ and $x$ are both even, so I can try to factor out a $2x$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I notice that the polynomial has a factor of $2x$ since $P(0) = 0$. So I can write $P(x) = 2x(x^2-ax+k)$ for some constant $k$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "A possible strategy is to use the rational root theorem, which says that any rational root of the polynomial $P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ must be of the form $\\frac{p}{q}$, where $p$ is a factor of $d$ and $q$ is a factor of $a$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the coefficient of $x^3$ is 2, so I can try to use the rational root theorem to look for possible rational roots of the form $\\pm \\frac{p}{2}$, where $p$ is a factor of $c$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the polynomial is of degree 3, so it should have at least one real root.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 323772, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T03:51:16.188161", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Steve says to Jon, \"I am thinking of a polynomial whose roots are all positive integers. The polynomial has the form $P(x) = 2x^3-2ax^2+(a^2-81)x-c$ for some positive integers $a$ and $c$. Can you tell me the values of $a$ and $c$?\"\n\nAfter some calculations, Jon says, \"There is more than one such polynomial.\"\n\nSteve says, \"You're right. Here is the value of $a$.\" He writes down a positive integer and asks, \"Can you tell me the value of $c$?\"\n\nJon says, \"There are still two possible values of $c$.\"\n\nFind the sum of the two possible values of $c$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $r, s, t$ be the three positive integer roots of $P(x).$ Then by Vieta's formulas, \\[\\begin{aligned} r+s+t &= a, \\\\ rs+st+rt &= \\frac{a^2-81}{2}, \\\\ rst &= \\frac{c}{2}. \\end{aligned}\\]Substituting the first equation into the second to eliminate $a,$ we have \\[rs+st+rt = \\frac{(r+s+t)^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{(r^2+s^2+t^2) + 2(rs+st+rt) - 81}{2}.\\]This simplifies to \\[r^2 + s^2 + t^2 = 81.\\]Therefore, each of $r, s, t$ lies in the set $\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\}.$ Assuming without loss of generality that $r \\le s \\le t,$ we have $81=r^2+s^2+t^2 \\le 3t^2,$ so $t^2 \\ge 27,$ and $t \\ge 6.$ We take cases:\n\nIf $t = 6,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81 - 6^2 = 45;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 6$ is $(r, s) = (3, 6).$\nIf $t = 7,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-7^2 = 32;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 7$ is $(r, s) = (4, 4).$\nIf $t = 8,$ then $r^2+s^2 = 81-8^2 = 17;$ the only solution where $r \\le s \\le 8$ is $(r, s) = (1, 4).$\n\nTherefore, the possible sets of roots of such a polynomial are $(3, 6, 6), (4, 4, 7),$ and $(1, 4, 8).$ Calculating $a = r+s+t$ and $c=2rst$ for each set, we have $(a, c) = (15, 216), (15, 224), (13, 64).$\n\nSince, given the value of $a,$ there are still two possible values of $c,$ it must be that $a = 15,$ since two of the pairs $(a, c)$ have $a = 15,$ but only one has $a = 13.$ Then the sum of the two possible values of $c$ is \\[216 + 224 = \\boxed{440}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "440", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the roots of a polynomial are the values of $x$ that make $P(x) = 0$.", "I also know that if $r$ is a root of $P(x)$, then $x-r$ is a factor of $P(x)$.", "Since Steve says the roots are all positive integers, I can try to factor $P(x)$ into the form $P(x) = 2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)(x-r_3)$, where $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ are the roots.", "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $P(x) = 2x^3-2(r_1+r_2+r_3)x^2+(r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3)x-2r_1r_2r_3$.", "Comparing the coefficients of $P(x)$, I see that $-2a = -2(r_1+r_2+r_3)$, so $a = r_1+r_2+r_3$.", "I also see that $a^2-81 = r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3$ and $-c = -2r_1r_2r_3$.", "Since $a$ is a positive integer, I know that $r_1+r_2+r_3$ is a positive integer.", "I also know that $r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3$ is a perfect square, since it is equal to $a^2-81$.", "Moreover, $r_1r_2r_3$ is a multiple of $2$, since it is equal to $-c/2$.", "I can try to find some possible values of $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ that satisfy these conditions.", "One possibility is $r_1 = 1$, $r_2 = 2$, and $r_3 = 9$.", "This gives $a = 1+2+9 = 12$ and $c = 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 9 = 36$.", "Another possibility is $r_1 = 1$, $r_2 = 3$, and $r_3 = 8$.", "This gives $a = 1+3+8 = 12$ and $c = 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot 8 = 48$.", "I notice that both possibilities have the same value of $a$, so Steve could have written down $12$ as the value of $a$.", "However, the values of $c$ are different, so Jon could not determine $c$ from $a$ alone.", "I wonder if there are any other possibilities for $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$.", "I observe that $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ cannot be too large, since their sum is equal to $a$ and their product is a multiple of $2$.", "I also observe that $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ cannot have a common factor, since that would imply $P(x)$ has a factor of the form $x-k$ for some integer $k > 1$.", "I try to find other triples of positive integers that satisfy these conditions and the equation $a^2-81 = r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3$.", "I find that there are no other such triples, so the only possible values of $c$ are $36$ and $48$.", "The sum of these two values is $36+48 = 84$.", "# Answer\n\n84"], "pre_generated_answer": "84", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 6.080573889595176e-06}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the roots of a polynomial are the values of $x$ that make $P(x) = 0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also know that if $r$ is a root of $P(x)$, then $x-r$ is a factor of $P(x)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since Steve says the roots are all positive integers, I can try to factor $P(x)$ into the form $P(x) = 2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)(x-r_3)$, where $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_3$ are the roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $P(x) = 2x^3-2(r_1+r_2+r_3)x^2+(r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3)x-2r_1r_2r_3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $P(x) = 2x^3-2(r_1+r_2+r_3)x^2+(r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3)x-2r_1r_2r_3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To find the roots, I need to compare the coefficients of $P(x)$ and $2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)(x-r_3)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Expanding this, I get $P(x) = 2x^3-2(r_1+r_2+r_3)x^2+(r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3)x-2r_1r_2r_3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $P(x) = 2x^3 - 2(r_1+r_2+r_3)x^2 + 2(r_1r_2 + r_1r_3 + r_2r_3)x - 2r_1r_2r_3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 