{"labeler": "2d45c8c4-b303-4c12-9fc7-12dcb4f1d028", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T00:24:47.471434", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $z$ be a complex number such that\n\\[z + \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]Find\n\\[z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "From $z + \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2},$\n\\[z + \\frac{1}{z} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]Squaring both sides, we end up with\n\\[z^2 - z + \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{1}{z} + \\frac{1}{z^2} = \\frac{5}{4}.\\]Then\n\\[z^2 - z + 1 - \\frac{1}{z} + \\frac{1}{z^2} = 0.\\]Hence, $z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1 = 0.$  Then\n\\[(z + 1)(z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1) = 0,\\]which expands as $z^5 + 1 = 0.$  This gives us $z^5 = -1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\\[z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}} = (z^5)^{17} + \\frac{1}{(z^5)^{17}} = (-1)^{17} + \\frac{1}{(-1)^{17}} = \\boxed{-2}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "-2", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression on the left-hand side of the given equation resembles the expansion of $(z + 1/z)^2$, so I try to see what happens if I square both sides.", "I get\n\\[\\left(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} + 2 = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\]\n\\[\\implies z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} = \\frac{2 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "This is interesting, because I see that the right-hand side is the same as before, and it also happens to be a famous irrational number called the golden ratio, usually denoted by $\\phi$.", "I wonder if this pattern continues if I keep squaring both sides.", "I square both sides again and get\n\\[\\left(z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^4 + \\frac{1}{z^4} + 2 = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\]\n\\[\\implies z^4 + \\frac{1}{z^4} = \\frac{2 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "I see that the pattern does continue, and I suspect that it will hold for any even power of $z$.", "In other words, I conjecture that\n\\[z^{2n} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n}} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\]\nfor any positive integer $n$.", "I try to prove my conjecture by induction.", "The base case of $n = 1$ is given by the original equation.", "Now, suppose the statement is true for some $n \\geq 1$.", "Then\n\\[z^{2n+2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n+2}} = \\left(z^{2n} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n}}\\right)\\left(z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2}\\right) - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{2n+2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n+2}} = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2 - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{2n+2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n+2}} = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{2n+2} + \\frac{1}{z^{2n+2}} = \\frac{2 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "This proves the induction step and completes the proof of the conjecture.", "Now, I can use the conjecture to answer the question.", "Since 85 is an odd number, I write it as 85 = 84 + 1.", "Then\n\\[z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}} = \\left(z^{84} + \\frac{1}{z^{84}}\\right)\\left(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\right) - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}} = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2 - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}} = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} - 2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^{85} + \\frac{1}{z^{85}} = \\frac{2 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.4551298169633464e-06}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression on the left-hand side of the given equation resembles the expansion of $(z + 1/z)^2$, so I try to see what happens if I square both sides.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get\n\\[\\left(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]\n\\[\\implies z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} + 2 = \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\]\n\\[\\implies z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} = \\frac{2 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{4} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get\n\\[(z + \\frac{1}{z})^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]\n[\\*] Expanding the left-hand side, I obtain\n\\[z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} + 2 = \\frac{1 + 2\\sqrt{5} + 5}{4}\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[\\left(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get \\[z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} + 2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[\\left(z + \\frac{1}{z}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2\\]\nwhich simplifies to\n\\[z^2 + \\frac{1}{z^2} + 2 = \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 274948, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "78d2023a-0bfc-486e-bd72-a1785e777ccb", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T00:18:56.438942", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ then $x$ satisfies a cubic polynomial of the form\n\\[ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0,\\]where $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d$ are integers, and $a \\neq 0.$  Find the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|.$", "ground_truth_solution": "From the equation $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x = \\pi - \\arccos 3x,$ so\n\\[\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = \\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x).\\]From the angle addition formula, the left-hand side becomes\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) &= \\cos (\\arccos x) \\cos (\\arccos 2x) - \\sin (\\arccos x) \\sin (\\arccos 2x) \\\\\n&= (x)(2x) - (\\sqrt{1 - x^2})(\\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}) \\\\\n&= 2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)}.\n\\end{align*}The right-hand side becomes\n\\[\\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x) = -\\cos (\\arccos 3x) = -3x,\\]so\n\\[2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = -3x.\\]Then $\\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = 2x^2 + 3x.$  Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2) = (2x^2 + 3x)^2.\\]This simplifies to $12x^3 + 14x^2 - 1 = 0.$  Thus, the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ is $12 + 14 + 0 + 1 = \\boxed{27}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "27", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find a way to simplify the expression $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x.$", "One idea is to use the fact that $\\arccos x$ is the angle $\\theta$ such that $\\cos \\theta = x.$", "Then I can use the cosine addition formula to write $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = \\cos \\arccos x \\cos \\arccos 2x - \\sin \\arccos x \\sin \\arccos 2x.$", "But I also know that $\\cos \\arccos x = x$ and $\\sin \\arccos x = \\sqrt{1 - x^2},$ so I can plug those in and get\n\\[\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}.\\]", "I also need to use the fact that $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ which means that $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos \\pi = -1.$", "So I can write\n\\[-1 = \\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) \\cos \\arccos 3x - \\sin(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) \\sin \\arccos 3x.\\]", "Now I can use the same trick as before and plug in the values of $\\cos \\arccos 3x = 3x$ and $\\sin \\arccos 3x = \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}.$", "I get\n\\[-1 = (x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}) \\cdot 3x - \\sqrt{1 - (x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2})^2} \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}.\\]", "This looks complicated, but I can simplify it by expanding and collecting like terms.", "After some algebra, I get\n\\[0 = 9x^3 - 7x + 1.\\]", "This is the cubic polynomial I was looking for, and it has the form $ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0,$ where $a = 9,$ $b = 0,$ $c = -7,$ and $d = 1.$", "The smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ is then $9 + 0 + 7 + 1 = 17.$", "# Answer\n\n17"], "pre_generated_answer": "17", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 8.020592545270836e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find a way to simplify the expression $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One idea is to use the fact that $\\arccos x$ is the angle $\\theta$ such that $\\cos \\theta = x.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I can use the cosine addition formula to write $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = \\cos \\arccos x \\cos \\arccos 2x - \\sin \\arccos x \\sin \\arccos 2x.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But I also know that $\\cos \\arccos x = x$ and $\\sin \\arccos x = \\sqrt{1 - x^2},$ so I can plug those in and get\n\\[\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also need to use the fact that $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ which means that $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos \\pi = -1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I can write\n\\[-1 = \\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) \\cos \\arccos 3x - \\sin(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) \\sin \\arccos 3x.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now I can use the same trick as before and plug in the values of $\\cos \\arccos 3x = 3x$ and $\\sin \\arccos 3x = \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get\n\\[-1 = (x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}) \\cdot 3x - \\sqrt{1 - (x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2})^2} \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}.\\]", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This looks complicated, but I can simplify it by expanding and collecting like terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "After some algebra, I get\n\\[0 = 9x^3 - 7x + 1.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get\n\\[0 = -24x^4 + 6x^3 + 19x^2 - x - 1.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[0 = 8x^4 - 4x^2 - 12x^3 + \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)(1 - 9x^2)}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[-1 = 6x^3 - 3x \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2) (1 - 4x^2) - 9x^2 (x \\cdot 2x - \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2})^2}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[-12x^3 + 9x^2 + 1 = (1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)(1 - 9x^2).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1863330, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "8ad3298c-1676-43c3-bd7b-dbf2f030cd43", "timestamp": "2022-11-14T00:06:25.120224", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ then $x$ satisfies a cubic polynomial of the form\n\\[ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0,\\]where $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d$ are integers, and $a \\neq 0.$  Find the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|.$", "ground_truth_solution": "From the equation $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x = \\pi - \\arccos 3x,$ so\n\\[\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = \\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x).\\]From the angle addition formula, the left-hand side becomes\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) &= \\cos (\\arccos x) \\cos (\\arccos 2x) - \\sin (\\arccos x) \\sin (\\arccos 2x) \\\\\n&= (x)(2x) - (\\sqrt{1 - x^2})(\\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}) \\\\\n&= 2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)}.\n\\end{align*}The right-hand side becomes\n\\[\\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x) = -\\cos (\\arccos 3x) = -3x,\\]so\n\\[2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = -3x.\\]Then $\\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = 2x^2 + 3x.$  Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2) = (2x^2 + 3x)^2.\\]This simplifies to $12x^3 + 14x^2 - 1 = 0.$  Thus, the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ is $12 + 14 + 0 + 1 = \\boxed{27}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "27", "pre_generated_steps": ["To solve this problem, I need to find a way to relate the inverse cosine functions to a polynomial.", "One idea is to use the identity $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ which means that I can replace $\\arcsos x$ with $\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x$ for any $x$ in the domain of both functions.", "So, I can rewrite the given equation as\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin 3x = \\pi.\\]", "Simplifying this, I get\n\\[\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x + \\arcsin 3x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\]", "Now, I need to somehow eliminate the inverse sine functions and get a polynomial.", "One way to do this is to use the sine addition formula, which states that $\\sin(A + B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B.$", "I can apply this formula to the sum of the first two terms, and then again to the sum of the result and the third term.", "This gives me\n\\[\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x) + \\sin(\\arcsin 3x) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\]", "\\[\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin 3x\\right).\\]", "Using the sine addition formula again, I get\n\\[(x\\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} + 2x\\sqrt{1 - x^2}) = \\cos(\\arcsin 3x).\\]", "Now, I can use the identity $\\cos(\\arcsin x) = \\sqrt{1 - x^2}$ to replace the right-hand side.", "This gives me\n\\[(x\\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} + 2x\\sqrt{1 - x^2}) = \\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}.\\]", "Squaring both sides, I get\n\\[(x^2(1 - 4x^2) + 4x^2(1 - x^2) + 4x^2(1 - 4x^2)(1 - x^2)) = (1 - 9x^2).\\]", "Expanding and simplifying, I get\n\\[33x^6 - 22x^4 - 2x^2 + 1 = 0.\\]", "This is the cubic polynomial that $x$ satisfies, and it has the form\n\\[ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0,\\]\nwhere $a = 33,$ $b = -22,$ $c = -2,$ and $d = 1.$", "Therefore, the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ is\n\\[|33| + |-22| + |-2| + |1| = 58.\\]", "# Answer\n\n58"], "pre_generated_answer": "58", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 6.285858472444619e-09}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To solve this problem, I need to find a way to relate the inverse cosine functions to a polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One idea is to use the identity $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ which means that I can replace $\\arcsos x$ with $\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x$ for any $x$ in the domain of both functions.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, I can rewrite the given equation as\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin 3x = \\pi.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying this, I get\n\\[\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x + \\arcsin 3x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, I need to somehow eliminate the inverse sine functions and get a polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to do this is to use the sine addition formula, which states that $\\sin(A + B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can apply this formula to the sum of the first two terms, and then again to the sum of the result and the third term.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "If I apply this formula to $\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x,$ I get\n\\[\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x) = \\sin \\arcsin x \\cos \\arcsin 2x + \\cos \\arcsin x \\sin \\arcsin 2x.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I apply this formula to $\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x),$ I get\n\\[\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x) = \\sin(\\arcsin x) \\cos(\\arcsin 2x) + \\cos(\\arcsin x) \\sin(\\arcsin 2x).\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I can apply this formula to $\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x$ and write it as $\\arcsin(\\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x)).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I let $A = \\arcsin x$ and $B = \\arcsin 2x,$ then I can apply the formula to get\n\\[\\sin(A + B) = \\sin(\\arcsin x + \\arcsin 2x) = x \\sqrt{1 - 4x^2} + 2x \\sqrt{1 - x^2}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 520125, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "d8aa7923-b970-45e1-9734-e4a7f6c4a7db", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T23:59:11.699807", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ then $x$ satisfies a cubic polynomial of the form\n\\[ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0,\\]where $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d$ are integers, and $a \\neq 0.$  Find the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|.$", "ground_truth_solution": "From the equation $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x = \\pi - \\arccos 3x,$ so\n\\[\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) = \\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x).\\]From the angle addition formula, the left-hand side becomes\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos (\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x) &= \\cos (\\arccos x) \\cos (\\arccos 2x) - \\sin (\\arccos x) \\sin (\\arccos 2x) \\\\\n&= (x)(2x) - (\\sqrt{1 - x^2})(\\sqrt{1 - 4x^2}) \\\\\n&= 2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)}.\n\\end{align*}The right-hand side becomes\n\\[\\cos (\\pi - \\arccos 3x) = -\\cos (\\arccos 3x) = -3x,\\]so\n\\[2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = -3x.\\]Then $\\sqrt{(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2)} = 2x^2 + 3x.$  Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[(1 - x^2)(1 - 4x^2) = (2x^2 + 3x)^2.\\]This simplifies to $12x^3 + 14x^2 - 1 = 0.$  Thus, the smallest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ is $12 + 14 + 0 + 1 = \\boxed{27}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "27", "pre_generated_steps": ["I recall that $\\arccos x$ is the angle $\\theta$ in the interval $[0,\\pi]$ such that $\\cos \\theta = x.$", "Therefore, if $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ then $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos \\pi = -1.$", "I can use the identity $\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\cos \\gamma$ to expand the left-hand side of the equation.", "Plugging in $\\alpha = \\arccos x,$ $\\beta = \\arccos 2x,$ and $\\gamma = \\arccos 3x,$ I get\n\\begin{align*}\n-1 &= x \\cdot 2x \\cdot 3x - x \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} \\\\ &- 2x \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - 3x \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2}.\n\\end{align*}", "I can simplify this equation by multiplying both sides by $4$ and rearranging the terms.", "I get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= 24x^3 + 4 - 4x(1 - 9x^2) - 8x(1 - 4x^2) - 12x(1 - x^2) \\\\\n&= 24x^3 - 4x(1 + 9x^2 + 4x^2 + x^2) + 4 \\\\\n&= 24x^3 - 56x^3 - 4x + 4 \\\\\n&= -32x^3 - 4x + 4.\n\\end{align*}", "So the cubic polynomial that $x$ satisfies is $-32x^3 - 4x + 4 = 0.$", "The value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ for this polynomial is $|-32| + |0| + |-4| + |4| = 40.$", "I wonder if this is the smallest possible value, or if there is another polynomial with smaller coefficients that is equivalent to this one.", "I notice that I can divide the polynomial by $-4$ to get $8x^3 + x - 1 = 0.$", "The value of $|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|$ for this polynomial is $|8| + |0| + |1| + |-1| = 10.$", "I think this is the smallest possible value, because if I divide by any other factor, I will either get a non-integer coefficient, or increase the absolute value of one of the coefficients.", "Therefore, the answer is $10.$", "# Answer\n\n10"], "pre_generated_answer": "10", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.007212321147274718}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I recall that $\\arccos x$ is the angle $\\theta$ in the interval $[0,\\pi]$ such that $\\cos \\theta = x.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, if $\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi,$ then $\\cos(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x) = \\cos \\pi = -1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can use the identity $\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\cos \\gamma - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta \\cos \\gamma$ to expand the left-hand side of the equation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in $\\alpha = \\arccos x,$ $\\beta = \\arccos 2x,$ and $\\gamma = \\arccos 3x,$ I get\n\\begin{align*}\n-1 &= x \\cdot 2x \\cdot 3x - x \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} \\\\ &- 2x \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} - 3x \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2}.\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can simplify this equation by multiplying both sides by $4$ and rearranging the terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= 24x^3 + 4 - 4x(1 - 9x^2) - 8x(1 - 4x^2) - 12x(1 - x^2) \\\\\n&= 24x^3 - 4x(1 + 9x^2 + 4x^2 + x^2) + 4 \\\\\n&= 24x^3 - 56x^3 - 4x + 4 \\\\\n&= -32x^3 - 4x + 4.\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= 24x^3 + 4 - (4x^2 - 12x - 1) (1 - 9x^2) - (4x^3 - 12x - 2) (1 - 4x^2).\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Doing so, I get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= 24x^3 + 4 \\\\ &- 4x^3 \\left[1 - (2x)^2\\right] \\left[1 - (3x)^2\\right] \\\\ &- 8x^2 \\left[1 - x^2\\right] \\left[1 - (3x)^2\\right]\\\\ &- 12x^2 \\left[1 - x^2\\right] \\left[1 - (2x)^2\\right].\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\begin{align*}\n0 &= (24x^3 + 4)x - (12x - 4) \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2}.\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This gives me\n\\begin{align*}\n12x^3 - 4 &= 4x \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} \\\\ &+ 8x^2 \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3x)^2} + 12x^3 \\sqrt{1 - x^2} \\sqrt{1 - (2x)^2}.\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 613893, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "eceb40b3-f8f5-47bc-8044-ecdc5c05d2fa", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T17:41:46.202192", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Four points, $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, are chosen randomly and independently on the circumference of a circle. What is the probability that segments $AB$ and $CD$ intersect?", "ground_truth_solution": "Consider the four random points before they are labeled $A$, $B$, $C$, or $D$. In the general case, they will be distinct, forming a convex quadrilateral. Suppose $A$ is labeled. If $B$ is labeled as the vertex opposite $A$, segments $AB$ and $CD$ will intersect; otherwise, they will not. Since there are 3 points to label as $B$, the probability these segments intersect is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}$. [asy]\ndraw((0,1)..(1,0)..(0,-1)..(-1,0)..cycle);\ndot((0,1)); dot((-5/13,-12/13)); dot((-1,0)); dot((4/5,3/5));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(0,1),N); label(\"$B$\",(-5/13,-12/13),SSW); label(\"$C$\",(-1,0),W); label(\"$D$\",(4/5,3/5),NE);\ndraw((0,1)--(-5/13,-12/13),green); draw((-1,0)--(4/5,3/5),green);\ndraw((0,1)--(4/5,3/5),blue); draw((-1,0)--(-5/13,-12/13),blue);\ndraw((0,1)--(-1,0),red); draw((-5/13,-12/13)--(4/5,3/5),red);\n[/asy] In this diagram, the green edges represent the labeling where $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, and the blue and red edges represent the equally likely labelings where $AB$ and $CD$ do not intersect.", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{1}{3}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a tricky geometry problem, but there is a nice way to approach it using symmetry and angle arguments.", "First, I observe that if I fix $A$ and $B$, then the probability that $C$ and $D$ are on opposite sides of $AB$ is $1/2$, since there is a half-circle of points for each choice.", "If $C$ and $D$ are on opposite sides of $AB$, then $AB$ and $CD$ must intersect, by the properties of inscribed angles.", "So, the probability that $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, given $A$ and $B$, is also $1/2$.", "But, since $A$ and $B$ were arbitrary, this probability does not depend on their locations, and so it is the same for any four points on the circle.", "Therefore, the answer is $1/2$.", "# Answer\n\n1/2"], "pre_generated_answer": "1/2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.03253627915961717}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a tricky geometry problem, but there is a nice way to approach it using symmetry and angle arguments.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "First, I observe that if I fix $A$ and $B$, then the probability that $C$ and $D$ are on opposite sides of $AB$ is $1/2$, since there is a half-circle of points for each choice.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Let's imagine the circle is centered at the origin and has radius 1, so it is the unit circle. Then any point on the circle can be represented by an angle $\\theta$ such that $0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let's fix one point, say $A$, on the circle and consider how the other three points can be chosen. We can draw a diameter through $A$, and label the opposite point $O$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let's draw a circle and label four points on it: $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$. We want to find the probability that $AB$ and $CD$ intersect, so let's draw those segments as well.\n\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]\n\\draw (0,0) circle (1cm);\n\\node[label=90:$A$] (A) at (0,1) {};\n\\node[fill=black,circle,inner sep=1pt] at (A) {};\n\\node[label=63:$B$] (B) at (0.8,0.6) {};\n\\node[fill=black,circle,inner sep=1pt] at (B) {};\n\\node[label=270:$C$] (C) at (0.3,-0.95) {};\n\\node[fill=black,circle,inner sep=1pt] at (C) {};\n\\node[label=198:$D$] (D) at (-0.6,-0.8) {};\n\\node[fill=black,circle,inner sep=1pt] at (D) {};\n\\draw (A)--(B);\n\\draw (C)--(", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I can imagine the four points on the circle as the endpoints of two chords, $AB$ and $CD$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 636079, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "406137be-0120-4487-a50b-c5652df1eebf", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T17:30:34.013302", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $re^{i \\theta}$ is a root of\n\\[z^8 - z^7 + z^6 - z^5 + z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1 = 0,\\]where $r > 0$ and $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ then find the sum of all possible values of $\\theta.$", "ground_truth_solution": "The given equation can be written as\n\\[\\frac{z^9 + 1}{z + 1} = 0.\\]Then $z^9 + 1 = 0,$ or $z^9 = -1.$  Since $z = e^{i \\theta},$\n\\[e^{9i \\theta} = -1.\\]This means $9 \\theta = \\pi + 2 \\pi k$ for some integer $k.$  Since $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ the possible values of $k$ are 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, and 8.   (We omit $k = 4,$ because if $k = 4,$ then $\\theta = \\pi,$ so $z = -1,$ which makes $z + 1 = 0.$)  Therefore, the sum of all possible values of $\\theta$ is\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{9} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{5 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{7 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{11 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{13 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{15 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{17 \\pi}{9} = \\boxed{8 \\pi}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "8 \\pi", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a complex polynomial equation of degree 8, so it has 8 roots in the complex plane.", "One way to approach this problem is to use the fact that the coefficients are symmetric, which means that the polynomial is palindromic.", "This implies that if $z$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{z}.$", "Therefore, if $re^{i \\theta}$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{re^{i \\theta}} = \\frac{e^{-i \\theta}}{r}.$", "This means that the roots come in pairs of the form $re^{i \\theta}$ and $\\frac{e^{-i \\theta}}{r},$ and the product of each pair is 1.", "Furthermore, the sum of the arguments of each pair is $\\theta - \\theta = 0.$", "This means that the sum of all the arguments of the roots is zero, because they cancel out in pairs.", "Therefore, the sum of all possible values of $\\theta$ is zero as well.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0098565771035681}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a complex polynomial equation of degree 8, so it has 8 roots in the complex plane.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to approach this problem is to use the fact that the coefficients are symmetric, which means that the polynomial is palindromic.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This implies that if $z$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{z}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, if $re^{i \\theta}$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{re^{i \\theta}} = \\frac{e^{-i \\theta}}{r}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the roots come in pairs of the form $re^{i \\theta}$ and $\\frac{e^{-i \\theta}}{r},$ and the product of each pair is 1.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Furthermore, the sum of the arguments of each pair is $\\theta - \\theta = 0.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Now, let's think about the modulus and the argument of each root.", "rating": null, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, I can use the fact that the sum of the roots of a polynomial is equal to the negative coefficient of the term of degree one less than the degree, divided by the leading coefficient.", "rating": null, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, let's think about the modulus and argument of these roots. Since the product of each pair is 1, the moduli must be reciprocals: $r \\cdot \\frac{1}{r} = 1.$", "rating": null, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, let's look at the argument of each root. The argument of $re^{i \\theta}$ is $\\theta,$ and the argument of $\\frac{e^{-i \\theta}}{r}$ is $-\\theta.$", "rating": null, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 2629333, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "9ef92825-c3e7-4666-8db8-1ebc83fcfdfd", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T16:45:10.937738", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $re^{i \\theta}$ is a root of\n\\[z^8 - z^7 + z^6 - z^5 + z^4 - z^3 + z^2 - z + 1 = 0,\\]where $r > 0$ and $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ then find the sum of all possible values of $\\theta.$", "ground_truth_solution": "The given equation can be written as\n\\[\\frac{z^9 + 1}{z + 1} = 0.\\]Then $z^9 + 1 = 0,$ or $z^9 = -1.$  Since $z = e^{i \\theta},$\n\\[e^{9i \\theta} = -1.\\]This means $9 \\theta = \\pi + 2 \\pi k$ for some integer $k.$  Since $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ the possible values of $k$ are 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, and 8.   (We omit $k = 4,$ because if $k = 4,$ then $\\theta = \\pi,$ so $z = -1,$ which makes $z + 1 = 0.$)  Therefore, the sum of all possible values of $\\theta$ is\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{9} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{5 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{7 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{11 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{13 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{15 \\pi}{9} + \\frac{17 \\pi}{9} = \\boxed{8 \\pi}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "8 \\pi", "pre_generated_steps": ["I recognize this equation as a special case of the cyclotomic equation, which has roots of the form\n\\[e^{i \\frac{2 \\pi k}{n}},\\]\nwhere $n$ is the degree of the equation and $k$ is any integer that is relatively prime to $n.$", "In this case, $n = 8,$ so the roots are\n\\[e^{i \\frac{2 \\pi k}{8}} = e^{i \\frac{\\pi k}{4}},\\]\nwhere $k = 1, 3, 5,$ or $7.$", "If $re^{i \\theta}$ is one of these roots, then $r = 1$ and $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi k}{4},$ where $k$ is an odd integer between $1$ and $7.$", "Therefore, the possible values of $\\theta$ are\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}, \\frac{5 \\pi}{4}, \\text{ and } \\frac{7 \\pi}{4}.\\]", "The sum of these values is\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4} + \\frac{5 \\pi}{4} + \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} = \\frac{16 \\pi}{4} = 4 \\pi.\\]", "# Answer\n\n4 \\pi"], "pre_generated_answer": "4 \\pi", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.003860856426424582}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I recognize this equation as a special case of the cyclotomic equation, which has roots of the form\n\\[e^{i \\frac{2 \\pi k}{n}},\\]\nwhere $n$ is the degree of the equation and $k$ is any integer that is relatively prime to $n.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The first thing I notice is that the equation has a lot of symmetry: the coefficients are either 1 or -1, and they alternate signs.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the polynomial is symmetric about the $z^4$ term, meaning that if $z$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{z}.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the coefficient of each term in the polynomial is either 1 or -1, and the constant term is 1. That suggests that the sum of the roots must be -1, by Vieta's formulas.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem involves complex numbers and roots of unity, so I will try to use some basic facts and properties of these concepts to find a solution.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1024689, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "78d2023a-0bfc-486e-bd72-a1785e777ccb", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:49:37.343262", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the greatest integer less than $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6.$  (Do not use a calculator!)", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFirst, we can square $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5},$ to get\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 + 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}, \\\\\ny^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 - 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Note that $x^2$ and $y^2$ are radical conjugates.  Also, $x^2 y^2 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}) = 12^2 - 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 4,$ so\n\\[y^2 = \\frac{4}{x^2} = \\frac{4}{12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}} < 1.\\]Then\n\\[x^4 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})^2 = 12^2 + 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{35} + 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 284 + 48 \\sqrt{35},\\]and\n\\begin{align*}\nx^6 &= x^2 \\cdot x^4 \\\\\n&= (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(284 + 48 \\sqrt{35}) \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 284 + 12 \\cdot 48 \\sqrt{35} + 2 \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 284 + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 48 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\\\\n&= 6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Then $y^6$ is the radical conjugate of $x^6,$ so $y^6 = 6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}.$  Hence,\n\\[x^6 + y^6 = (6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}) + (6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}) = 13536.\\]Since $0 < y^6 < 1,$ the greatest integer less than $x^6$ is $\\boxed{13535}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "13535", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ is the sixth power of a binomial, so I can use the binomial theorem to expand it.", "The binomial theorem states that $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where $\\binom{n}{k}$ is the binomial coefficient that counts the number of ways to choose $k$ items from $n$.", "Applying the binomial theorem to $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$, I get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 &= \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k \\\\\n&= \\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + \\binom{6}{1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5}) + \\binom{6}{2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + \\cdots + \\binom{6}{6} (\\sqrt{5})^6 \\\\\n&= 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6 \\cdot 5^{5/2} \\sqrt{7} + 5^3 \\\\\n&= 343 + 210 \\sqrt{35} + 1475 + 700 \\sqrt{35} + 1750 + 150 \\sqrt{35} + 125 \\\\\n&= 3693 + 1060 \\sqrt{35}\n\\end{align*}", "So the exact value of $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ is $3693 + 1060 \\sqrt{35}$, but the question asks for the greatest integer less than this value.", "To find this integer, I need to estimate the value of $\\sqrt{35}$.", "I know that $35$ is between $36$ and $25$, which are perfect squares, so $\\sqrt{35}$ must be between $6$ and $5$.", "To get a better estimate, I can use the fact that $\\sqrt{35} = \\sqrt{36 - 1} = 6 - \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{36 - 1}} \\approx 6 - \\frac{1}{12}$, since $\\sqrt{36 - 1}$ is close to $6$.", "So $\\sqrt{35} \\approx 6 - \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{71}{12}$, which means that $1060 \\sqrt{35} \\approx 1060 \\cdot \\frac{71}{12} = \\frac{75260}{12} = 6271.666\\ldots$", "Adding this to $3693$, I get $3693 + 1060 \\sqrt{35} \\approx 3693 + 6271.666\\ldots = 9964.666\\ldots$", "The greatest integer less than this value is $9964$.", "# Answer\n\n9964"], "pre_generated_answer": "9964", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.005670797133544062}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ is the sixth power of a binomial, so I can use the binomial theorem to expand it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The binomial theorem states that $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where $\\binom{n}{k}$ is the binomial coefficient that counts the number of ways to choose $k$ items from $n$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Applying the binomial theorem to $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$, I get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 &= \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k \\\\\n&= \\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + \\binom{6}{1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5}) + \\binom{6}{2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + \\cdots + \\binom{6}{6} (\\sqrt{5})^6 \\\\\n&= 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6 \\cdot 5^{5/2} \\sqrt{7} + 5^3 \\\\\n&= 343 + 210 \\sqrt{35} + 1475 + 700 \\sqrt{35} + 1750 + 150 \\sqrt{35} + 125 \\\\\n&= 3693 + 1060 \\sqrt{35}\n\\end{align*}", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "In this case, $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$, so $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "In this case, $a = \\sqrt{7}$, $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and $n = 6$, so the expansion is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 &= \\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 (\\sqrt{5})^0 + \\binom{6}{1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5})^1 + \\binom{6}{2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 \\\\ &+ \\binom{6}{3} (\\sqrt{7})^3 (\\sqrt{5})^3 + \\binom{6}{4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + \\binom{6}{5} (\\sqrt{7})^1 (\\sqrt{5})^5 + \\binom{6}{6} (\\sqrt{7})^0 (\\sqrt{5})^6\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Plugging in $a = \\sqrt{7}, b = \\sqrt{5},$ and $n = 6$, I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} 7^{3-k} 5^k$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, in this case, I have $a = \\sqrt{7}$, $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and $n = 6$. Plugging these into the formula, I get\n\\begin{align*}\n(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 &= \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k \\\\\n&= \\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + \\binom{6}{1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5}) + \\binom{6}{2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + \\cdots \\\\\n&\\quad+ \\binom{6}{4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + \\binom{6}{5} (\\sqrt{7}) (\\sqrt{5})^5 + \\binom{6}{6} (\\sqrt{5})^6\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 678253, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "7ca54fa1-32a2-43c7-a908-de90c1605568", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:49:33.210733", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the greatest integer less than $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6.$  (Do not use a calculator!)", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFirst, we can square $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5},$ to get\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 + 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}, \\\\\ny^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 - 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Note that $x^2$ and $y^2$ are radical conjugates.  Also, $x^2 y^2 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}) = 12^2 - 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 4,$ so\n\\[y^2 = \\frac{4}{x^2} = \\frac{4}{12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}} < 1.\\]Then\n\\[x^4 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})^2 = 12^2 + 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{35} + 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 284 + 48 \\sqrt{35},\\]and\n\\begin{align*}\nx^6 &= x^2 \\cdot x^4 \\\\\n&= (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(284 + 48 \\sqrt{35}) \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 284 + 12 \\cdot 48 \\sqrt{35} + 2 \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 284 + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 48 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\\\\n&= 6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Then $y^6$ is the radical conjugate of $x^6,$ so $y^6 = 6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}.$  Hence,\n\\[x^6 + y^6 = (6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}) + (6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}) = 13536.\\]Since $0 < y^6 < 1,$ the greatest integer less than $x^6$ is $\\boxed{13535}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "13535", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks intimidating, but there might be a way to simplify it using the binomial theorem.", "The binomial theorem tells us that $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n {n \\choose k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where ${n \\choose k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$ is the binomial coefficient.", "Applying this to $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$, we get\n\n$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k$", "Now, the trick is to notice that the terms in this sum are either integers or irrational numbers, depending on the value of $k$.", "If $k$ is even, then both $(\\sqrt{7})^{6-k}$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^k$ are integers, since they are perfect squares.", "For example, when $k=0$, we have $(\\sqrt{7})^6 = 7^3 = 343$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^0 = 1$.", "If $k$ is odd, then one of $(\\sqrt{7})^{6-k}$ or $(\\sqrt{5})^k$ is irrational, since it involves a square root of a non-perfect square.", "For example, when $k=1$, we have $(\\sqrt{7})^5 = 7^{5/2}$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^1 = \\sqrt{5}$, and $7^{5/2}$ is irrational.", "This means that the sum of the terms with even $k$ is an integer, and the sum of the terms with odd $k$ is irrational.", "For example, when $n=6$, we have\n\n$\\sum_{k=0,2,4,6} {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k = {6 \\choose 0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + {6 \\choose 2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + {6 \\choose 4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + {6 \\choose 6} (\\sqrt{5})^6$\n\n$= 343 + 15 \\cdot 49 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 + 15625$\n\n$= 47818$\n\n$\\sum_{k=1,3,5} {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k = {6 \\choose 1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5}) + {6 \\choose 3} (\\sqrt{7})^3 (\\sqrt{5})^3 + {6 \\choose 5} (\\sqrt{7}) (\\sqrt{5})^5$\n\n$= 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\sqrt{5} + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\sqrt{5}^3 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\sqrt{5}^5$\n\n$= \\sqrt{5} (6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^2)$\n\n$= \\sqrt{5} (417.920216 \\dots)$\n\n$= 186.547698 \\dots$", "Therefore, $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 47818 + 186.547698 \\dots = 48004.547698 \\dots$", "The greatest integer less than this is simply $48004$.", "# Answer\n\n48004"], "pre_generated_answer": "48004", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.006580393531600722}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks intimidating, but there might be a way to simplify it using the binomial theorem.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The binomial theorem tells us that $(a+b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n {n \\choose k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where ${n \\choose k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$ is the binomial coefficient.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Applying this to $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$, we get\n\n$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, the trick is to notice that the terms in this sum are either integers or irrational numbers, depending on the value of $k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k$ is even, then both $(\\sqrt{7})^{6-k}$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^k$ are integers, since they are perfect squares.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, when $k=0$, we have $(\\sqrt{7})^6 = 7^3 = 343$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^0 = 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k$ is odd, then one of $(\\sqrt{7})^{6-k}$ or $(\\sqrt{5})^k$ is irrational, since it involves a square root of a non-perfect square.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, when $k=1$, we have $(\\sqrt{7})^5 = 7^{5/2}$ and $(\\sqrt{5})^1 = \\sqrt{5}$, and $7^{5/2}$ is irrational.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the sum of the terms with even $k$ is an integer, and the sum of the terms with odd $k$ is irrational.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, when $n=6$, we have\n\n$\\sum_{k=0,2,4,6} {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k = {6 \\choose 0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + {6 \\choose 2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + {6 \\choose 4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + {6 \\choose 6} (\\sqrt{5})^6$\n\n$= 343 + 15 \\cdot 49 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 + 15625$\n\n$= 47818$\n\n$\\sum_{k=1,3,5} {6 \\choose k} (\\sqrt{7})^{6-k} (\\sqrt{5})^k = {6 \\choose 1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5}) + {6 \\choose 3} (\\sqrt{7})^3 (\\sqrt{5})^3 + {6 \\choose 5} (\\sqrt{7}) (\\sqrt{5})^5$\n\n$= 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\sqrt{5} + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\sqrt{5}^3 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\sqrt{5}^5$\n\n$= \\sqrt{5} (6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^2)$\n\n$= \\sqrt{5} (417.920216 \\dots)$\n\n$= 186.547698 \\dots$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Therefore, $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ is an irrational number slightly larger than the integer part of the sum of the even terms.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let's call these sums $E$ and $I$, respectively. Then we have\n\n$(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = E + I$, where $E$ is an integer and $I$ is irrational.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Therefore, the whole sum is irrational, and the greatest integer less than it is the integer part of the sum of the terms with even $k$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let's call these sums $E$ and $I$, respectively. Then,\n\n$E = {6 \\choose 0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 (\\sqrt{5})^0 + {6 \\choose 2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + {6 \\choose 4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + {6 \\choose 6} (\\sqrt{7})^0 (\\sqrt{5})^6$\n\n$E = 343 + 15 \\cdot 49 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 + 25^3$\n\n$E = 343 + 3675 + 2625 + 15625$\n\n$E = 23268$\n\n$~$\n\n$I = {6 \\choose 1} (\\sqrt{7})^5 (\\sqrt{5})^1 + {6 \\choose 3} (\\sqrt{7})^3 (\\sqrt{5})^3 + {6 \\choose 5} (\\sqrt{7})^1 (\\sqrt{5})^5$\n\n$I = 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 756297, "