{"labeler": "88155943-d0dc-4c73-8e21-0f35c0cf8f0a", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:48:23.903057", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the greatest integer less than $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6.$  (Do not use a calculator!)", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFirst, we can square $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5},$ to get\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 + 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}, \\\\\ny^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 - 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Note that $x^2$ and $y^2$ are radical conjugates.  Also, $x^2 y^2 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}) = 12^2 - 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 4,$ so\n\\[y^2 = \\frac{4}{x^2} = \\frac{4}{12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}} < 1.\\]Then\n\\[x^4 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})^2 = 12^2 + 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{35} + 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 284 + 48 \\sqrt{35},\\]and\n\\begin{align*}\nx^6 &= x^2 \\cdot x^4 \\\\\n&= (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(284 + 48 \\sqrt{35}) \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 284 + 12 \\cdot 48 \\sqrt{35} + 2 \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 284 + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 48 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\\\\n&= 6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Then $y^6$ is the radical conjugate of $x^6,$ so $y^6 = 6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}.$  Hence,\n\\[x^6 + y^6 = (6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}) + (6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}) = 13536.\\]Since $0 < y^6 < 1,$ the greatest integer less than $x^6$ is $\\boxed{13535}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "13535", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks hard, but maybe there is a way to simplify $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ without expanding it fully.", "I recall that there is a binomial formula for $(a+b)^6$, which is $(a+b)^6 = a^6 + 6a^5b + 15a^4b^2 + 20a^3b^3 + 15a^2b^4 + 6ab^5 + b^6$.", "Maybe I can use this formula with $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$.", "Then I would get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^2 \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} + 15 \\cdot 5 \\sqrt{7} + 6 \\cdot 5^2 + 5^3$.", "This still looks complicated, but maybe I can estimate the value of this expression by rounding some of the terms.", "I know that $\\sqrt{5}$ is a bit more than 2, so $6 \\cdot 7^2 \\sqrt{5}$ is a bit more than $6 \\cdot 49 \\cdot 2 = 588$.", "I also know that $\\sqrt{7}$ is a bit less than 3, so $15 \\cdot 5 \\sqrt{7}$ is a bit less than $15 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 = 225$.", "And I know that $\\sqrt{35}$ is a bit less than 6, so $20 \\cdot \\sqrt{35}$ is a bit less than $20 \\cdot 6 = 120$.", "So I can rewrite the expression as $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\approx 7^3 + 588 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5 + 120 + 225 + 6 \\cdot 5^2 + 5^3$.", "Now I can simplify this by adding the constants and multiplying the powers.", "I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\approx 343 + 588 + 525 + 120 + 225 + 150 + 125$.", "Adding these up, I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 \\approx 2076$.", "This is an approximation, so the actual value might be slightly more or less than this.", "But I want the greatest integer less than this value, so I need to round down to the nearest whole number.", "The answer is 2075.", "# Answer\n\n2075"], "pre_generated_answer": "2075", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 9.374009082277876e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks hard, but maybe there is a way to simplify $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ without expanding it fully.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that there is a binomial formula for $(a+b)^6$, which is $(a+b)^6 = a^6 + 6a^5b + 15a^4b^2 + 20a^3b^3 + 15a^2b^4 + 6ab^5 + b^6$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Maybe I can use this formula with $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I would get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^2 \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} + 15 \\cdot 5 \\sqrt{7} + 6 \\cdot 5^2 + 5^3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Then I would have $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6\\cdot 7^{\\frac{5}{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} + 15\\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 20\\cdot 7^{\\frac{3}{2}} \\cdot 5^{\\frac{3}{2}} + 15\\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6\\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^{\\frac{5}{2}} + 5^3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I plug these values into the formula, I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5^{3/2} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^{5/2} + 5^3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I do that, I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6(7^{5/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}) + 15(7^2 \\cdot 5) + 20(7^{3/2} \\cdot 5^{3/2}) + 15(7 \\cdot 5^2) + 6(\\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^{5/2}) + 5^3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Then I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^{5/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} + 15 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 20 \\cdot 7^{3/2} \\cdot 5^{3/2} + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot 5^{5/2} + 5^3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 457335, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "7af970f3-c874-4e6e-8be8-864b8e9ed7f6", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:48:06.819439", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the greatest integer less than $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6.$  (Do not use a calculator!)", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFirst, we can square $x = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}$ and $y = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5},$ to get\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 + 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}, \\\\\ny^2 &= (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^2 = 7 - 2 \\sqrt{35} + 5 = 12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Note that $x^2$ and $y^2$ are radical conjugates.  Also, $x^2 y^2 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(12 - 2 \\sqrt{35}) = 12^2 - 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 4,$ so\n\\[y^2 = \\frac{4}{x^2} = \\frac{4}{12 + 2 \\sqrt{35}} < 1.\\]Then\n\\[x^4 = (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})^2 = 12^2 + 2 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 2 \\sqrt{35} + 2^2 \\cdot 35 = 284 + 48 \\sqrt{35},\\]and\n\\begin{align*}\nx^6 &= x^2 \\cdot x^4 \\\\\n&= (12 + 2 \\sqrt{35})(284 + 48 \\sqrt{35}) \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 284 + 12 \\cdot 48 \\sqrt{35} + 2 \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 284 + 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\cdot 48 \\cdot \\sqrt{35} \\\\\n&= 6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}.\n\\end{align*}Then $y^6$ is the radical conjugate of $x^6,$ so $y^6 = 6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}.$  Hence,\n\\[x^6 + y^6 = (6768 + 1144 \\sqrt{35}) + (6768 - 1144 \\sqrt{35}) = 13536.\\]Since $0 < y^6 < 1,$ the greatest integer less than $x^6$ is $\\boxed{13535}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "13535", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that this problem is asking me to find the greatest integer less than a certain expression, which means I need to find an upper bound for that expression.", "I also notice that the expression involves powers of square roots, which makes me think of the binomial theorem and how it can be used to expand $(a + b)^n$ for any positive integers $n$.", "I recall that the binomial theorem states that $(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$ is the binomial coefficient.", "I apply the binomial theorem to the given expression, with $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} 7^{3-k} 5^k.$", "I simplify this expression by expanding the binomial coefficients and the powers of $7$ and $5$, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 20 \\cdot 5^3 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^3 + 5^6.$", "I further simplify this expression by adding the terms with the same powers of $5$, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 7^3 + 6 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 30 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 5^2 + 26 \\cdot 5^3 + 5^6.$", "I evaluate the numerical values of these terms, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 343 + 1470 + 5250 + 16250 + 15625.$", "I add these terms, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = 44938.$", "I conclude that the greatest integer less than $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ is $44937.$", "# Answer\n\n44937"], "pre_generated_answer": "44937", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 7.304988076240387e-09}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this problem is asking me to find the greatest integer less than a certain expression, which means I need to find an upper bound for that expression.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also notice that the expression involves powers of square roots, which makes me think of the binomial theorem and how it can be used to expand $(a + b)^n$ for any positive integers $n$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that the binomial theorem states that $(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k$, where $\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$ is the binomial coefficient.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I apply the binomial theorem to the given expression, with $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} 7^{3-k} 5^k.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I wonder if I can apply the binomial theorem to the expression $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6$ by letting $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and see what happens.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I apply the binomial theorem to the given expression, with $a = \\sqrt{7}$, $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and $n = 6$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I apply the binomial theorem to the given expression, with $a = \\sqrt{7}$ and $b = \\sqrt{5}$, and get that $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} 7^{3-k} 5^k.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I apply the binomial theorem to the given expression, and I get $(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^6 \\binom{6}{k} 7^{3-k} 5^k.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 243081, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "68e6d5f3-747c-4420-97b4-a30a73f639f9", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:53:00.147080", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["I see a fraction with $c$ in the numerator and another one with $c$ in the denominator, so I wonder if I can simplify the expression by multiplying everything by $c$.", "Multiplying by $c$ gives $c^2 + \\frac{ac^2}{b + c} + b(c)$.", "I notice that the middle term is the only one that still has a fraction, so I want to get rid of it somehow.", "Maybe I can use the fact that $a + b + c = (b + c) + a$ to rewrite the numerator of the middle term.", "Then I have $c^2 + \\frac{(b + c)c + ac}{b + c} + bc$, and I can split the middle fraction into two fractions.", "This gives $c^2 + c + \\frac{ac}{b + c} + bc$, and now I see that the first and the last terms are perfect squares.", "I wonder if I can complete the square by adding and subtracting $\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)^2$.", "That would give me $c^2 + c + \\frac{a^2}{4(b + c)} + \\frac{a^2}{4(b + c)} - \\frac{a^2}{4(b + c)} + bc - \\frac{a^2}{4} + \\frac{a^2}{4}$.", "Simplifying a bit, I get $c^2 + c + \\frac{a^2}{2(b + c)} + bc + \\frac{a^2}{4} - \\frac{a^2}{4(b + c)}$.", "Now I can group the terms into two perfect squares: $(c + \\frac{a}{2})^2$ and $(b + \\frac{a}{2c})^2$.", "So the original expression is equivalent to $\\frac{(c + \\frac{a}{2})^2}{c} + \\frac{(b + \\frac{a}{2c})^2}{c}$.", "I notice that this is a symmetric expression in $a$ and $b$, so I can interchange them and get the same value.", "I also notice that this expression is always positive, since $a, b, c$ are positive and squares are nonnegative.", "I wonder what happens when I make $a, b, c$ very large or very small.", "If I make $a, b, c$ very large, then the fractions $\\frac{a}{2}$ and $\\frac{a}{2c}$ become negligible, and the expression approaches $1 + 1 = 2$.", "If I make $a, b, c$ very small, then the fractions $\\frac{a}{2}$ and $\\frac{a}{2c}$ become dominant, and the expression approaches infinity.", "So the expression can take any value between $2$ and infinity, but not $2$ or infinity itself.", "Therefore, the set of all possible values is $(2, \\infty)$, the open interval from $2$ to infinity.", "# Answer\n\n(2, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "(2, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 5.6603715988760936e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I see a fraction with $c$ in the numerator and another one with $c$ in the denominator, so I wonder if I can simplify the expression by multiplying everything by $c$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Multiplying by $c$ gives $c^2 + \\frac{ac^2}{b + c} + b(c)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "If I do that, I get\n\\[c^2 + \\frac{ac^2}{b + c} + bc.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Doing so, I get \\[c^2 + \\frac{ac^2}{b + c} + b.\\] This looks a bit simpler, but I still have a fraction to deal with.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I do that, I get\n\\[\\frac{c^2}{a} + \\frac{ac}{b + c} + b.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If I do that, I get $c^2 + a + b + \\frac{bc}{c} + \\frac{ac}{b + c} + b$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 188939, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "fbc13b41-a7f0-4f72-b9b9-bb66101e39f3", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:44:10.098641", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a tricky problem involving fractions and inequalities.", "I notice that the fractions have a common denominator of $abc$, so I might try to rewrite the expression as a single fraction over $abc$ and see if that simplifies things.", "Multiplying the numerator and denominator of each term by $abc$, I get\n\\[\\frac{c^2 b + a^2 c + ab^2}{abc}.\\]", "Now, I wonder if I can factor the numerator somehow, or use some identity or inequality to relate it to something else.", "One thing that comes to mind is the arithmetic mean-geometric mean (AM-GM) inequality, which says that for any nonnegative numbers $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$, we have\n\\[\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n}{n} \\ge \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\ldots x_n},\\]\nwith equality if and only if $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n$.", "Maybe I can apply this to the three terms in the numerator, since they are all nonnegative.", "Letting $x_1 = c^2 b$, $x_2 = a^2 c$, and $x_3 = ab^2$, I get\n\\[\\frac{c^2 b + a^2 c + ab^2}{3} \\ge \\sqrt[3]{c^2 b a^2 c ab^2}.\\]", "Simplifying the right-hand side, I get\n\\[\\frac{c^2 b + a^2 c + ab^2}{3} \\ge abc.\\]", "Multiplying both sides by $3/abc$, I get\n\\[\\frac{c^2 b + a^2 c + ab^2}{abc} \\ge 3.\\]", "This means that the original expression is always greater than or equal to 3, and it is equal to 3 only when $c^2 b = a^2 c = ab^2$, which implies that $a = b = c$.", "So, the minimum possible value of the expression is 3, and it occurs when $a = b = c$.", "What about the maximum possible value?", "Is there one?", "I notice that if I fix two of the variables, say $a$ and $b$, and let the third one, say $c$, go to infinity, then the expression goes to infinity as well.", "To see this, I can use limits.", "As $c \\to \\infty$, we have\n\\[\\lim_{c \\to \\infty} \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} = \\lim_{c \\to \\infty} \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{b}{c} + \\lim_{c \\to \\infty} \\frac{a}{b + c}.\\]", "The first two terms go to infinity, since we have a constant over a variable that goes to zero, and the third term goes to zero, since we have a constant over a variable that goes to infinity.", "So, the expression has no upper bound, and it can take on any value greater than or equal to 3, depending on the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$.", "Therefore, the set of all possible values of the expression is the interval $[3, \\infty)$.", "# Answer\n\n[3, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "[3, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0024950299624064143}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a tricky problem involving fractions and inequalities.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the fractions have a common denominator of $abc$, so I might try to rewrite the expression as a single fraction over $abc$ and see if that simplifies things.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the expression or rewrite it in a more convenient form.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if there is a way to simplify the expression or make it more symmetric.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if there is some way to simplify the expression or write it in a different form.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 598308, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "8ad3298c-1676-43c3-bd7b-dbf2f030cd43", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:16:35.422854", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves finding the range of an expression involving three variables.", "One possible strategy is to try to eliminate some variables and write the expression in terms of one variable only.", "To do this, I might look for some common factors or symmetries in the expression.", "I notice that the expression is cyclically symmetric in $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$", "That is, if I swap any two of them, the expression stays the same.", "This means that I can assume, without loss of generality, that $a \\leq b \\leq c.$", "This reduces the number of cases I have to consider.", "Now, I want to see how the expression behaves when $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ vary within this constraint.", "I can try some extreme cases, such as when $a$ is very small or very large compared to $b$ and $c.$", "If $a$ is very small, then $\\frac{c}{a}$ is very large, and the other two terms are close to $\\frac{a}{c}$ and $\\frac{b}{c},$ which are both small.", "So the expression is dominated by the first term, and it can get arbitrarily large as $a$ approaches zero.", "If $a$ is very large, then $\\frac{a}{b + c}$ is very large, and the other two terms are close to $\\frac{c}{a}$ and $\\frac{b}{a},$ which are both small.", "So the expression is again dominated by one term, and it can get arbitrarily large as $a$ goes to infinity.", "This tells me that the expression has no upper bound, and it can take any value greater than some minimum.", "To find the minimum, I need to look for a situation where the expression is as small as possible.", "This might happen when the variables are close to each other, so that none of the terms is much larger or smaller than the others.", "In fact, I can guess that the minimum occurs when $a = b = c.$", "In that case, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{3}{2}.$", "To verify that this is indeed the minimum, I can use a technique called Lagrange multipliers, which involves finding the stationary points of the expression subject to the constraint that $a + b + c$ is constant.", "Alternatively, I can use a method called the AM-GM inequality, which states that for any non-negative numbers $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n,$ the arithmetic mean is always greater than or equal to the geometric mean.", "That is, \n\\[\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\dots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\dots x_n}.\\]", "This inequality is useful for finding the minimum or maximum of expressions involving products or quotients of variables.", "In this problem, I can apply the AM-GM inequality to the three terms in the expression, with $n = 3.$", "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}}.\\]", "Simplifying, I get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{a + b + c}}.\\]", "Since $a, b, c$ are positive, the right-hand side is positive as well.", "Therefore, the expression is always greater than or equal to $3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{a + b + c}}.$", "This lower bound is achieved when the equality in the AM-GM inequality holds, which happens if and only if the three terms are equal.", "This implies that $\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b}{c}.$", "Solving for $a, b, c$ in terms of one of them, say $a,$ I get $b = a$ and $c = a.$", "So the minimum occurs when $a = b = c,$ as I guessed before.", "Plugging in these values, I get the minimum value of the expression as $\\frac{3}{2}.$", "# Answer\n\n\\[\\frac{3}{2}, \\infty\\)"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\[\\frac{3}{2}, \\infty\\)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.7372562996170326e-12}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem involves finding the range of an expression involving three variables.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One possible strategy is to try to eliminate some variables and write the expression in terms of one variable only.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To do this, I might look for some common factors or symmetries in the expression.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression is cyclically symmetric in $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $b$, meaning that swapping them does not change the value.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the first and last terms have $c$ in the denominator, so I can factor out $c$ from the whole expression and get\n\\[\\frac{1}{c}\\left(c + \\frac{a^2}{b + c} + b\\right).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $b$, meaning that swapping $a$ and $b$ does not change the value of the expression.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $b$, meaning that swapping $a$ and $b$ does not change the value.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 870749, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "121b3235-f674-44e4-9390-f48bbc88ea87", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:07:35.211649", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is an interesting problem, because it seems like there could be many different values for this expression depending on the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$", "However, there might be some simplifications or transformations that could help us find a more restricted range of values.", "One idea is to use the fact that $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are positive, and try to apply the AM-GM inequality, which says that for any positive numbers $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n,$ the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean, that is,\n\\[\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.\\]", "The equality holds if and only if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n.$", "In our case, we can apply the AM-GM inequality to the three terms of the expression, that is,\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}}.\\]", "Simplifying the radical, we get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3.\\]", "The equality holds if and only if $\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b}{c},$ which implies that $a = b = c.$", "So we have found a lower bound for the expression, and we know when it is attained.", "But what about the upper bound?", "Is there a maximum value for the expression, or can it be arbitrarily large?", "One way to approach this question is to try to make one of the terms as large as possible, and see what happens to the other two terms.", "For example, suppose we try to make $\\frac{c}{a}$ as large as possible by letting $c$ be very large and $a$ be very small.", "Then we have\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\approx \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{b}{c},\\]\nsince the term $\\frac{a}{b + c}$ becomes negligible.", "Now, to make $\\frac{c}{a}$ even larger, we can also let $b$ be very small.", "Then we have\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\approx \\frac{c}{a}.\\]", "This suggests that there is no upper bound for the expression, as we can make $\\frac{c}{a}$ arbitrarily large by choosing appropriate values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$", "To confirm this, we can also use calculus to show that the expression has no local maximum.", "If we treat $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ as independent variables, then we can find the partial derivatives of the expression with respect to each of them, and set them equal to zero to find the critical points.", "After some algebra, we find that the only critical point is $(a, b, c) = (1, 1, 1),$ which is the same as the point where the expression attains its minimum value of 3.", "To see that this is not a local maximum, we can use the second derivative test, or simply observe that if we perturb any of the variables slightly, the expression increases.", "Therefore, we have shown that the expression has no upper bound, and its minimum value is 3.", "# Answer\n\n[3, \\infty)\n\n# Answer\n\n[3, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "[3, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.009958826235649311}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is an interesting problem, because it seems like there could be many different values for this expression depending on the values of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "However, there might be some simplifications or transformations that could help us find a more restricted range of values.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One idea is to use the fact that $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are positive, and try to apply the AM-GM inequality, which says that for any positive numbers $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n,$ the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean, that is,\n\\[\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The equality holds if and only if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In our case, we can apply the AM-GM inequality to the three terms of the expression, that is,\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying the radical, we get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Simplifying the right hand side, we get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{(b+c)a}}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, we can simplify the right-hand side by cancelling out some factors, and we get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{ab}{a(b + c)c}}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying the right-hand side, we get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{ab}{(b + c)^2}}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The right-hand side simplifies nicely, as we can cancel out the $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ terms, and get\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{(b + c)^2}}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 459333, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5b158ac4-13d1-40db-8987-3856a5926c39", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:06:26.441557", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ so I can assume without loss of generality that $a \\leq b \\leq c.$", "I also notice that each term in the expression is positive, so the expression is minimized when each term is as small as possible, and maximized when each term is as large as possible.", "To minimize the first term, $\\frac{c}{a},$ I want $c$ to be as small as possible and $a$ to be as large as possible, subject to the constraint that $a \\leq b \\leq c.$", "This happens when $a = b = c,$ so the first term is $\\frac{c}{a} = 1.$", "To minimize the second term, $\\frac{a}{b + c},$ I want $a$ to be as small as possible and $b + c$ to be as large as possible, subject to the same constraint.", "This also happens when $a = b = c,$ so the second term is $\\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{1}{2}.$", "To minimize the third term, $\\frac{b}{c},$ I want $b$ to be as small as possible and $c$ to be as large as possible, subject to the same constraint.", "This also happens when $a = b = c,$ so the third term is $\\frac{b}{c} = 1.$", "Therefore, the minimum value of the expression is $1 + \\frac{1}{2} + 1 = \\frac{5}{2}.$", "To maximize the first term, $\\frac{c}{a},$ I want $c$ to be as large as possible and $a$ to be as small as possible, subject to the constraint that $a \\leq b \\leq c.$", "This happens when $a$ approaches zero and $c$ approaches infinity, so the first term is unbounded above.", "To maximize the second term, $\\frac{a}{b + c},$ I want $a$ to be as large as possible and $b + c$ to be as small as possible, subject to the same constraint.", "This happens when $a$ approaches infinity and $b$ and $c$ approach zero, so the second term is unbounded above.", "To maximize the third term, $\\frac{b}{c},$ I want $b$ to be as large as possible and $c$ to be as small as possible, subject to the same constraint.", "This happens when $b$ approaches infinity and $c$ approaches zero, so the third term is unbounded above.", "Therefore, the maximum value of the expression is infinite.", "Thus, the set of all possible values of the expression is $(\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty).$", "# Answer\n\n(\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "(\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.8514862707190408e-08}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ so I ca