{"labeler": "6c655923-0179-4f96-afbc-f52af3de908d", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:04:59.135354", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a hard problem, but maybe there is some symmetry or algebraic manipulation that can simplify it.", "I notice that the denominators of the fractions are all sums of two of the variables, and the numerators are the other variables.", "That reminds me of a famous identity involving the arithmetic mean and the harmonic mean of two numbers.", "Recall that the arithmetic mean of two numbers $x$ and $y$ is $\\frac{x+y}{2}$ and the harmonic mean of $x$ and $y$ is $\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}}$.", "The identity says that the arithmetic mean is always greater than or equal to the harmonic mean, with equality if and only if $x=y$.", "How can I use this identity to help me with the problem?", "Well, I can rewrite each fraction as a ratio of an arithmetic mean and a harmonic mean.", "For example, \\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{\\frac{c+a}{2}}{\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{c}+\\frac{1}{a}}}.\\]", "Similarly, I can rewrite the other fractions in the same way.", "Then, using the identity, I can see that each fraction is greater than or equal to 1, with equality if and only if the two variables in the denominator are equal.", "So, for example, \\[\\frac{c}{a} \\geq 1, \\quad \\text{with equality if and only if } a=c.\\]", "Now I have a lower bound for each fraction, which means I also have a lower bound for their sum.", "Adding them up, I get \\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, \\quad \\text{with equality if and only if } a=b=c.\\]", "So the set of all possible values of the expression is the interval $[3, \\infty)$.", "To see that there is no upper bound, I can take any one of the fractions and make it arbitrarily large by making the numerator much larger than the denominator.", "For example, if I let $c$ go to infinity and keep $a$ and $b$ fixed, then \\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\approx \\frac{c}{a} + 0 + 0 \\to \\infty.\\]", "# Answer\n\n[3, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "[3, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00047441983675847134}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a hard problem, but maybe there is some symmetry or algebraic manipulation that can simplify it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the denominators of the fractions are all sums of two of the variables, and the numerators are the other variables.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I notice that the expression has three terms, each with a different fraction.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if it helps to rewrite the expression using a common denominator of $abc(a + b + c)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the expression has three terms, and each term involves one of the variables in the numerator and the other two in the denominator. Maybe I can use that to find some patterns or relationships.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the fractions have a common denominator of $abc$, so I can write the expression as\n\\[\\frac{c^2 + a^2 + b^2 + ab}{abc}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 233432, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "e20e861c-11d7-4a17-ac55-818f71d72641", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T19:04:48.954584", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the set of all possible values of\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let\n\\[S = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]Then\n\\[S + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} + 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c}.\\]By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nS + 1 &= \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b + c}{c} \\\\\n&\\ge 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b + c}{c}} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{align*}Note that equality occurs if and only if\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{a}{b + c} = \\frac{b + c}{c} = 1.\\]Since $b$ and $c$ are positive,\n\\[\\frac{b + c}{c} > 1,\\]which tells us that equality cannot occur.  Therefore, $S + 1 > 3,$ which means $S > 2.$\n\nWe claim that $S$ can take on all real numbers that are greater than 2.  Let $c = a,$ so\n\\[S = 1 + \\frac{a}{b + a} + \\frac{b}{a}.\\]As $b$ approaches 0, this expression approaches 2.  This tells us that we can make this expression arbitrarily close to 2 as we want.\n\nOn the other hand, as $b$ becomes very large, the expression also becomes very large.  This tells us that can we can make this expression arbitrarily large.  Hence, by a continuity argument, $S$ can take on all values in $\\boxed{(2,\\infty)}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "(2,\\infty)", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a hard problem, but maybe there is some symmetry or algebraic manipulation that can simplify it.", "I notice that the denominators of the fractions are all sums of two of the variables, and the numerators are the other variables.", "That reminds me of a famous identity involving the arithmetic mean and the harmonic mean of two numbers.", "Recall that the arithmetic mean of two numbers $x$ and $y$ is $\\frac{x+y}{2}$ and the harmonic mean of $x$ and $y$ is $\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}}$.", "The identity says that the arithmetic mean is always greater than or equal to the harmonic mean, with equality if and only if $x=y$.", "How can I use this identity to help me with the problem?", "Well, I can rewrite each fraction as a ratio of an arithmetic mean and a harmonic mean.", "For example, \\[\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{\\frac{c+a}{2}}{\\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{c}+\\frac{1}{a}}}.\\]", "Similarly, I can rewrite the other fractions in the same way.", "Then, using the identity, I can see that each fraction is greater than or equal to 1, with equality if and only if the two variables in the denominator are equal.", "So, for example, \\[\\frac{c}{a} \\geq 1, \\quad \\text{with equality if and only if } a=c.\\]", "Now I have a lower bound for each fraction, which means I also have a lower bound for their sum.", "Adding them up, I get \\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, \\quad \\text{with equality if and only if } a=b=c.\\]", "So the set of all possible values of the expression is the interval $[3, \\infty)$.", "To see that there is no upper bound, I can take any one of the fractions and make it arbitrarily large by making the numerator much larger than the denominator.", "For example, if I let $c$ go to infinity and keep $a$ and $b$ fixed, then \\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\approx \\frac{c}{a} + 0 + 0 \\to \\infty.\\]", "# Answer\n\n[3, \\infty)"], "pre_generated_answer": "[3, \\infty)", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00047441983675847134}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a hard problem, but maybe there is some symmetry or algebraic manipulation that can simplify it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the denominators of the fractions are all sums of two of the variables, and the numerators are the other variables.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "One thing I notice is that the expression is homogeneous, meaning that if I multiply all of $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ by the same constant, the expression doesn't change.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One thing that stands out to me is that the denominators of the fractions are always sums of two of the variables. Maybe I can use that to rewrite the expression in a different way.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the denominators are all sums of two of the variables, so maybe it would help to find a common denominator for the whole expression and see what happens.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the expression involves fractions with $a$, $b$, and $c$ in various combinations. Maybe I can write it as a common fraction with a single denominator.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 231408, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "78d2023a-0bfc-486e-bd72-a1785e777ccb", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:56:54.589307", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the inverse function of $f$, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "But $f$ is defined by two different formulas depending on the value of $x$, so I need to consider each case separately.", "For the first case, when $x \\leq 3$, I have $y=3-x$.", "Switching $x$ and $y$, I get $x=3-y$, or $y=3-x$.", "So the inverse function for this case is $f^{-1}(x) = 3-x$, but only when $x \\leq 3$.", "For the second case, when $x>3$, I have $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "Switching $x$ and $y$, I get $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "This is a cubic equation in $y$, which is harder to solve.", "I can try to factor it or use the rational root theorem, but there is an easier way.", "I notice that the original function $f$ is decreasing when $x>3$, because the derivative is $f'(x) = -3x^2+4x+3$, which is negative for $x>3$.", "That means that the inverse function $f^{-1}$ is also decreasing when $x>3$, because if $f(a) > f(b)$, then $f^{-1}(f(a)) < f^{-1}(f(b))$.", "So if I want to find $f^{-1}(0)$, I just need to find the largest value of $x$ for which $f(x)=0$, and that will be the same as $f^{-1}(0)$.", "To find that value of $x$, I can use the second formula for $f$ and set it equal to zero: $-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$.", "This equation has a factor of $-x$, so I can divide both sides by $-x$ and get $x^2-2x-3=0$.", "This is a quadratic equation, which I can solve by factoring or using the quadratic formula.", "I see that it factors as $(x-3)(x+1)=0$.", "So the solutions are $x=3$ and $x=-1$.", "But remember, I only care about the values of $x$ that are greater than $3$, because that's the domain of the second formula for $f$.", "So the only relevant solution is $x=3$, and that means $f^{-1}(0)=3$.", "Similarly, if I want to find $f^{-1}(6)$, I need to find the smallest value of $x$ for which $f(x)=6$, and that will be the same as $f^{-1}(6)$.", "To find that value of $x$, I can use the first formula for $f$ and set it equal to $6$: $3-x=6$.", "This is a simple linear equation, which I can solve by adding $x$ to both sides and subtracting $6$ from both sides: $x=-3$.", "So $f^{-1}(6)=-3$.", "Finally, to answer the question, I need to add $f^{-1}(0)$ and $f^{-1}(6)$: $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)=3+(-3)=0$.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.453453466805769e-06}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the inverse function of $f$, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But $f$ is defined by two different formulas depending on the value of $x$, so I need to consider each case separately.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the first case, when $x \\leq 3$, I have $y=3-x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Switching $x$ and $y$, I get $x=3-y$, or $y=3-x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the inverse function for this case is $f^{-1}(x) = 3-x$, but only when $x \\leq 3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the second case, when $x>3$, I have $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Switching $x$ and $y$, I get $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Switching $x$ and $y$, I get $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 411821, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "1d9e002e-fe77-4249-95c2-80ecb0128f59", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:56:15.172466", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the inverse of a function, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "For the piecewise function $f$, I need to consider two cases: when $y \\leq 3$ and when $y > 3$.", "If $y \\leq 3$, then I have $y = 3-x$, so switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x = 3-y$.", "Solving for $y$, I get $y = 3-x$, which is the same as the original equation.", "So this part of $f$ is self-inverse.", "If $y > 3$, then I have $y = -x^3+2x^2+3x$, so switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x = -y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "Solving for $y$ in this case is more complicated.", "I need to use the cubic formula or some other method to find the roots of the polynomial $-y^3+2y^2+3y-x$.", "Since I only care about the values of $y$ that make $x$ equal to $0$ or $6$, I can try to factor the polynomial by guessing some possible roots.", "I notice that $y = 0$ is a root, since plugging it in gives me $x = 0$.", "So I can divide the polynomial by $y$ to get $-y^2+2y+3-x/y$.", "Now I need to find the roots of the quadratic equation $-y^2+2y+3-x/y = 0$.", "I can multiply both sides by $y$ and rearrange to get $y^3-2y^2-3y+x = 0$.", "I can use the rational root theorem to look for possible rational roots of this equation.", "The possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm x, \\pm x/3$.", "I can try plugging in these values and see if any of them work.", "I find that $y = -x/3$ is a root, since it gives me $-x^3/27+2x^2/9-3x/3+x = 0$.", "So I can divide the polynomial by $y+x/3$ to get $y^2-x^2/9-4x/3$.", "To find the last root, I can use the quadratic formula on $y^2-x^2/9-4x/3 = 0$.", "I get \\[y = \\frac{-(-x^2/9-4x/3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-x^2/9-4x/3)^2-4(1)(0)}}{2(1)}\\]", "Simplifying, I get \\[y = \\frac{x^2/9+4x/3 \\pm \\sqrt{x^4/81+8x^3/27+16x^2/9}}{2}\\]", "Now I have three possible values of $y$ for each value of $x$, but only one of them is the inverse of $f$.", "I need to check which one satisfies the condition that $y > 3$.", "If $x = 0$, then the three values of $y$ are $0, -0, 4$.", "Only $4$ is greater than $3$, so $f^{-1}(0) = 4$.", "If $x = 6$, then the three values of $y$ are $-2, -10, 8$.", "Only $8$ is greater than $3$, so $f^{-1}(6) = 8$.", "Therefore, the answer is $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 4 + 8 = 12$.", "# Answer\n\n12"], "pre_generated_answer": "12", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.5706516991137007e-20}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the inverse of a function, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the piecewise function $f$, I need to consider two cases: when $y \\leq 3$ and when $y > 3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "For the piecewise function $f$, I will have to do this separately for each case, and then match up the correct domains and ranges for $f^{-1}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So for $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to find the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Since $f$ has two pieces, I need to consider each case separately and find the corresponding piece of $f^{-1}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "That means I need to consider two cases: one where $y=3-x$ and one where $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 400599, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5b158ac4-13d1-40db-8987-3856a5926c39", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:54:28.914297", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to solve $f(x)=0$.", "There are two cases to consider: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x>3$.", "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x)=3-x=0$ implies that $x=3$.", "So $f^{-1}(0)=3$ in this case.", "If $x>3$, then $f(x)=-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$ is a cubic equation.", "I can try to factor this equation by looking for a rational root.", "A rational root must be of the form $\\pm p/q$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term (0) and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient (-1).", "So the only possible rational roots are $\\pm 1$.", "I can use synthetic division to test these candidates.", "If I try $x=1$, I get:\n\n\\begin{array}{r|rrr}\n 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n   &    & -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ \\hline\n   & -1 & 1 & 4 & 4 \\\\\n\\end{array}", "This means that $-x^3+2x^2+3x=(x-1)(-x^2+x+4)$.", "The remainder is not zero, so $x=1$ is not a root.", "If I try $x=-1$, I get:\n\n\\begin{array}{r|rrr}\n -1 & -1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n    &    & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ \\hline\n    & -1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}", "This means that $-x^3+2x^2+3x=(x+1)(-x^2+3x)$.", "The remainder is zero, so $x=-1$ is a root.", "I can now factor the quadratic factor further by finding two numbers that multiply to 0 and add to 3.", "The numbers are 0 and 3, so $-x^2+3x=-x(x-3)$.", "Therefore, the complete factorization of $-x^3+2x^2+3x$ is $(x+1)(-x)(x-3)$.", "The other two roots are $x=0$ and $x=3$.", "However, these roots are not valid for the case $x>3$, so I can ignore them.", "The only root that satisfies $f(x)=0$ and $x>3$ is $x=-1$.", "So $f^{-1}(0)=-1$ in this case.", "To find $f^{-1}(6)$, I need to solve $f(x)=6$.", "Again, there are two cases to consider: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x>3$.", "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x)=3-x=6$ implies that $x=-3$.", "So $f^{-1}(6)=-3$ in this case.", "If $x>3$, then $f(x)=-x^3+2x^2+3x=6$ is another cubic equation.", "I can use the same factorization that I found earlier: $(x+1)(-x)(x-3)=6$.", "This equation has three solutions: $x=-2$, $x=-3/2$, and $x=4$.", "The only solution that satisfies $f(x)=6$ and $x>3$ is $x=4$.", "So $f^{-1}(6)=4$ in this case.", "To find the final answer, I need to add $f^{-1}(0)$ and $f^{-1}(6)$.", "There are two possible ways to do this, depending on which case I choose for each inverse value.", "If I choose $f^{-1}(0)=3$ and $f^{-1}(6)=-3$, then the sum is 0.", "If I choose $f^{-1}(0)=-1$ and $f^{-1}(6)=4$, then the sum is 3.", "The problem does not specify which case to use, so I will report both possible answers.", "# Answer\n\n0 or 3"], "pre_generated_answer": "0 or 3", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.3196260180880242e-11}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to solve $f(x)=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "There are two cases to consider: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x>3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x)=3-x=0$ implies that $x=3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So $f^{-1}(0)=3$ in this case.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $x>3$, then $f(x)=-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$ is a cubic equation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can try to factor this equation by looking for a rational root.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "A rational root must be of the form $\\pm p/q$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term (0) and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient (-1).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the only possible rational roots are $\\pm 1$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Since 0 has only one factor, namely itself, the only possible rational roots are $\\pm 0$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The only possibility is $p=0$ and $q=1$, so the only rational root is 0.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "But the only factor of 0 is 0, so the only possible rational root is 0.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So the only possible rational roots are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 0$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 268343, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "121b3235-f674-44e4-9390-f48bbc88ea87", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:53:38.775362", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the inverse of a function, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and then solve for $y$.", "In this case, the function $f$ has two different expressions depending on whether $x$ is less than or equal to $3$ or greater than $3$.", "So I need to consider two cases: when $y=0$ and when $y=6$.", "When $y=0$, I can use the first expression for $f(x)$ and write $0=3-x$.", "Solving for $x$, I get $x=3$.", "This means that $f^{-1}(0)=3$, since $f(3)=0$.", "When $y=6$, I can use the second expression for $f(x)$ and write $6=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "This is a cubic equation, which is harder to solve, but I can try to look for a rational root using the rational root theorem.", "The possible rational roots are the ratios of the factors of the constant term ($6$) over the factors of the leading coefficient ($-1$).", "So the possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6$.", "I can try plugging in these values into the equation and see if any of them work.", "I see that $x=2$ works, since $6=-(2)^3+2(2)^2+3(2)$.", "This means that $f^{-1}(6)=2$, since $f(2)=6$.", "Now I have both values of $f^{-1}(0)$ and $f^{-1}(6)$, so I can add them up to get the final answer.", "Therefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)=3+2=5$.", "# Answer\n\n5"], "pre_generated_answer": "5", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.012204402364006068}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the inverse of a function, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and then solve for $y$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In this case, the function $f$ has two different expressions depending on whether $x$ is less than or equal to $3$ or greater than $3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I need to consider two cases: when $y=0$ and when $y=6$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "When $y=0$, I can use the first expression for $f(x)$ and write $0=3-x$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "When $y=0$, I have two possibilities: either $x \\leq 3$ and $3-x=0$, or $x>3$ and $-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If $y=0$, then I have either $3-x=0$ or $-x^3+2x^2+3x=0$ depending on the value of $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "When $y=0$, I have two possible equations:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n3-x &= 0 \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x &= 0 \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{align*}", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "When $y=0$, I have $0=f(x)$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 506335, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6bc09742-9848-409e-bac0-ad7f7aa7216c", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:51:21.794592", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to solve for $x$ when $f(x) = 0$.", "There are two cases to consider: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x > 3$.", "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x) = 3-x$, so $f(x) = 0$ implies $3-x = 0$ or $x = 3$.", "If $x > 3$, then $f(x) = -x^3+2x^2+3x$, so $f(x) = 0$ implies $-x^3+2x^2+3x = 0$ or $x(x^2-2x-3) = 0$.", "This factors as $x(x-3)(x+1) = 0$, so the possible solutions are $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.", "However, only $x = -1$ is valid, since $x = 0$ and $x = 3$ do not satisfy $x > 3$.", "Therefore, $f^{-1}(0) = -1$ or $3$, depending on which branch of $f$ is used.", "Similarly, to find $f^{-1}(6)$, I need to solve for $x$ when $f(x) = 6$.", "Again, there are two cases: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x > 3$.", "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x) = 3-x$, so $f(x) = 6$ implies $3-x = 6$ or $x = -3$.", "If $x > 3$, then $f(x) = -x^3+2x^2+3x$, so $f(x) = 6$ implies $-x^3+2x^2+3x = 6$ or $-x^3+2x^2+3x-6 = 0$.", "This does not factor easily, so I use the rational root theorem to look for possible rational roots.", "The possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6$.", "I try each one and find that only $x = 2$ works.", "Therefore, $f^{-1}(6) = -3$ or $2$, depending on which branch of $f$ is used.", "Now, to find $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$, I need to add the corresponding values of $x$ from each branch.", "There are four possible combinations: $(-1)+(-3) = -4$, $(-1)+2 = 1$, $3+(-3) = 0$, and $3+2 = 5$.", "However, not all of these are valid, since they imply that $f$ is not one-to-one.", "For $f$ to be one-to-one, it must map different inputs to different outputs.", "This means that if $f^{-1}(0) = a$ and $f^{-1}(6) = b$, then $f(a) = 0$ and $f(b) = 6$ must be true, and $f(a) \\neq f(b)$.", "This eliminates the cases where $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 0$ or $5$, since they imply that $f(3) = 0$ and $f(3) = 6$, which is a contradiction.", "Therefore, the only valid answers are $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = -4$ or $1$, depending on which branch of $f$ is used.", "# Answer\n\n-4 \\text{ or } 1"], "pre_generated_answer": "-4 \\text{ or } 1", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00035255104373041837}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find $f^{-1}(0)$, I need to solve for $x$ when $f(x) = 0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "There are two cases to consider: either $x \\leq 3$ or $x > 3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $x \\leq 3$, then $f(x) = 3-x$, so $f(x) = 0$ implies $3-x = 0$ or $x = 3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $x > 3$, then $f(x) = -x^3+2x^2+3x$, so $f(x) = 0$ implies $-x^3+2x^2+3x = 0$ or $x(x^2-2x-3) = 0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This factors as $x(x-3)(x+1) = 0$, so the possible solutions are $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "However, only $x = -1$ is valid, since $x = 0$ and $x = 3$ do not satisfy $x > 3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "However, since $x > 3$ in this case, only $x = 3$ is valid.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, only $x = -1$ is valid, since $x > 3$ in this case. Therefore, $f^{-1}(0) = -1$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, only $x = -1$ satisfies the condition that $x > 3$, so this is the only solution in this case.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, $x = 3$ is not a valid solution, since it does not satisfy $x > 3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 254836, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "bcaf5f62-1dea-4a15-ad90-1e3cf079832f", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:36:03.710851", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a$ and $b$ be positive real numbers such that $ab^2 = 5.$  Find the minimum value of $a^4 + b^8.$", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a^4 + b^8 \\ge 2 \\sqrt{a^4 b^8} = 2a^2 b^4 = 2(ab^2)^2 = 50.\\]Equality occurs when $a^4 = b^8$ and $ab^2 = 5;$ we can solve to get $a = \\sqrt{5}$ and $b = \\sqrt[4]{5},$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{50}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "50", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that this problem involves a product of two variables ($ab^2$) and a sum of two powers ($a^4 + b^8$).", "I wonder if I can use the AM-GM inequality, which says that for any non-negative numbers $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n,$ the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean: $\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\dots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\dots x_n}.$", "If I can write $a^4 + b^8$ as the arithmetic mean of some numbers, and $ab^2$ as the geometric mean of the same numbers, then I can apply the inequality and find a lower bound for $a^4 + b^8.$", "I notice that $a^4 = (a^2)^2$ and $b^8 = (b^4)^2.$", "This suggests that I can write $a^4 + b^8$ as the sum of two squares, which is the same as the arithmetic mean of four equal terms.", "Specifically, $a^4 + b^8 = \\frac{a^4 + a^4 + b^8 + b^8}{4} = \\frac{(a^2)^2 + (a^2)^2 + (b^4)^2 + (b^4)^2}{4}.$", "Now, I can use the AM-GM inequality with $n = 4$ and $x_1 = x_2 = a^2, x_3 = x_4 = b^4.$", "This gives me $\\frac{a^4 + b^8}{4} \\geq \\sqrt[4]{a^2 a^2 b^4 b^4} = \\sqrt{a^4 b^8} = (ab^2)^2.$", "Multiplying both sides by $4$ and using the given condition that $ab^2 = 5,$ I get $a^4 + b^8 \\geq 4(ab^2)^2 = 4 \\cdot 5^2 = 100.$", "Therefore, the minimum value of $a^4 + b^8$ is $100,$ and it is attained when $a^2 = b^4,$ or equivalently, $a = b^2.$", "# Answer\n\n100"], "pre_generated_answer": "100", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.05593283805654969}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this problem involves a product of two variables ($ab^2$) and a sum of two powers ($a^4 + b^8$).", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can use the AM-GM inequality, which says that for any non-negative numbers $x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n,$ the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean: $\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\dots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\dots x_n}.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I can write $a^4 + b^8$ as the arithmetic mean of some numbers, and $ab^2$ as the geometric mean of the same numbers, then I can apply the inequality and find a lower bound for $a^4 + b^8.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that $a^4 = (a^2)^2$ and $b^8 = (b^4)^2.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This suggests that I can write $a^4 + b^8$ as the sum of two squares, which is the same as the arithmetic mean of four equal terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Specifically, $a^4 + b^8 = \\frac{a^4 + a^4 + b^8 + b^8}{4} = \\frac{(a^2)^2 + (a^2)^2 + (b^4)^2 + (b^4)^2}{4}.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "That is, $a^4 + b^8 = \\frac{(a^2)^2 + (a^2)^2 + (b^4)^2 + (b^4)^2}{4}.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "In other words, $a^4 + b^8 = 2(a^2)^2 + 2(b^4)^2 = 4\\left(\\frac{(a^2)^2 + (b^4)^2}{4}\\right).$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Specifically, $a^4 + b^8 = \\frac{1}{4}(a^2)^2 + \\frac{1}{4}(a^2)^2 + \\frac{1}{4}(b^4)^2 + \\frac{1}{4}(b^4)^2 = \\frac{a^2 + a^2 + b^4 + b^4}{4}.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1702230, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "121b3235-f674-44e4-9390-f48bbc88ea87", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:30:34.745004", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "For how many real values of $x$ is $\\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$ an integer?", "ground_truth_solution": "Suppose that  $k = \\sqrt{120 - \\sqrt{x}}$ is an integer. Then $0\\le k \\le \\sqrt{120}$, and because $k$ is an integer, we have $0\\le k \\le 10$. Thus there are 11 possible integer values of $k$. For each such $k$, the corresponding value of $x$ is $\\left(120 - k^2\\right)^2$. Because  $\\left(120 -\nk^2\\right)^2$ is positive and decreasing for $0\\le k \\le 10$, the $\\boxed{11}$ values of $x$ are distinct.", "ground_truth_answer": "11", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find the possible values of $x$ that make the expression $\\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$ a whole number.", "One way to approach this is to work backwards from the possible values of the square root.", "If the square root is an integer, then it must be a positive integer, since the square root of a negative number is not real.", "So, let me call the square root $n$, where $n$ is a positive integer.", "Then, I can rewrite the expression as $n = \\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$.", "Squaring both sides, I get $n^2 = 120 - \\sqrt{x}$.", "Isolating $\\sqrt{x}$, I get $\\sqrt{x} = 120 - n^2$.", "Now, I need to think about what values of $n$ make this expression valid.", "First, I need to make sure that $120 - n^2$ is not negative, since the square root of a negative number is not real.", "This means that $n^2 \\leq 120$, or $n \\leq \\sqrt{120}$.", "Second, I need to make sure that $120 - n^2$ is a perfect square, since the square root of a non-perfect square is not an integer.", "This means that $120 - n^2$ must be equal to some integer $k^2$, where $k$ is also a positive integer.", "So, I have the equation $120 - n^2 = k^2$.", "Rearranging, I get $n^2 + k^2 = 120$.", "Now, I recognize this as a Pythagorean equation, where $n$ and $k$ are the legs of a right triangle and 120 is the hypotenuse squared.", "So, I need to find the pairs of positive integers $(n, k)$ that satisfy this equation.", "One way to do this is to factor 120 and look for pairs of factors that add up to an even number.", "This is because if $n$ and $k$ are both odd, then $n^2 + k^2$ is even, but not divisible by 4, which 120 is.", "And if one of $n$ and $k$ is even and the other is odd, then $n^2 + k^2$ is odd, which 120 is not.", "So, both $n$ and $k$ must be even.", "The factors of 120 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, 24, 30, 40, 60, and 120.", "The pairs of factors that add up to an even number are (1, 119), (2, 60), (3, 40), (4, 30), (5, 24), (6, 20), (8, 15), and (10, 12).", "However, not all of these pairs are valid, since some of them have one factor larger than $\\sqrt{120}$, which is about 10.95.", "The only pairs that are valid are (2, 10), (4, 8), and (6, 6).", "These correspond to the values of $n$ and $k$, which then determine the values of $x$.", "For example, if $n = 2$ and $k = 10$, then $x = (120 - n^2)^2 = (120 - 4)^2 = 116^2 = 13456$.", "Similarly, if $n = 4$ and $k = 8$, then $x = (120 - n^2)^2 = (120 - 16)^2 = 104^2 = 10816$.", "And if $n = 6$ and $k = 6$, then $x = (120 - n^2)^2 = (120 - 36)^2 = 84^2 = 7056$.", "So, there are three possible values of $x$ that make the expression $\\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$ an integer.", "# Answer\n\n3"], "pre_generated_answer": "3", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.0816476557563792e-13}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find the possible values of $x$ that make the expression $\\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$ a whole number.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to approach this is to work backwards from the possible values of the square root.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If the square root is an integer, then it must be a positive integer, since the square root of a negative number is not real.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, let me call the square root $n$, where $n$ is a positive integer.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then, I can rewrite the expression as $n = \\sqrt{120-\\sqrt{x}}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides, I get $n^2 = 120 - \\sqrt{x}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Isolating $\\sqrt{x}$, I get $\\sqrt{x} = 120 - n^2$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now, I need to think about what values of $n$ make this expression valid.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Now, I need to find the possible values of $n$ that make $\\sqrt{x}$ a real number as well.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Squaring again, I get $x = (120 - n^2)^2$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now, I need to find the possible values of $n$ that make $\\sqrt{x}$ a real number.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 579834, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "9ef92825-c3e7-4666-8db8-1ebc83fcfdfd", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:29:09.630261", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Solve\n\\[\\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 6.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} + 2 \\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36.\\]Then\n\\[2x + 2 \\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 36,\\]so\n\\[\\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 18 - x.\\]Squaring both sides, we get $x^2 - 3x - 6 = 324 - 36x + x^2.$  Hence, $x = \\boxed{10}.$  We check that this solution works.", "ground_truth_answer": "10", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression inside the square root is the same for both terms, except for the sign of the second square root.", "This makes me think of the identity\n\\[\\sqrt{a + \\sqrt{b}} + \\sqrt{a - \\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{2a}\\]\nwhen $a$ and $b$ are non-negative.", "If I can use this identity, I need to have $a = x$ and $b = 3x + 6$.", "But this only works if $x \\geq 0$ and $3x + 6 \\geq 0$, which are equivalent conditions.", "So I will assume that $x \\geq 0$ and check for any extraneous solutions later.", "Applying the identity, I get\n\\[\\sqrt{2x} = 6.\\]", "Squaring both sides, I get\n\\[2x = 36.\\]", "Dividing by 2, I get\n\\[x = 18.\\]", "To check if this is a valid solution, I plug it back into the original equation and simplify:\n\\[\\sqrt{18 + \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 18 + 6}} + \\sqrt{18 - \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 18 + 6}}\\]\n\\[= \\sqrt{18 + \\sqrt{60}} + \\sqrt{18 - \\sqrt{60}}\\]\n\\[= \\sqrt{9 + 3\\sqrt{20}} + \\sqrt{9 - 3\\sqrt{20}}\\]\n\\[= \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{3 + \\sqrt{5}} + \\sqrt{3 - \\sqrt{5}}\\right)\\]\n\\[= \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\]\n\\[= 6.\\]", "So $x = 18$ is indeed a solution to the equation.", "# Answer\n\n18"], "pre_generated_answer": "18", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.006914323565054971}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression inside the square root is the same for both terms, except for the sign of the second square root.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This makes me think of the identity\n\\[\\sqrt{a + \\sqrt{b}} + \\sqrt{a - \\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{2a}\\]\nwhen $a$ and $b$ are non-negative.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This makes me think of using the identity \\(\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{a + 2\\sqrt{ab} + b}\\), which works when \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are nonnegative.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This makes me think of trying to square both sides of the equation, since that might get rid of the square roots and simplify the expression.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if I can write this as a difference of squares, using the identity $a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This makes me think of multiplying by a conjugate, which is a common technique for simplifying square roots expressions.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 786533, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "423ec391-7def-48bd-821a-5e231333d6ce", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:26:19.411113", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Suppose that $ABCD$ is a trapezoid in which $\\overline{AD}|| \\overline{BC}$.  Given $\\overline{AC}\\perp\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{AC}$ bisects angle $\\angle BAD$, and $[ABCD]=42$, then compute $[\\triangle ACD]$.", "ground_truth_solution": "There are many ways to draw the diagram; one possibility is shown below.  We know that $\\angle BAC \\cong \\angle CAD\\cong \\angle BCA$ since $\\overline{AC}$ bisects $\\angle BAD$ and $\\overline{AD} || \\overline{BC}$.  Therefore $\\triangle BAC$ is isosceles.  In the diagram we have added segment $\\overline{BE}$ splitting $\\triangle BAC$ into two smaller congruent right triangles.  We also know that $\\triangle ACD$ is a right triangle by the givens, so we conclude that $\\triangle ACD \\sim \\triangle CEB$ since we already know that $\\angle CAD\\cong\\angle ECB$.  In fact, $\\triangle ACD $ is exactly four times the size of $\\triangle CEB$ since $AC=2(EC)$.  If we let $[\\triangle CEB]=K$, then $[\\triangle AEB]=K$ while $[\\triangle ACD]=4K$.  Thus $6K=42$, so $K=7$ and $[\\triangle ACD]=4K=\\boxed{28}$.\n\n[asy]\nimport olympiad; import graph; size(150); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)); dotfactor=4;\nint randangle = 50;\ndraw((-5,0)--(5*dir(randangle))--(5,0)--cycle);\npath x1 = (5*Cos(randangle),5*Sin(randangle))--(-10,5*Sin(randangle));\npath x2 = (-5,0)--(5dir(2*randangle));\npair X = intersectionpoint(x1,x2);\ndraw((-5,0)--X--(5*dir(randangle)));\ndraw(rightanglemark((5,0),(5*dir(50)),(-5,0),s=14));\ndraw(anglemark((5,0),(-5,0),X,18));\ndraw(anglemark(X,5*dir(50),(-5,0),18));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(-5,0),W); label(\"$D$\",(5,0),E); label(\"$C$\",(5*dir(50)),E);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(X),N);\n\npair L = foot(X,(-5,0),5*dir(50));\ndraw(X--L);\ndraw(rightanglemark(X,L,(-5,0),14)); draw(rightanglemark(X,L,(5*dir(50)),14));\nlabel(\"$E$\",L,SE);\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "28", "pre_generated_steps": ["I see a trapezoid with a right triangle inside it.", "I know that the area of the trapezoid is the average of the bases times the height, and the area of the triangle is half the base times the height.", "I don't have enough information to find the height or the bases, so I need to use the given angle conditions to find some relationships.", "I notice that since $\\overline{AC}$ bisects $\\angle BAD$, then $\\angle DAC = \\angle BAC$.", "That means $\\triangle ACD$ and $\\triangle ABC$ are similar, since they have a right angle and another congruent angle.", "Since the triangles are similar, their corresponding sides are proportional.", "So, $\\frac{AD}{BC} = \\frac{CD}{AB} = \\frac{AC}{AC} = 1$.", "That means $AD = BC$ and $CD = AB$.", "So, the trapezoid is actually an isosceles trapezoid, and the triangle is half of it.", "Therefore, the area of the triangle is half the area of the trapezoid, which is given as 42.", "So, $[\\triangle ACD] = \\frac{42}{2} = 21$.", "# Answer\n\n21"], "pre_generated_answer": "21", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00628848109692008}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I see a trapezoid with a right triangle inside it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the area of the trapezoid is the average of the bases times the height, and the area of the triangle is half the base times the height.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I don't have enough information to find the height or the bases, so I need to use the given angle conditions to find some relationships.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that since $\\overline{AC}$ bisects $\\angle BAD$, then $\\angle DAC = \\angle BAC$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That means $\\triangle ACD$ and $\\triangle ABC$ are similar, since they have a right angle and another congruent angle.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I also notice that since $\\overline{AC}\\perp\\overline{CD}$, then $\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ - \\angle DAC$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also notice that since $\\overline{AD}||\\overline{BC}$, then the alternate interior angles $\\angle CAD$ and $\\angle ACB$ are congruent.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also notice that since $\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}$, then $\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ - \\angle DAC$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also notice that since $\\overline{AD}|| \\overline{BC}$, then $\\angle DCA = \\angle CBA$ by alternate interior angles.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1165269, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5b158ac4-13d1-40db-8987-3856a5926c39", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:23:35.702660", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Solve\n\\[\\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 6.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} + 2 \\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36.\\]Then\n\\[2x + 2 \\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 36,\\]so\n\\[\\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 18 - x.\\]Squaring both sides, we get $x^2 - 3x - 6 = 324 - 36x + x^2.$  Hence, $x = \\boxed{10}.$  We check that this solution works.", "ground_truth_answer": "10", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a tricky equation with nested square roots.", "One possible strategy is to try to isolate one of the square roots and square both sides to eliminate it.", "Let's start with the leftmost square root and move the other one to the right side.", "Then we get\n\\[\\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 6 - \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}}.\\]", "Now we can square both sides and simplify.", "Remember to use the FOIL method for the right side, and be careful with the signs.", "Squaring both sides gives us\n\\[x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36 - 12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}.\\]", "Simplifying, we get\n\\[2\\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36 - 12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}}.\\]", "We still have two square roots, but we can repeat the same strategy and isolate one of them.", "Let's move the left square root to the right and the right one to the left.", "Then we get\n\\[12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 36 - 2\\sqrt{3x + 6}.\\]", "Squaring both sides again and simplifying, we get\n\\[144x - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} = 1296 - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} - 12x + 36.\\]", "Simplifying further, we get\n\\[132x - 216 = 0.\\]", "Solving for x, we get\n\\[x = \\frac{216}{132} = \\frac{18}{11}.\\]", "We have found a possible value for x, but we need to check if it satisfies the original equation.", "Sometimes, squaring both sides can introduce extraneous solutions that don't work.", "Let's plug in x = 18/11 and see if the equation is true.", "The left side of the equation is\n\\[\\sqrt{\\frac{18}{11} + \\sqrt{\\frac{54}{11} + 6}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{18}{11} - \\sqrt{\\frac{54}{11} + 6}}.\\]", "Simplifying, we get\n\\[\\sqrt{\\frac{98}{11}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{-62}{11}}.\\]", "Uh oh, we have a problem.", "The second square root is not defined, since we can't take the square root of a negative number.", "This means that x = 18/11 is not a valid solution to the equation.", "In fact, there is no real solution to this equation, since no matter what x we choose, one of the square roots will be negative.", "This equation is a trick question, and the answer is that there is no solution.", "# Answer\n\nNo solution"], "pre_generated_answer": "No solution", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.56317774356984e-12}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a tricky equation with nested square roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One possible strategy is to try to isolate one of the square roots and square both sides to eliminate it.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Let's start with the leftmost square root and move the other one to the right side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then we get\n\\[\\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 6 - \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now we can square both sides and simplify.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Remember to use the FOIL method for the right side, and be careful with the signs.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides gives us\n\\[x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36 - 12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying, we get\n\\[2\\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36 - 12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "We still have two square roots, but we can repeat the same strategy and isolate one of them.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Let's move the left square root to the right and the right one to the left.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then we get\n\\[12\\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 36 - 2\\sqrt{3x + 6}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides again and simplifying, we get\n\\[144x - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} = 1296 - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} - 12x + 36.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Squaring both sides again, we get\n\\[144x - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} = 1296 - 144\\sqrt{3x + 6} - 4(3x + 6).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Again, we can square both sides and simplify.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "We can square both sides again and simplify.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 326859, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
