\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{15 + \\sqrt{3(15) + 6}} + \\sqrt{15 - \\sqrt{3(15) + 6}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\sqrt{15 + 3\\sqrt{11}} + \\sqrt{15 - 3\\sqrt{11}} &= 6 \\\\\n3 + 3 &= 6 \\\\\n6 &= 6\n\\end{align*}\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So x = 15 is a valid solution.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "What about x = -22?", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{-22 + \\sqrt{3(-22) + 6}} + \\sqrt{-22 - \\sqrt{3(-22) + 6}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\sqrt{-22 + \\sqrt{-60}} + \\sqrt{-22 - \\sqrt{-60}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\sqrt{-22 + i\\sqrt{60}} + \\sqrt{-22 - i\\sqrt{60}} &= 6 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is not a real solution, because the square roots are complex numbers.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So x = -22 is an extraneous solution, and I can discard it.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, the only real solution to the equation is x = 15.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n15", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 214305, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "2ebca76d-77fc-4678-8ece-ca65e2a2eb68", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T18:21:13.812471", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Solve\n\\[\\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} = 6.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} + 2 \\sqrt{x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}} \\sqrt{x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}} + x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} = 36.\\]Then\n\\[2x + 2 \\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 36,\\]so\n\\[\\sqrt{x^2 - 3x - 6} = 18 - x.\\]Squaring both sides, we get $x^2 - 3x - 6 = 324 - 36x + x^2.$  Hence, $x = \\boxed{10}.$  We check that this solution works.", "ground_truth_answer": "10", "pre_generated_steps": ["This equation looks complicated, but maybe I can simplify it by squaring both sides.", "That will get rid of the square roots on the left side.", "But before I do that, I need to be careful about the domain of the equation.", "Since I can't take the square root of a negative number, I need to make sure that both x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} and x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} are nonnegative.", "For the first expression, x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} to be nonnegative, I need x to be at least -\\sqrt{3x + 6}.", "Squaring both sides of this inequality, I get x^2 + 6 \\geq 3x + 6, which simplifies to x^2 - 3x \\geq 0.", "Factoring, I get x(x - 3) \\geq 0.", "This means that either x \\geq 3 or x \\leq 0.", "For the second expression, x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} to be nonnegative, I need x to be at least \\sqrt{3x + 6}.", "Squaring both sides of this inequality, I get x^2 + 6 \\geq 3x + 6, which is the same as before.", "So I still have the same domain restriction: either x \\geq 3 or x \\leq 0.", "Now that I have checked the domain, I can square both sides of the equation and see what happens.", "Squaring the left side, I get (x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}) + (x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}) + 2\\sqrt{(x + \\sqrt{3x + 6})(x - \\sqrt{3x + 6})}.", "Squaring the right side, I get 36.", "Simplifying the left side, I get 2x + 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)}.", "So the equation becomes 2x + 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 36.", "Subtracting 2x from both sides, I get 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 36 - 2x.", "Dividing by 2, I get \\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 18 - x.", "Squaring both sides again, I get x^2 - (3x + 6) = (18 - x)^2.", "Expanding the right side, I get x^2 - (3x + 6) = 324 - 36x + x^2.", "Simplifying, I get 33x = 330.", "Dividing by 33, I get x = 10.", "But wait, I can't just assume that this is the only solution.", "I need to check if it satisfies the original equation and the domain restrictions.", "Plugging in x = 10, I get \\sqrt{10 + \\sqrt{36}} + \\sqrt{10 - \\sqrt{36}} = 6, which is true.", "So x = 10 is a solution.", "But does it satisfy the domain restrictions?", "I need x to be either at least 3 or at most 0.", "Well, 10 is at least 3, so that's fine.", "So x = 10 is the only solution that works.", "# Answer\n\n10"], "pre_generated_answer": "10", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0003787211910364051}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This equation looks complicated, but maybe I can simplify it by squaring both sides.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That will get rid of the square roots on the left side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But before I do that, I need to be careful about the domain of the equation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since I can't take the square root of a negative number, I need to make sure that both x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} and x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} are nonnegative.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the first expression, x + \\sqrt{3x + 6} to be nonnegative, I need x to be at least -\\sqrt{3x + 6}.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides of this inequality, I get x^2 + 6 \\geq 3x + 6, which simplifies to x^2 - 3x \\geq 0.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Factoring, I get x(x - 3) \\geq 0.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that either x \\geq 3 or x \\leq 0.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the second expression, x - \\sqrt{3x + 6} to be nonnegative, I need x to be at least \\sqrt{3x + 6}.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides of this inequality, I get x^2 + 6 \\geq 3x + 6, which is the same as before.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I still have the same domain restriction: either x \\geq 3 or x \\leq 0.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now that I have checked the domain, I can square both sides of the equation and see what happens.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring the left side, I get (x + \\sqrt{3x + 6}) + (x - \\sqrt{3x + 6}) + 2\\sqrt{(x + \\sqrt{3x + 6})(x - \\sqrt{3x + 6})}.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring the right side, I get 36.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying the left side, I get 2x + 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)}.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the equation becomes 2x + 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 36.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Subtracting 2x from both sides, I get 2\\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 36 - 2x.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Dividing by 2, I get \\sqrt{x^2 - (3x + 6)} = 18 - x.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides again, I get x^2 - (3x + 6) = (18 - x)^2.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Expanding the right side, I get x^2 - (3x + 6) = 324 - 36x + x^2.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying, I get 33x = 330.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Dividing by 33, I get x = 10.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But wait, I can't just assume that this is the only solution.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I need to check if it satisfies the original equation and the domain restrictions.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in x = 10, I get \\sqrt{10 + \\sqrt{36}} + \\sqrt{10 - \\sqrt{36}} = 6, which is true.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So x = 10 is a solution.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But does it satisfy the domain restrictions?", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I need x to be either at least 3 or at most 0.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Well, 10 is at least 3, so that's fine.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So x = 10 is the only solution that works.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n10", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 152944, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "f8eeff50-2b57-4d80-a977-e0f8af62018e", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T20:45:30.240851", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f$ be defined by  \\[f(x) = \\left\\{\n\\begin{array}{cl}\n3-x & \\text{ if } x \\leq 3, \\\\\n-x^3+2x^2+3x & \\text{ if } x>3.\n\\end{array}\n\\right.\\]Calculate $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "The number $f^{-1}(0)$ is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 0$.  Since the function $f$ is defined piecewise, to find this value, we must consider both cases $x \\le 3$ and $x > 3$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $3 - x = 0$, which leads to $x = 3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 0$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 0$.  This equation factors as $-x(x - 3)(x + 1) = 0$, so $x = 0$, $x = 3$, or $x = -1$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = 3$, which means $f^{-1}(0) = 3$.\n\nNow we compute $f^{-1}(6)$, which is the value of $x$ such that $f(x) = 6$.\n\nIf $x \\le 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $3 - x = 6$, which leads to $x = -3$.  Note that this value satisfies the condition $x \\le 3$.  If $x > 3$ and $f(x) = 6$, then $-x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x = 6$, or $x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x + 6 = 0$.  This equation factors as $(x - 2)(x^2 - 3) = 0$, so $x = 2$, $x = \\sqrt{3}$, or $x = -\\sqrt{3}$.  But none of these values satisfies $x > 3$, so the solution is $x = -3$, which means $f^{-1}(6) = -3$.\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6) = 3 + (-3) = \\boxed{0}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(3mm);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));\nimport graph;\n\ndraw((-20,0)--(20,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-20)--(0,20),Arrows(4));\n\nreal f(real x) {return 3-x;}\nreal g(real x) {return -x^3+2x^2+3x;}\n\nreal x;\n\ndraw(graph(f,-15,3),BeginArrow(4));\ndraw(graph(g,3,4),EndArrow(4));\n\nreal eps = 0.2;\n\ndraw((-eps,3)--(eps,3));\ndraw((-eps,0)--(eps,0));\ndraw((-eps,-3)--(eps,-3));\n\ndot(\"$(-3,6)$\",(-3,6),SW);\ndot(\"$(3,0)$\",(3,0),NE);\n\nlabel(\"$f(x)$\",(3,20.5));\nlabel(\"$x$\",(20.5,-1));\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "0", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the inverse function of $f$, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "For the first piece of $f$, I have $y=3-x$.", "Switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x=3-y$, and solving for $y$ gives me $y=3-x$.", "So the inverse of the first piece is also $y=3-x$.", "For the second piece of $f$, I have $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "Switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "Solving for $y$ in terms of $x$ is more complicated here.", "I can try to use the rational root theorem to find a possible factor of the cubic polynomial on the right side.", "The rational root theorem says that if the polynomial has a rational root, it must be of the form $\\pm p/q$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term (in this case, $x$) and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient (in this case, -1).", "So the possible rational roots are $\\pm x$.", "I can try plugging in $y=x$ into the equation and see if it works.", "If it does, then $(y-x)$ is a factor of the polynomial.", "I get $x=-x^3+2x^2+3x$, which simplifies to $x^3-2x^2-2x=0$.", "This is not zero unless $x=0$, so $y=x$ is not a root.", "I can try plugging in $y=-x$ into the equation and see if it works.", "If it does, then $(y+x)$ is a factor of the polynomial.", "I get $x=-(-x)^3+2(-x)^2+3(-x)$, which simplifies to $x+x^3-2x^2-3x=0$.", "This simplifies further to $x^3-2x^2-2x=0$.", "This is the same as before, so $y=-x$ is not a root either.", "This means that the polynomial has no rational roots, so I cannot easily factor it.", "I can try to use a numerical method, such as the Newton-Raphson method, to approximate a root.", "Alternatively, I can use a graphing calculator or software to graph the equation and see where it intersects the $x$-axis.", "I will use Desmos, an online graphing tool, to plot the equation $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "I see that it has three real roots, one at $y\\approx -1.7549$, one at $y\\approx 0$, and one at $y\\approx 1.0882$.", "Since the inverse function of $f$ is the reflection of $f$ across the line $y=x$, I can also plot $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$ and see where it intersects the line $y=x$.", "I see that it has two points of intersection, one at $(0,0)$ and one at $(1.0882,1.0882)$.", "These are the values of $f^{-1}(0)$ and $f^{-1}(6)$, respectively.", "Therefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)=0+1.0882=1.0882$.", "# Answer\n\n1.0882"], "pre_generated_answer": "1.0882", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0006799446606663244}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the inverse function of $f$, I need to switch the roles of $x$ and $y$ and solve for $y$ in terms of $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the first piece of $f$, I have $y=3-x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x=3-y$, and solving for $y$ gives me $y=3-x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the inverse of the first piece is also $y=3-x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For the second piece of $f$, I have $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Switching $x$ and $y$ gives me $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Solving for $y$ in terms of $x$ is more complicated here.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can try to use the rational root theorem to find a possible factor of the cubic polynomial on the right side.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The rational root theorem says that if the polynomial has a rational root, it must be of the form $\\pm p/q$, where $p$ is a factor of the constant term (in this case, $x$) and $q$ is a factor of the leading coefficient (in this case, -1).", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the possible rational roots are $\\pm x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can try plugging in $y=x$ into the equation and see if it works.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If it does, then $(y-x)$ is a factor of the polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $x=-x^3+2x^2+3x$, which simplifies to $x^3-2x^2-2x=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is not zero unless $x=0$, so $y=x$ is not a root.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can try plugging in $y=-x$ into the equation and see if it works.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If it does, then $(y+x)$ is a factor of the polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $x=-(-x)^3+2(-x)^2+3(-x)$, which simplifies to $x+x^3-2x^2-3x=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This simplifies further to $x^3-2x^2-2x=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is the same as before, so $y=-x$ is not a root either.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the polynomial has no rational roots, so I cannot easily factor it.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can try to use a numerical method, such as the Newton-Raphson method, to approximate a root.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Alternatively, I can use a graphing calculator or software to graph the equation and see where it intersects the $x$-axis.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will use Desmos, an online graphing tool, to plot the equation $x=-y^3+2y^2+3y$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I see that it has three real roots, one at $y\\approx -1.7549$, one at $y\\approx 0$, and one at $y\\approx 1.0882$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since the inverse function of $f$ is the reflection of $f$ across the line $y=x$, I can also plot $y=-x^3+2x^2+3x$ and see where it intersects the line $y=x$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I see that it has two points of intersection, one at $(0,0)$ and one at $(1.0882,1.0882)$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "These are the values of $f^{-1}(0)$ and $f^{-1}(6)$, respectively.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, $f^{-1}(0)+f^{-1}(6)=0+1.0882=1.0882$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n1.0882", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 864075, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "7af970f3-c874-4e6e-8be8-864b8e9ed7f6", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:29:42.401390", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Define\n\\[p = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}.\\]Find a way to write\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3}\\]in terms of $p$ and $q.$", "ground_truth_solution": "We count the number of times $\\frac{1}{n^3}$ appears in the sum\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3},\\]where $n$ is a fixed positive integer.  (In other words, we are conditioning the sum on $j + k$.)  We get a term of $\\frac{1}{n^3}$ each time $j + k = n.$  The pairs $(j,k)$ that work are $(1,n - 1),$ $(2,n - 2),$ $\\dots,$ $(n - 1,1),$ for a total of $n - 1$ pairs.  Therefore,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} &= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{n}{n^3} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{p - q}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "p - q", "pre_generated_steps": ["I recognize that $p$ is a well-known sum, the value of which is $\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$ by Euler's formula.", "I don't know the value of $q$, but I suspect it might be related to $p$ in some way.", "I also notice that the inner sum of the double sum can be written as\n\\[\\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{k = j + 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}.\\]", "This suggests that I can use a telescoping trick to simplify the double sum.", "I can write\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3} - \\sum_{k = j + 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3} \\right).\\]", "The second term inside the parentheses is the same as the first term, except with the first $j$ terms missing.", "So, I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( q - \\sum_{k = 1}^j \\frac{1}{k^3} \\right).\\]", "Now, I see that the term inside the parentheses is actually a partial sum of the series that defines $q$.", "So, I can use the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of an infinite series, which is\n\\[\\sum_{k = 1}^n a_k = S_n = S - a_{n + 1} - a_{n + 2} - \\cdots,\\]\nwhere $S$ is the value of the infinite series and $a_k$ are the terms.", "In this case, $S = q$ and $a_k = \\frac{1}{k^3}$.", "So, I get\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\left( q - q + \\frac{1}{(j + 1)^3} + \\frac{1}{(j + 2)^3} + \\cdots \\right).\\]", "Now, I can simplify this further by observing that the terms inside the parentheses form a telescoping series themselves.", "For each $j$, I have a term $\\frac{1}{(j + 1)^3}$ that cancels out with the term $\\frac{1}{(j + 2)^3}$ in the next parenthesis.", "So, all the terms except the first and the last cancel out, and I get\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{(\\infty + 1)^3}.\\]", "The last term is zero, so I end up with\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\frac{1}{8}.\\]", "Therefore, I have found a way to write the double sum in terms of $p$ and $q$, by using the fact that $p = \\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$ and $q$ is the value of the infinite series.", "The final answer is\n\n# Answer\n\n1/8"], "pre_generated_answer": "1/8", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 3.0327198882978645e-13}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I recognize that $p$ is a well-known sum, the value of which is $\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}$ by Euler's formula.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This problem looks pretty hard, but maybe there is a clever way to simplify it.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that this is a double sum, where we're adding up the same expression for different values of $j$ and $k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem looks scary, but I'm not going to panic. I'm going to look for patterns, symmetries, and ways to simplify the expression.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This problem involves sums of infinite series of reciprocals of perfect squares and cubes.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 342991, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "7cf8eaf6-f935-4e91-baa8-820465d13d27", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T15:00:28.500589", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Simplify the following expression to a simplified fraction: $$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt5}}.$$", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we'll divide $\\sqrt{5}$ into each term in the numerator of the fraction inside the big radical: $$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt5}}=\n\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{80}\\cdot\\sqrt{5}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{845}}{9\\sqrt{5}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}.\n$$Let's attack each fraction within the square root separately. First, $$\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}\\cdot\\sqrt5}=\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{400}}=\\dfrac{5}{20}=\\dfrac{1}{4}.$$The second one is trickier: $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9\\sqrt5}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{169}}{9}=\\dfrac{13}{9}.$$Finally, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt5}=\\sqrt9=3$. Adding these together, we get $$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{4}+\\dfrac{13}{9}+3}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9+52+108}{36}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{169}{36}}=\\boxed{\\frac{13}{6}}.$$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{13}{6}", "pre_generated_steps": ["To simplify a fraction, I want to eliminate any radicals in the denominator.", "In this case, the denominator is $\\sqrt5$, which I can multiply by $\\sqrt5$ to get 5, a rational number.", "To keep the fraction equivalent, I also need to multiply the numerator by $\\sqrt5$.", "So I have $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}\\right)}{5}.$$", "Now I can simplify the numerator by distributing the $\\sqrt5$ and simplifying the radicals.", "I notice that 80, 845, and 45 are all divisible by 5, so I can use the property $\\sqrt{ab}=\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}$ to factor out a $\\sqrt5$ from each radical.", "I get $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5\\sqrt5}{\\sqrt{16}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{169}}{9}+\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{9}\\right)}{5}.$$", "Next, I can simplify the radicals that are perfect squares, such as $\\sqrt{16}$, $\\sqrt{169}$, and $\\sqrt{9}$.", "I get $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5\\sqrt5}{4}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 13}{9}+3\\sqrt{5}\\right)}{5}.$$", "Now I can cancel out the common factor of $\\sqrt5$ in the numerator and denominator, and I get $$\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{4}+\\dfrac{13}{9}+3}{1}.$$", "This is a sum of three fractions with different denominators, so I need to find a common denominator to add them.", "The least common multiple of 4, 9, and 1 is 36, so I multiply each fraction by an appropriate factor to get the same denominator.", "I get $$\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{4}\\cdot \\dfrac{9}{9}+\\dfrac{13}{9}\\cdot \\dfrac{4}{4}+\\dfrac{3}{1}\\cdot \\dfrac{36}{36}}{1}.$$", "This simplifies to $$\\dfrac{\\dfrac{45}{36}+\\dfrac{52}{36}+\\dfrac{108}{36}}{1}.$$", "Now I can add the numerators and keep the denominator.", "I get $$\\dfrac{205}{36}.$$", "This is the simplest form of the fraction, since 205 and 36 have no common factors other than 1.", "# Answer\n\n\\dfrac{205}{36}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\dfrac{205}{36}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.00537657970752168}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To simplify a fraction, I want to eliminate any radicals in the denominator.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In this case, the denominator is $\\sqrt5$, which I can multiply by $\\sqrt5$ to get 5, a rational number.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To keep the fraction equivalent, I also need to multiply the numerator by $\\sqrt5$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I have $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}\\right)}{5}.$$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This gives me $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}\\right)}{5}.$$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This gives me $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt5\\left(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}\\right)}{5}$$ as the new fraction.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So the new fraction is $$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}\\left(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}\\right)}{5}.$$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1282672, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "7af970f3-c874-4e6e-8be8-864b8e9ed7f6", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:24:57.278191", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the degree measure of the least positive angle $\\theta$ for which\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}{\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "From the angle addition formula, the numerator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&(\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) + (\\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ -  \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}From the sum-to-product formula, $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ = 2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ.$\n\nSimilarly, the denominator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= (\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ +  \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) - (\\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ \\\\\n&= -2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}so the expression is equal to\n\\[\\frac{2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ}{-2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ} = -\\frac{\\cos 30^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = -\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 120^\\circ.\\]Hence, the smallest such $\\theta$ is $\\boxed{120^\\circ}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "120^\\circ", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the numerator and denominator of the fraction are both sums and differences of products of trigonometric functions, so I wonder if there is a way to simplify them using some identities.", "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos (A+B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$, and another one that says $\\sin (A+B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B$.", "I try to apply these identities to the numerator and denominator by looking for pairs of angles that add up to some other angle.", "For example, I see that $5^\\circ + 20^\\circ = 25^\\circ$ and $35^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 85^\\circ$, so I can write\n\\[\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\\]\n\\[= \\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ\\]\nand\n\\[\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\\]\n\\[= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ.\\]", "Now the fraction becomes\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}.\\]", "I notice that the numerator and denominator are still sums and differences of trigonometric functions, so I wonder if there is another identity that can simplify them further.", "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos A - \\cos B = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$, and another one that says $\\sin A - \\sin B = 2 \\cos \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$.", "I try to apply these identities to the numerator and denominator by using the averages and differences of the angles.", "For example, I see that $\\frac{25^\\circ + 85^\\circ}{2} = 55^\\circ$ and $\\frac{25^\\circ - 85^\\circ}{2} = -30^\\circ$, so I can write\n\\[\\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ = -2 \\sin 55^\\circ \\sin (-30^\\circ)\\]\nand\n\\[\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ = 2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\sin (-30^\\circ).\\]", "Now the fraction becomes\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{-2 \\sin 55^\\circ \\sin (-30^\\circ)}{2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\sin (-30^\\circ)}.\\]", "I notice that the factors of 2 and $\\sin (-30^\\circ)$ cancel out, leaving\n\\[\\tan \\theta = -\\frac{\\sin 55^\\circ}{\\cos 55^\\circ}.\\]", "I recognize this as the negative of the tangent of the angle $55^\\circ$, so I can write\n\\[\\tan \\theta = -\\tan 55^\\circ.\\]", "I know that the tangent function is periodic with a period of $180^\\circ$, so the equation $\\tan \\theta = -\\tan 55^\\circ$ has infinitely many solutions of the form $\\theta = -55^\\circ + 180^\\circ k$, where $k$ is any integer.", "However, I am looking for the least positive angle $\\theta$, so I want to find the smallest value of $k$ that makes $\\theta$ positive.", "I see that if $k = 0$, then $\\theta = -55^\\circ$, which is negative.", "But if $k = 1$, then $\\theta = 125^\\circ$, which is positive and less than $180^\\circ$.", "Therefore, the least positive angle $\\theta$ for which $\\tan \\theta = -\\tan 55^\\circ$ is $\\theta = 125^\\circ$.", "# Answer\n\n125"], "pre_generated_answer": "125", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.010995253217487327}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the numerator and denominator of the fraction are both sums and differences of products of trigonometric functions, so I wonder if there is a way to simplify them using some identities.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos (A+B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$, and another one that says $\\sin (A+B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I try to apply these identities to the numerator and denominator by looking for pairs of angles that add up to some other angle.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, I see that $5^\\circ + 20^\\circ = 25^\\circ$ and $35^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 85^\\circ$, so I can write\n\\[\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\\]\n\\[= \\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ\\]\nand\n\\[\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\\]\n\\[= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now the fraction becomes\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the numerator and denominator are still sums and differences of trigonometric functions, so I wonder if there is another identity that can simplify them further.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos A - \\cos B = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$, and another one that says $\\sin A - \\sin B = 2 \\cos \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos A - \\cos B = -2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$, and another one that says $\\sin A - \\sin B = 2 \\cos \\left(\\frac{A+B}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{A-B}{2}\\right)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos A - \\cos B = -2 \\sin \\frac{A+B}{2} \\sin \\frac{A-B}{2}$, and another one that says $\\sin A - \\sin B = 2 \\cos \\frac{A+B}{2} \\sin \\frac{A-B}{2}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B) = -2 \\sin A \\sin B$, and another one that says $\\sin (A - B) - \\sin (A + B) = -2 \\cos A \\sin B$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I recall that there is an identity that says $\\cos (A-B) - \\cos (A+B) = -2 \\sin A \\sin B$, and another one that says $\\sin (A-B) - \\sin (A+B) = -2 \\cos A \\sin B$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 625816, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "15751e52-7857-4e3b-89d7-1122b7973859", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:24:37.199828", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the degree measure of the least positive angle $\\theta$ for which\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}{\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "From the angle addition formula, the numerator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&(\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) + (\\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ -  \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}From the sum-to-product formula, $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ = 2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ.$\n\nSimilarly, the denominator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= (\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ +  \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) - (\\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ \\\\\n&= -2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}so the expression is equal to\n\\[\\frac{2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ}{-2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ} = -\\frac{\\cos 30^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = -\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 120^\\circ.\\]Hence, the smallest such $\\theta$ is $\\boxed{120^\\circ}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "120^\\circ", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks complicated, but I notice that it involves a lot of trigonometric identities, so I wonder if I can simplify it somehow.", "I recall that $\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$ and $\\sin(A + B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B$, so maybe I can use these to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the fraction.", "In fact, I see that the numerator is just $\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ = \\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ$.", "Similarly, the denominator is just $\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ = \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$.", "So the fraction simplifies to $\\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}$, which looks much nicer.", "Now I want to find an angle $\\theta$ such that $\\tan \\theta$ equals this fraction.", "I know that $\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}$, so I need to solve the equation $\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} = \\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}$.", "I can cross-multiply and get $\\sin \\theta (\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ) = \\cos \\theta (\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ)$.", "I also recall that $\\cos 85^\\circ = - \\sin 5^\\circ$ and $\\sin 85^\\circ = \\cos 5^\\circ$, so I can substitute these and get $\\sin \\theta (\\sin 25^\\circ - \\cos 5^\\circ) = \\cos \\theta (\\cos 25^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ)$.", "I can divide both sides by $\\cos \\theta$ and get $\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\cos 5^\\circ}$, which looks familiar.", "I remember that this is the tangent of the difference of two angles, namely $\\tan(A - B) = \\frac{\\tan A - \\tan B}{1 + \\tan A \\tan B}$, so I can try to find two angles whose tangent equals the numerator and denominator respectively.", "I see that $\\tan 25^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 25^\\circ}{\\cos 25^\\circ}$ and $\\tan 5^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 5^\\circ}{\\cos 5^\\circ}$, so these are good candidates for $A$ and $B$.", "So I can write $\\tan \\theta = \\tan(25^\\circ - 5^\\circ) = \\tan 20^\\circ$, which means that $\\theta = 20^\\circ + k \\pi$, where $k$ is any integer.", "The problem asks for the least positive angle, so I need to choose the smallest positive value of $k$ that makes $\\theta$ positive.", "Clearly, $k = 0$ works, so the answer is $\\theta = 20^\\circ$.", "# Answer\n\n20^\\circ"], "pre_generated_answer": "20^\\circ", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 2.0087402516537444e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks complicated, but I notice that it involves a lot of trigonometric identities, so I wonder if I can simplify it somehow.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that $\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B$ and $\\sin(A + B) = \\sin A \\cos B + \\cos A \\sin B$, so maybe I can use these to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the fraction.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In fact, I see that the numerator is just $\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ = \\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, the denominator is just $\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ = \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the fraction simplifies to $\\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}$, which looks much nicer.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now I want to find an angle $\\theta$ such that $\\tan \\theta$ equals this fraction.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that $\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}$, so I need to solve the equation $\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} = \\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can cross-multiply and get $\\sin \\theta (\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ) = \\cos \\theta (\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also recall that $\\cos 85^\\circ = - \\sin 5^\\circ$ and $\\sin 85^\\circ = \\cos 5^\\circ$, so I can substitute these and get $\\sin \\theta (\\sin 25^\\circ - \\cos 5^\\circ) = \\cos \\theta (\\cos 25^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I can also use the identity $\\sin(90^\\circ - x) = \\cos x$ to rewrite $\\sin 85^\\circ = \\cos 5^\\circ$ and $\\cos 85^\\circ = \\sin 5^\\circ$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I can move everything to one side and get $\\sin \\theta (\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ) - \\cos \\theta (\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ) = 0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that both sides of this equation have the same sign, since $\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$ and $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ$ are both negative (because 25 and 85 are in the second quadrant).", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I want to isolate $\\theta$ somehow, so I look for a way to write both sides in terms of $\\sin \\theta$ or $\\cos \\theta$ only.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 539319, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "215edb5c-9e68-4edb-8702-5a23153c45b5", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:20:12.119903", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the degree measure of the least positive angle $\\theta$ for which\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}{\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "From the angle addition formula, the numerator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&(\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) + (\\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ -  \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}From the sum-to-product formula, $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ = 2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ.$\n\nSimilarly, the denominator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= (\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ +  \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) - (\\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ \\\\\n&= -2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}so the expression is equal to\n\\[\\frac{2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ}{-2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ} = -\\frac{\\cos 30^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = -\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 120^\\circ.\\]Hence, the smallest such $\\theta$ is $\\boxed{120^\\circ}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "120^\\circ", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a complicated expression, but I notice that it has a lot of trigonometric identities that might simplify things.", "For example, I know that $\\cos(a+b) = \\cos a \\cos b - \\sin a \\sin b$ and $\\sin(a+b) = \\sin a \\cos b + \\cos a \\sin b$, so I can use these to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the fraction.", "The numerator becomes $\\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$.", "The denominator becomes $\\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) + \\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ + \\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ$.", "Now I see that the numerator and denominator have the same terms, but with opposite signs.", "This means that the fraction is equal to $-1$.", "Therefore, $\\tan \\theta = -1$.", "I know that this happens when $\\theta$ is an odd multiple of $45^\\circ$, so the least positive angle that satisfies this is $\\theta = 135^\\circ$.", "# Answer\n\n135^\\circ"], "pre_generated_answer": "135^\\circ", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.01317212057777413}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a complicated expression, but I notice that it has a lot of trigonometric identities that might simplify things.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, I know that $\\cos(a+b) = \\cos a \\cos b - \\sin a \\sin b$ and $\\sin(a+b) = \\sin a \\cos b + \\cos a \\sin b$, so I can use these to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the fraction.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The numerator becomes $\\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "The numerator becomes $\\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "In the numerator, I see that the terms $\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ$ and $- \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ$ both have the form of $\\cos(a+b)$ with different values of $a$ and $b$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The numerator becomes $\\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\cos(5^\\circ -20^\\circ) - \\cos(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ)$ and the denominator becomes $\\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) + \\sin(5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The numerator becomes $\\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\cos (5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ)$, which is $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\cos(-15^\\circ) - \\cos(-15^\\circ)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 420308, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:17:48.453574", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the degree measure of the least positive angle $\\theta$ for which\n\\[\\tan \\theta = \\frac{\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}{\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "From the angle addition formula, the numerator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&(\\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) + (\\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ -  \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}From the sum-to-product formula, $\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ = 2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ.$\n\nSimilarly, the denominator is\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= (\\sin 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ +  \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) - (\\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ \\\\\n&= -2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ,\n\\end{align*}so the expression is equal to\n\\[\\frac{2 \\cos 55^\\circ \\cos 30^\\circ}{-2 \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 55^\\circ} = -\\frac{\\cos 30^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = -\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 120^\\circ.\\]Hence, the smallest such $\\theta$ is $\\boxed{120^\\circ}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "120^\\circ", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a complicated expression for the tangent of $\\theta$, but I notice that it has a lot of trigonometric identities involving sums and differences of angles.", "Maybe I can simplify it by using those identities.", "Recall that for any angles $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we have\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) &= \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) &= \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) &= \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\sin(\\alpha - \\beta) &= \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta.", "\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\\]", "I can use these identities to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the given expression as follows:\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Numerator} &= \\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ, \\\\\n\\text{Denominator} &= \\sin(5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ) + \\cos(5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin(-15^\\circ) - \\sin(-15^\\circ) + \\cos(-15^\\circ) - \\cos(-15^\\circ) \\\\\n&= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\\]", "Oh, wait a minute.", "The denominator is zero.", "That means the expression is undefined, and there is no angle $\\theta$ that satisfies the equation.", "This problem has no solution!\n\n# Answer\n\nNo solution"], "pre_generated_answer": "No solution", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0029578284240919907}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a complicated expression for the tangent of $\\theta$, but I notice that it has a lot of trigonometric identities involving sums and differences of angles.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Maybe I can simplify it by using those identities.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Recall that for any angles $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we have\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) &= \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) &= \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) &= \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta + \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta, \\\\\n\\sin(\\alpha - \\beta) &= \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\beta.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "\\\\\n\\end{aligned}\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can use these identities to rewrite the numerator and denominator of the given expression as follows:\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Numerator} &= \\cos(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\sin(5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ, \\\\\n\\text{Denominator} &= \\sin(5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\sin(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ) + \\cos(5^\\circ - 20^\\circ) - \\cos(35^\\circ - 50^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\sin(-15^\\circ) - \\sin(-15^\\circ) + \\cos(-15^\\circ) - \\cos(-15^\\circ) \\\\\n&= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Oh, wait a minute.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The denominator is zero.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That means the expression is undefined, and there is no angle $\\theta$ that satisfies the equation.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This problem has no solution!\n\n# Answer\n\nNo solution", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion":