{"labeler": "6c655923-0179-4f96-afbc-f52af3de908d", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T05:10:03.042794", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Consider the function $z(x,y)$ describing the paraboloid\n\\[z = (2x - y)^2 - 2y^2 - 3y.\\]Archimedes and Brahmagupta are playing a game.  Archimedes first chooses $x.$  Afterwards, Brahmagupta chooses $y.$  Archimedes wishes to minimize $z$ while Brahmagupta wishes to maximize $z.$  Assuming that Brahmagupta will play optimally, what value of $x$ should Archimedes choose?", "ground_truth_solution": "Expanding $z,$ we get\n\\begin{align*}\nz &= 4x^2 - 4xy + y^2 - 2y^2 - 3y \\\\\n&= -y^2 - (4x + 3) y + 4x^2.\n\\end{align*}After Archimedes chooses $x,$ Brahmagupta will choose\n\\[y = -\\frac{4x + 3}{2}\\]in order to maximize $z.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nz &= -\\left( -\\frac{4x + 3}{2} \\right)^2 - (4x + 3) \\left( -\\frac{4x + 3}{2} \\right)^2 + 4x^2 \\\\\n&= 8x^2 + 6x + \\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{align*}To minimize this expression, Archimedes should choose $x = -\\frac{6}{16} = \\boxed{-\\frac{3}{8}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "-\\frac{3}{8}", "pre_generated_steps": ["Since Archimedes chooses $x$ first, he should try to make $z$ as insensitive to $y$ as possible.", "That is, he should try to eliminate the dependence of $z$ on $y$ or make it very weak.", "One way to do this is to make the partial derivative of $z$ with respect to $y$ zero, or very small.", "That is, he should try to find an $x$ such that\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 0 \\text{ or close to } 0.\\]", "To find the partial derivative of $z$ with respect to $y$, I use the power rule and the chain rule.", "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 2(2x-y)(-1) - 4y - 3 = -4x + 4y - 4y - 3 = -4x - 3.\\]", "This partial derivative does not depend on $y$, so it cannot be zero for any $y$.", "However, it can be close to zero if $x$ is close to $-3/4$.", "In fact, the closer $x$ is to $-3/4$, the smaller the absolute value of the partial derivative is, and the less $z$ changes with respect to $y$.", "Therefore, Archimedes should choose $x = -3/4$ as his best strategy, since this makes $z$ almost independent of $y$, and gives him the least disadvantage against Brahmagupta.", "# Answer\n\n-3/4"], "pre_generated_answer": "-3/4", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.01687766374292972}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "Since Archimedes chooses $x$ first, he should try to make $z$ as insensitive to $y$ as possible.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That is, he should try to eliminate the dependence of $z$ on $y$ or make it very weak.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to do this is to make the partial derivative of $z$ with respect to $y$ zero, or very small.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That is, he should try to find an $x$ such that\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 0 \\text{ or close to } 0.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the partial derivative of $z$ with respect to $y$, I use the power rule and the chain rule.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 2(2x-y)(-1) - 4y - 3 = -4x + 4y - 4y - 3 = -4x - 3.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 2(2x - y)(-1) - 4y - 3.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = 2(2x - y)(-1) - 4y - 3 = -4x + 6y - 3.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get\n\\[\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = -4(2x - y) + 2(-2y) - 3.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 396883, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T05:01:25.437355", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Solve for $x: 3^{2x} + 19 = 10^x$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Rewrite $3^{2x}$ as $(3^2)^x=9^x$, and subtract $9^x$ from both sides to obtain $19=10^x-9^x$.  There are no solutions of this equation for $x\\leq 0$, because neither $10^x$ nor $9^x$ is greater than 1 if $x\\leq 0$.  Trying $x=1$, $x=2$, and $x=3$, we see that $10^x-9^x$ is increasing for $x>0$, and it equals 19 when $x=\\boxed{2}$.\n\nNote: Using calculus, we could prove that $10^x-9^x$ is monotonically increasing for $x>0$, which would prove that the solution we found is unique.", "ground_truth_answer": "2", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that this equation involves two different bases: 3 and 10.", "I wonder if I can rewrite them in terms of a common base, like 2.", "I recall that 3 = 2^{\\log_2 3} and 10 = 2^{\\log_2 10}, where \\log_2 means the logarithm base 2.", "I substitute these expressions into the equation and use the exponent rules to simplify:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n3^{2x} + 19 &= 10^x \\\\\n(2^{\\log_2 3})^{2x} + 19 &= (2^{\\log_2 10})^x \\\\\n2^{2x \\log_2 3} + 19 &= 2^{x \\log_2 10} \\\\\n\\end{align*}", "Now I have the same base on both sides, so I can equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n2x \\log_2 3 &= x \\log_2 10 - 19 \\\\\nx (\\log_2 3 - \\log_2 10) &= -19 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-19}{\\log_2 3 - \\log_2 10} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\n# Answer\n\n\\frac{-19}{\\log_2 3 - \\log_2 10}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{-19}{\\log_2 3 - \\log_2 10}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0063445534367645995}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this equation involves two different bases: 3 and 10.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can rewrite them in terms of a common base, like 2.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I wonder if I can rewrite them in terms of a common base, such as 2, to simplify the equation.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if I can rewrite them in terms of a common base, such as 2, using the property of logarithms: $\\log_b a = \\frac{\\log_c a}{\\log_c b}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if there is a way to write both of them in terms of a common base, so I can use the properties of exponents to simplify the equation.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I wonder if I can find a common base for both of them, or somehow use logarithms to simplify the equation.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 583472, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "9ef92825-c3e7-4666-8db8-1ebc83fcfdfd", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:57:52.871774", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f(x)=|x-p|+|x-15|+|x-p-15|,$ where $0 < p < 15.$ Determine the minimum value taken by $f(x)$ for $x$ in the interval $p \\leq x\\leq15.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Since $0 < p \\le x \\le 15,$ the absolute values simplify to \\[f(x) = (x-p) - (x-15) - (x-p-15) = -x+30.\\]The value of this expression is minimized when $x=15,$ giving $-15+30=\\boxed{15}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three absolute value expressions, each of which is a piecewise linear function.", "To find the minimum value of $f(x),$ I need to analyze how $f(x)$ behaves on different intervals determined by the endpoints of the absolute value expressions.", "These endpoints are $p, 15,$ and $p+15.$", "Since $0 < p < 15,$ there are four possible cases to consider: $x < p, p \\leq x < 15, 15 \\leq x < p+15,$ and $x \\geq p+15.$", "I will use a table to organize the values of each absolute value expression and the sum $f(x)$ for each case.", "| Case | $|x-p|$ | $|x-15|$ | $|x-p-15|$ | $f(x)$ |\n|------|---------|----------|------------|--------|\n| $x < p$ | $p-x$ | $15-x$ | $p+15-x$ | $2p+30-3x$ |\n| $p \\leq x < 15$ | $x-p$ | $15-x$ | $p+15-x$ | $2p$ |\n| $15 \\leq x < p+15$ | $x-p$ | $x-15$ | $p+15-x$ | $30$ |\n| $x \\geq p+15$ | $x-p$ | $x-15$ | $x-p-15$ | $3x-2p-30$ |", "I can see from the table that the minimum value of $f(x)$ occurs when $p \\leq x < 15,$ in which case $f(x) = 2p.$", "Since $p$ is a fixed constant, $f(x)$ is constant on this interval, and any value of $x$ in this interval will give the same minimum value.", "Therefore, the minimum value of $f(x)$ for $x$ in the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$ is $2p.$", "# Answer\n\n2p"], "pre_generated_answer": "2p", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.005212722708041413}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three absolute value expressions, each of which is a piecewise linear function.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the minimum value of $f(x),$ I need to analyze how $f(x)$ behaves on different intervals determined by the endpoints of the absolute value expressions.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "These endpoints are $p, 15,$ and $p+15.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $0 < p < 15,$ there are four possible cases to consider: $x < p, p \\leq x < 15, 15 \\leq x < p+15,$ and $x \\geq p+15.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "To simplify the notation, let me call these endpoints $a, b,$ and $c,$ where $a< b < c.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To avoid confusion, I will sort these endpoints in increasing order and label them as $a,b,$ and $c,$ respectively.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Since $p < 15,$ I can divide the interval $p \\leq x\\leq15$ into two subintervals: $p \\leq x < 15$ and $15 \\leq x \\leq p+15.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Depending on the value of $x,$ one or more of the absolute value expressions will change signs, and the slope of $f(x)$ will change accordingly.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 10101403, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "5a1ab09c-9262-405a-aa6f-b44212a6a84b", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:50:36.552606", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Positive integers $a$, $b$, and $2009$, with $a<b<2009$, form a geometric sequence with an integer ratio. What is $a$?", "ground_truth_solution": "The prime factorization of $2009$ is $2009 = 7\\cdot 7\\cdot 41$. As $a<b<2009$, the ratio must be positive and larger than $1$. Hence, there is only one possibility: the ratio must be $7$, and thus $b=7\\cdot 41$ and $a=\\boxed{41}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "41", "pre_generated_steps": ["A geometric sequence is a sequence where each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by a constant ratio, which in this case is an integer.", "So, we can write $b=a\\cdot r$ and $2009=b\\cdot r$, where $r$ is the integer ratio.", "Substituting for $b$, we get $2009=a\\cdot r^2$.", "To find possible values of $a$ and $r$, we need to factor $2009$ into two factors that are both perfect squares, since $a$ and $r^2$ are both integers.", "One way to do this is to use the prime factorization of $2009$: $2009=7^2\\cdot 41$.", "Then, we can choose either $a=7^2=49$ and $r^2=41$, or $a=41$ and $r^2=7^2$.", "But, we also have the condition that $a<b<2009$, which rules out the second choice, since $41\\cdot 7^2>2009$.", "So, the only possible value of $a$ is $49$.", "# Answer\n\n49"], "pre_generated_answer": "49", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.010476742527377847}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "A geometric sequence is a sequence where each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by a constant ratio, which in this case is an integer.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, we can write $b=a\\cdot r$ and $2009=b\\cdot r$, where $r$ is the integer ratio.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Substituting for $b$, we get $2009=a\\cdot r^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find possible values of $a$ and $r$, we need to factor $2009$ into two factors that are both perfect squares, since $a$ and $r^2$ are both integers.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to do this is to use the prime factorization of $2009$: $2009=7^2\\cdot 41$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then, we can choose either $a=7^2=49$ and $r^2=41$, or $a=41$ and $r^2=7^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But, we also have the condition that $a<b<2009$, which rules out the second choice, since $41\\cdot 7^2>2009$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "But we have to check if these values satisfy the condition that $a<b<2009$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, the second option does not satisfy the condition that $a<b<2009$, since $b=a\\cdot r=41\\cdot 7=287$ is not less than $a=41$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, the second option does not work, since it implies $b=41\\cdot 7=287$, which is not an integer ratio of $2009$ (as $2009/287$ is not an integer).", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, the second option does not satisfy the condition that $a<b<2009$, since $b=a\\cdot r=41\\cdot 7=287>2009$. So this option is invalid.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 265714, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "e492b9ab-1083-4bb9-b67a-98531c908684", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T04:45:53.482413", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The equation\n\\[x^{10}+(13x-1)^{10}=0\\,\\]has 10 complex roots $r_1,$ $\\overline{r}_1,$ $r_2,$ $\\overline{r}_2,$ $r_3,$ $\\overline{r}_3,$ $r_4,$ $\\overline{r}_4,$ $r_5,$ $\\overline{r}_5,$ where the bar denotes complex conjugation. Find the value of\n\\[\\frac 1{r_1\\overline{r}_1}+\\frac 1{r_2\\overline{r}_2}+\\frac 1{r_3\\overline{r}_3}+\\frac 1{r_4\\overline{r}_4}+\\frac 1{r_5\\overline{r}_5}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $p(x) = x^{10} + (13x - 1)^{10}.$  If $r$ is a root of $p(x),$ then $r^{10} + (13x - 1)^{10} = 0.$  Then $(13r - 1)^{10} = -r^{10},$ so\n\\[-1 = \\left( \\frac{13r - 1}{r} \\right)^{10} = \\left( \\frac{1}{r} - 13 \\right)^{10}.\\]Then $\\frac{1}{r} - 13$ has magnitude 1, so\n\\[\\left( \\frac{1}{r} - 13 \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}} - 13 \\right) = 1,\\]so\n\\[\\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - 13 \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_1} - 13 \\right) + \\dots + \\left( \\frac{1}{r_5} - 13 \\right) \\left( \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_5} - 13 \\right) = 5.\\]Expanding, we get\n\\[\\frac{1}{r_1 \\overline{r}_1} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{r_5 \\overline{r}_5} - 13 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} + \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_1} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{r_5} + \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_5} \\right) + 5 \\cdot 169 = 5.\\]We see that $\\frac{1}{r_1},$ $\\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_1},$ $\\dots,$ $\\frac{1}{r_5},$ $\\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_5}$ are the solutions to\n\\[\\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^{10} + \\left( \\frac{13}{x} - 1 \\right)^{10} = 0,\\]or $1 + (13 - x)^{10} = 0.$  The first few terms in the expansion as\n\\[x^{10} - 130x^9 + \\dotsb = 0,\\]so by Vieta's formulas,\n\\[\\frac{1}{r_1} + \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_1} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{r_5} + \\frac{1}{\\overline{r}_5} = 130.\\]Hence,\n\\[\\frac{1}{r_1 \\overline{r}_1} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{r_5 \\overline{r}_5} = 13 \\cdot 130 - 5 \\cdot 169 + 5 = \\boxed{850}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "850", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that this equation is symmetric under the transformation $x \\mapsto \\frac{1}{x}$.", "That is, if $x$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{x}$.", "This means that the roots must come in pairs of the form $r$ and $\\frac{1}{r}$, unless $r = \\pm 1$.", "So, the complex roots $r_1, \\ldots, r_5$ and their conjugates must be of this form, and the only possible real roots are $x = \\pm 1$.", "However, I can quickly check that neither $x = 1$ nor $x = -1$ satisfy the equation, so there are no real roots.", "Therefore, the expression I want to find is equivalent to\n\\[\\frac 1{r_1\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_1}}+\\frac 1{r_2\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_2}}+\\frac 1{r_3\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_3}}+\\frac 1{r_4\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_4}}+\\frac 1{r_5\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_5}},\\]\nwhich simplifies to $5$.", "# Answer\n\n5"], "pre_generated_answer": "5", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0792007215793157}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this equation is symmetric under the transformation $x \\mapsto \\frac{1}{x}$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "That is, if $x$ is a root, then so is $\\frac{1}{x}$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the roots must come in pairs of the form $r$ and $\\frac{1}{r}$, unless $r = \\pm 1$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, the complex roots $r_1, \\ldots, r_5$ and their conjugates must be of this form, and the only possible real roots are $x = \\pm 1$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "However, I can quickly check that neither $x = 1$ nor $x = -1$ satisfy the equation, so there are no real roots.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, the expression I want to find is equivalent to\n\\[\\frac 1{r_1\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_1}}+\\frac 1{r_2\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_2}}+\\frac 1{r_3\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_3}}+\\frac 1{r_4\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_4}}+\\frac 1{r_5\\cdot \\frac{1}{r_5}},\\]\nwhich simplifies to $5$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n5", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 669968, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "80cac07a-618b-4d1c-a9f9-6af9ef621fde", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T08:34:08.174163", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Suppose the polynomial\n$$f(x) = a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0$$has integer coefficients, and its roots are distinct integers.\n\nGiven that $a_n=2$ and $a_0=66$, what is the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$?", "ground_truth_solution": "Since $f(x)$ has integer coefficients, the Integer Root Theorem tells us that all integer roots of $f(x)$ must divide the constant term $66=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 11$. Thus, the possible integer roots of $f(x)$ are\n$$\\pm 1,~\\pm 2,~\\pm 3,~\\pm 6,~\\pm 11,~\\pm 22,~\\pm 33,~\\pm 66.$$Moreover, since we know that all roots of $f(x)$ are integers, we know that all roots of $f(x)$ appear in the list above.\n\nNow we apply Vieta's formulas. The product of the roots of $f(x)$ is $(-1)^n\\cdot\\frac{a_0}{a_n}$, which is $33$ or $-33$. Also, the sum of the roots is $-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}=-\\frac{a_{n-1}}2$. Thus, in order to minimize $|a_{n-1}|$, we should make the absolute value of the sum of the roots as small as possible, working under the constraint that the product of the roots must be $33$ or $-33$.\n\nWe now consider two cases.\n\nCase 1 is that one of $33,-33$ is a root, in which case the only other possible roots are $\\pm 1$. In this case, the absolute value of the sum of the roots is at least $32$.\n\nThe alternative, Case 2, is that one of $11,-11$ is a root and one of $3,-3$ is a root. Again, the only other possible roots are $\\pm 1$, so the absolute value of the sum of the roots is at least $11-3-1=7$, which is better than the result of Case 1. If the absolute value of the sum of the roots is $7$, then $|a_{n-1}|=7|a_n|=7\\cdot 2=14$.\n\nTherefore, we have shown that $|a_{n-1}|\\ge 14$, and we can check that equality is achieved by\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= 2(x+11)(x-3)(x-1) \\\\\n&= 2x^3+14x^2-82x+66,\n\\end{align*}which has integer coefficients and integer roots. So the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is $\\boxed{14}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "14", "pre_generated_steps": ["I want to find the least possible value of the absolute value of the coefficient of the second-highest degree term of this polynomial.", "I know that the polynomial has integer coefficients and integer roots, and that the roots are distinct.", "This means that the polynomial can be factored as\n$$f(x) = 2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\\cdots(x-r_n)$$where $r_1,r_2,\\ldots,r_n$ are the distinct integer roots of the polynomial.", "I also know that the constant term of the polynomial is 66, which means that\n$$a_0 = 66 = 2\\cdot r_1\\cdot r_2\\cdots r_n$$", "This tells me that the product of the roots is 33, and since they are distinct integers, there are only a few possibilities for the roots.", "They could be $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 33$, or $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 11$.", "I want to find the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$, which is the absolute value of the coefficient of the second-highest degree term of the polynomial.", "To find this coefficient, I can use the formula\n$$a_{n-1} = -2\\sum_{i=1}^n r_i$$", "This means that $|a_{n-1}|$ is twice the sum of the absolute values of the roots.", "So I want to minimize this sum by choosing the roots with the smallest absolute values possible.", "If I choose $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 33$ as the roots, then the sum of the absolute values is $1+1+33+33=68$, and $|a_{n-1}|=2\\cdot 68=136$.", "If I choose $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 11$ as the roots, then the sum of the absolute values is $3+3+11+11=28$, and $|a_{n-1}|=2\\cdot 28=56$.", "I cannot choose any other roots, because they would not multiply to 33.", "So the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is 56.", "# Answer\n\n56"], "pre_generated_answer": "56", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.03981750142910041}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I want to find the least possible value of the absolute value of the coefficient of the second-highest degree term of this polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the polynomial has integer coefficients and integer roots, and that the roots are distinct.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the polynomial can be factored as\n$$f(x) = 2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\\cdots(x-r_n)$$where $r_1,r_2,\\ldots,r_n$ are the distinct integer roots of the polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also know that the constant term of the polynomial is 66, which means that\n$$a_0 = 66 = 2\\cdot r_1\\cdot r_2\\cdots r_n$$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This tells me that the product of the roots is 33, and since they are distinct integers, there are only a few possibilities for the roots.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "They could be $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 33$, or $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 11$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to find the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$, which is the absolute value of the coefficient of the second-highest degree term of the polynomial.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find this coefficient, I can use the formula\n$$a_{n-1} = -2\\sum_{i=1}^n r_i$$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $|a_{n-1}|$ is twice the sum of the absolute values of the roots.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This means that $|a_{n-1}|$ is the sum of the absolute values of the roots, multiplied by 2.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that $|a_{n-1}|$ is proportional to the sum of the absolute values of the roots, and I want to minimize this sum.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that $a_{n-1}$ is the opposite of twice the sum of the roots.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 11826677, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "70214e5e-a3d9-4f92-b100-11401778bb12", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T08:01:37.059393", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the number of integer values of $k$ in the closed interval $[-500,500]$ for which the equation $\\log(kx)=2\\log(x+2)$ has exactly one real solution.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, note that if $k < 0,$ then $\\log(kx)$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, 0),$ and is strictly decreasing on that interval. Since $2\\log(x+2)$ is defined for $x \\in (-2, \\infty)$ and is strictly increasing on that interval, it follows that $\\log(kx) = 2\\log(x+2)$ has exactly one real solution, which must lie in the interval $(-2, 0).$ Therefore, all the values $k = -500, -499, \\ldots, -2, -1$ satisfy the condition.\n\nIf $k = 0,$ then the left-hand side is never defined, so we may assume now that $k > 0.$ In this case, converting to exponential form, we have \\[ kx = (x+2)^2\\]or \\[x^2 + (4-k)x + 4 = 0.\\]Any solution of this equation satisfies $\\log(kx) = 2\\log(x+2)$ as well, as long as the two logarithms are defined; since $k > 0,$ the logarithms are defined exactly when $x > 0.$ Therefore, this quadratic must have exactly one positive root.\n\nBut by Vieta's formulas, the product of the roots of this quadratic is $4,$ which is positive, so the only way for it to have exactly one positive root is if it has $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ as a double root. That is, \\[x^2 + (4-k)x + 4 = (x-2)^2 = x^2 - 4x + 4\\]for all $x,$ so $4-k=-4,$ and $k=8,$ which is the only positive value of $k$ satisfying the condition.\n\nIn total, there are $500 + 1 = \\boxed{501}$ values of $k$ satisfying the condition.", "ground_truth_answer": "501", "pre_generated_steps": ["To solve this problem, I need to use some properties of logarithms and some algebraic manipulation.", "First, I can rewrite the equation using the power rule of logarithms: $\\log(kx)=\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "This means that $kx$ and $(x+2)^2$ have the same logarithm, so they must be equal: $kx=(x+2)^2$.", "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $kx=x^2+4x+4$.", "Rearranging, I get $x^2+(4-k)x+4=0$.", "This is a quadratic equation in $x$, and I want to find the values of $k$ that make it have exactly one real solution.", "I know that a quadratic equation has one real solution if and only if its discriminant is zero.", "The discriminant is the part under the square root in the quadratic formula: $b^2-4ac$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are the coefficients of the quadratic.", "In this case, $a=1$, $b=4-k$, and $c=4$.", "So the discriminant is $(4-k)^2-4(1)(4)$.", "Setting this equal to zero and simplifying, I get $(4-k)^2=16$.", "Taking the square root of both sides, I get $4-k=\\pm 4$.", "Solving for $k$, I get $k=4\\pm 4$.", "This means there are two possible values of $k$: $k=0$ or $k=8$.", "However, I need to check if these values are in the given interval $[-500,500]$.", "They both are, so I have found two values of $k$ that make the equation have exactly one real solution.", "Therefore, the answer is 2.", "# Answer\n\n2"], "pre_generated_answer": "2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.001070792573318147}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To solve this problem, I need to use some properties of logarithms and some algebraic manipulation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "First, I can rewrite the equation using the power rule of logarithms: $\\log(kx)=\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $kx$ and $(x+2)^2$ have the same logarithm, so they must be equal: $kx=(x+2)^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Expanding the right-hand side, I get $kx=x^2+4x+4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Rearranging, I get $x^2+(4-k)x+4=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is a quadratic equation in $x$, and I want to find the values of $k$ that make it have exactly one real solution.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that a quadratic equation has one real solution if and only if its discriminant is zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The discriminant is the part under the square root in the quadratic formula: $b^2-4ac$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are the coefficients of the quadratic.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "In this case, $a=1$, $b=4-k$, and $c=4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the discriminant is $(4-k)^2-4(1)(4)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Setting this equal to zero and simplifying, I get $(4-k)^2=16$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Taking the square root of both sides, I get $4-k=\\pm 4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Solving for $k$, I get $k=4\\pm 4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means there are two possible values of $k$: $k=0$ or $k=8$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This gives me two possible values of $k$: $k=0$ and $k=8$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So there are two possible values of $k$: $k=0$ and $k=8$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Therefore, there are two possible values of $k$ that make the equation have exactly one real solution: $k=0$ and $k=8$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means that $k$ can be either $0$ or $8$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1923823, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6ea71b48-2a87-43c0-aabf-b31a8831b803", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T07:46:44.587831", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the number of integer values of $k$ in the closed interval $[-500,500]$ for which the equation $\\log(kx)=2\\log(x+2)$ has exactly one real solution.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, note that if $k < 0,$ then $\\log(kx)$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, 0),$ and is strictly decreasing on that interval. Since $2\\log(x+2)$ is defined for $x \\in (-2, \\infty)$ and is strictly increasing on that interval, it follows that $\\log(kx) = 2\\log(x+2)$ has exactly one real solution, which must lie in the interval $(-2, 0).$ Therefore, all the values $k = -500, -499, \\ldots, -2, -1$ satisfy the condition.\n\nIf $k = 0,$ then the left-hand side is never defined, so we may assume now that $k > 0.$ In this case, converting to exponential form, we have \\[ kx = (x+2)^2\\]or \\[x^2 + (4-k)x + 4 = 0.\\]Any solution of this equation satisfies $\\log(kx) = 2\\log(x+2)$ as well, as long as the two logarithms are defined; since $k > 0,$ the logarithms are defined exactly when $x > 0.$ Therefore, this quadratic must have exactly one positive root.\n\nBut by Vieta's formulas, the product of the roots of this quadratic is $4,$ which is positive, so the only way for it to have exactly one positive root is if it has $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ as a double root. That is, \\[x^2 + (4-k)x + 4 = (x-2)^2 = x^2 - 4x + 4\\]for all $x,$ so $4-k=-4,$ and $k=8,$ which is the only positive value of $k$ satisfying the condition.\n\nIn total, there are $500 + 1 = \\boxed{501}$ values of $k$ satisfying the condition.", "ground_truth_answer": "501", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that this equation involves logarithms, so I want to use some properties of logarithms to simplify it.", "One property I know is that $\\log(a^b)=b\\log(a)$, so I can rewrite the left-hand side of the equation as $\\log(x^k)$.", "Another property I know is that $\\log(a)+\\log(b)=\\log(ab)$, so I can rewrite the right-hand side of the equation as $\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "Now the equation becomes $\\log(x^k)=\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "Since the logarithm function is one-to-one, this means that the arguments of the logarithms must be equal, so I can write $x^k=(x+2)^2$.", "Now I have a polynomial equation, which might be easier to solve than a logarithmic equation.", "However, the degree of the polynomial depends on the value of $k$, so I want to consider different cases for $k$.", "If $k=0$, then the equation becomes $1=(x+2)^2$, which has no real solutions, so I can eliminate this case.", "If $k=1$, then the equation becomes $x=(x+2)^2$, which simplifies to $x^2+4x+3=0$.", "I can factor this quadratic as $(x+1)(x+3)=0$, which has two real solutions, $x=-1$ and $x=-3$, so I can eliminate this case as well.", "If $k=2$, then the equation becomes $x^2=(x+2)^2$, which simplifies to $4x+4=0$.", "This has one real solution, $x=-1$, so this is a possible value of $k$.", "If $k>2$, then the equation becomes $x^k>(x+2)^2$ for all $x$ except $x=-2$, where the equation is undefined.", "I notice that the left-hand side is always positive, while the right-hand side is negative for $x<-2$ and positive for $x>-2$.", "This means that the equation has no real solutions for $x<-2$, and at most one real solution for $x>-2$.", "To find out if there is a solution for $x>-2$, I need to compare the rates of growth of the two sides.", "I know that $x^k$ grows faster than $(x+2)^2$ as $x$ increases, so there must be some point where they are equal, and after that point, $x^k$ is larger.", "This means that there is exactly one real solution for $x>-2$, and it is the point where the two curves intersect.", "Therefore, any value of $k>2$ satisfies the condition of the problem.", "If $k<0$, then the equation becomes $x^k<(x+2)^2$ for all $x$ except $x=-2$, where the equation is undefined.", "I notice that the left-hand side is positive for $x>0$ and negative for $x<0$, while the right-hand side is positive for $x\\neq -2$.", "This means that the equation has no real solutions for $x<0$, and at most one real solution for $x>0$.", "To find out if there is a solution for $x>0$, I need to compare the rates of decay of the two sides.", "I know that $x^k$ decays faster than $(x+2)^2$ as $x$ decreases, so there must be some point where they are equal, and before that point, $x^k$ is smaller.", "This means that there is exactly one real solution for $x>0$, and it is the point where the two curves intersect.", "Therefore, any value of $k<0$ satisfies the condition of the problem.", "To summarize, the only values of $k$ that do not satisfy the condition of the problem are $k=0$ and $k=1$.", "Since $k$ can take any integer value in the closed interval $[-500,500]$, there are $1000$ possible values of $k$ in total.", "Therefore, the number of values of $k$ that satisfy the condition of the problem is $1000-2=998$.", "# Answer\n\n998"], "pre_generated_answer": "998", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.7756888825414126e-19}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that this equation involves logarithms, so I want to use some properties of logarithms to simplify it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One property I know is that $\\log(a^b)=b\\log(a)$, so I can rewrite the left-hand side of the equation as $\\log(x^k)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Another property I know is that $\\log(a)+\\log(b)=\\log(ab)$, so I can rewrite the right-hand side of the equation as $\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now the equation becomes $\\log(x^k)=\\log((x+2)^2)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since the logarithm function is one-to-one, this means that the arguments of the logarithms must be equal, so I can write $x^k=(x+2)^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now I have a polynomial equation, which might be easier to solve than a logarithmic equation.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "However, the degree of the polynomial depends on the value of $k$, so I want to consider different cases for $k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k=0$, then the equation becomes $1=(x+2)^2$, which has no real solutions, so I can eliminate this case.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k=1$, then the equation becomes $x=(x+2)^2$, which simplifies to $x^2+4x+3=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can factor this quadratic as $(x+1)(x+3)=0$, which has two real solutions, $x=-1$ and $x=-3$, so I can eliminate this case as well.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k=2$, then the equation becomes $x^2=(x+2)^2$, which simplifies to $4x+4=0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This has one real solution, $x=-1$, so this is a possible value of $k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k>2$, then the equation becomes $x^k>(x+2)^2$ for all $x$ except $x=-2$, where the equation is undefined.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the left-hand side is always positive, while the right-hand side is negative for $x<-2$ and positive for $x>-2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the equation has no real solutions for $x<-2$, and at most one real solution for $x>-2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find out if there is a solution for $x>-2$, I need to compare the rates of growth of the two sides.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that $x^k$ grows faster than $(x+2)^2$ as $x$ increases, so there must be some point where they are equal, and after that point, $x^k$ is larger.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that there is exactly one real solution for $x>-2$, and it is the point where the two curves intersect.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, any value of $k>2$ satisfies the condition of the problem.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If $k<0$, then the equation becomes $x^k<(x+2)^2$ for all $x$ except $x=-2$, where the equation is undefined.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the left-hand side is positive for $x>0$ and negative for $x<0$, while the right-hand side is positive for $x\\neq -2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the equation has no real solutions for $x<0$, and at most one real solution for $x>0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find out if there is a solution for $x>0$, I need to compare the rates of decay of the two sides.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that $x^k$ decays faster than $(x+2)^2$ as $x$ decreases, so there must be some point where they are equal, and before that point, $x^k$ is smaller.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that there is exactly one real solution for $x>0$, and it is the point where the two curves intersect.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, any value of $k<0$ satisfies the condition of the problem.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To summarize, the only values of $k$ that do not satisfy the condition of the problem are $k=0$ and $k=1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $k$ can take any integer value in the closed interval $[-500,500]$, there are $1000$ possible values of $k$ in total.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Since $k$ is an integer in the interval $[-500,500]$, there are 1001 possible values of $k$ in total, and I need to subtract the two values that do not work.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "All other values of $k$ in the interval $[-500,500]$ do satisfy the condition, and there are $1000$ of them.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "All other values in the interval $[-500,500]$ work, so there are $500-(-500)-2=998$ such values.\n\n# Answer\n\n998", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Since $k$ is an integer in the interval $[-500,500]$, there are $500-(-500)+1=1001$ possible values of $k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "tota