{"labeler": "5a1ab09c-9262-405a-aa6f-b44212a6a84b", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T02:24:08.121853", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ.$", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we know $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\sin 120^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ so\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}We can then write $\\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin 100^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ,$ $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ,$ $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ,$ so\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{3}{4} \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ &= \\frac{3}{4} \\cos^2 10^\\circ \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} (\\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ)^2.\n\\end{align*}By product-to-sum,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ &= \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} (\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ) - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 30^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, the expression is equal to $\\frac{3}{4} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\right)^2 = \\boxed{\\frac{9}{256}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{9}{256}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a very complicated expression, but I notice a few things that might help me simplify it.", "First, I see that all the angles are multiples of 20 degrees, and that they form an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of 20 degrees.", "So maybe there is some symmetry or pattern that I can exploit.", "Second, I recall that the sine function has some useful properties, such as $\\sin (\\pi - x) = \\sin x$ and $\\sin (x + \\pi) = -\\sin x$, where $x$ is any angle in radians.", "These properties mean that I can rewrite some of the angles in terms of others, and maybe cancel out some factors.", "Third, I also remember that $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$, which are nice rational numbers that might make the calculation easier.", "So let me start by using the first property to rewrite some of the angles.", "I can write $\\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin (\\pi - 80^\\circ) = \\sin 80^\\circ$, and similarly $\\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin 60^\\circ$, $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ$, and $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ$.", "This means that the expression is actually equal to $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$, which I can rearrange as $(\\sin 20^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 40^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 60^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 80^\\circ)^2$.", "Now I can use the second property to rewrite some of the angles as negative angles.", "I can write $\\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin (80^\\circ - \\pi) = -\\sin (-80^\\circ) = -\\sin (40^\\circ - \\pi) = -\\sin (-40^\\circ + \\pi) = -\\sin (-40^\\circ)$.", "So $\\sin 80^\\circ = -\\sin 40^\\circ$.", "This means that the expression is actually equal to $(\\sin 20^\\circ)^2 (-\\sin 40^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 60^\\circ)^2 (-\\sin 40^\\circ)^2 = (\\sin 20^\\circ)^2 (\\sin 40^\\circ)^4 (\\sin 60^\\circ)^2 (-1)^2$.", "Now I can use the values of $\\sin 60^\\circ$ and $\\sin 30^\\circ$ to simplify the expression further.", "I know that $\\sin 40^\\circ = \\sin (60^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ \\sin 20^\\ci