{"labeler": "215edb5c-9e68-4edb-8702-5a23153c45b5", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T02:22:38.873278", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ.$", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we know $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\sin 120^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ so\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ.\n\\end{align*}We can then write $\\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin 100^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ,$ $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ,$ $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ,$ so\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{3}{4} \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ &= \\frac{3}{4} \\cos^2 10^\\circ \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} (\\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ)^2.\n\\end{align*}By product-to-sum,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ &= \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos 20^\\circ - \\cos 60^\\circ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\left( \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} (\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ) - \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 10^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cos 30^\\circ \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, the expression is equal to $\\frac{3}{4} \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8} \\right)^2 = \\boxed{\\frac{9}{256}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{9}{256}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a tricky trigonometry problem, but I notice some patterns in the angles that might help.", "First of all, I can rewrite each angle as a multiple of $20^\\circ$ and use the fact that $\\sin (180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x$ to simplify some terms.", "For example, $\\sin 40^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ \\times 2$ and $\\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 100^\\circ) = \\sin 100^\\circ$.", "So the product becomes $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin^2 80^\\circ.$", "Now, I can use another identity, $\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x$, to write $\\sin^2 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin^2 80^\\circ$ and $\\sin^2 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin^2 160^\\circ$.", "So the product becomes $\\frac{1}{16} \\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 160^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ.$", "Next, I can use the identity $\\sin x \\sin y = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (x - y) - \\cos (x + y))$ to write $\\sin^2 20^\\circ \\sin^2 160^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} (\\cos 140^\\circ - \\cos 180^\\circ - \\cos 0^\\circ + \\cos 40^\\circ)$.", "Using the fact that $\\cos 180^\\circ = -1$ and $\\cos 0^\\circ = 1$, this simplifies to $\\frac{1}{4} (\\cos 140^\\circ + 2 + \\cos 40^\\circ)$.", "Now, I can use another identity, $\\cos x \\cos y = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (x + y) + \\cos (x - y))$, to write $\\cos 140^\\circ + \\cos 40^\\circ = \\cos 180^\\circ + \\cos 100^\\circ$.", "Using the fact that $\\cos 180^\\circ = -1$, this simplifies to $-2 + \\cos 100^\\circ$.", "So the product becomes $\\frac{1}{16} (-2 + \\cos 100^\\circ) \\sin 60^\\circ$.", "Finally, I can use the values of $\\cos 100^\\circ = -\\cos 80^\\circ$ and $\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ to get the final answer.", "# Answer\n\n$\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{16} (\\cos 80^\\circ + 1)$"], "pre_generated_answer": "$\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{16} (\\cos 80^\\circ + 1)$", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 8.100413491981909e-11}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a tricky trigonometry problem, but I notice some patterns in the angles that might help.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "First of all, I can rewrite each angle as a multiple of $20^\\circ$ and use the fact that $\\sin (180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x$ to simplify some terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, $\\sin 40^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ \\times 2$ and $\\sin 80^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 100^\\circ) = \\sin 100^\\circ$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "For example, $\\sin 100^\\circ = \\sin 5\\cdot 20^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 4\\cdot 20^\\circ) = \\sin 4\\cdot 20^\\circ.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The expression becomes $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin (180^\\circ - 120^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 100^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ)$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For example, $\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) = \\sin 40^\\circ$ and $\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So I have $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin (180^\\circ - 80^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 40^\\circ) \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ)$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 350348, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "ad240c6d-1f78-41db-aebe-27cdd9eedca6", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T02:22:24.657434", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ $c$ be real numbers such that\n\\[|ax^2 + bx + c| \\le 1\\]for all $0 \\le x \\le 1.$  Find the largest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c|.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Setting $x = 0,$ we get $|c| \\le 1.$  Setting $x = 1,$ we get\n\\[|a + b + c| \\le 1.\\]Setting $x = \\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\\[\\left| \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\right| \\le 1.\\]Let\n\\begin{align*}\np &= c, \\\\\nq &= \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c, \\\\\nr &= a + b + c,\n\\end{align*}so $-1 \\le p,$ $q,$ $r \\le 1.$  Solving for $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we find\n\\begin{align*}\na &= 2p - 4q + 2r, \\\\\nb &= -3p + 4q - r, \\\\\nc &= p.\n\\end{align*}Hence, by Triangle Inequality,\n\\begin{align*}\n|a| &= |2p - 4q + 2r| \\le |2p| + |4q| + |2r| = 8, \\\\\n|b| &= |-3p + 4q - r| \\le |3p| + |4q| + |r| = 8, \\\\\n|c| &= |p| \\le 1.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, $|a| + |b| + |c| = 8 + 8 + 1 = 17.$\n\nConsider the quadratic $f(x) = 8x^2 - 8x + 1.$  We can write\n\\[f(x) = 8 \\left( x - \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 - 1.\\]For $0 \\le x \\le 1,$ $0 \\le \\left( x - \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\le \\frac{1}{4},$ so $-1 \\le f(x) \\le 1.$\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c|$ is $\\boxed{17}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "17", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the given inequality is true for all $0 \\le x \\le 1,$ so it must be true in particular for the endpoints $x = 0$ and $x = 1.$", "Plugging in $x = 0,$ I get $|c| \\le 1.$", "Plugging in $x = 1,$ I get $|a + b + c| \\le 1.$", "Since $|a|, |b|, |c|$ are nonnegative, I can use the triangle inequality to get\n\\[|a| + |b| + |c| \\le |a + b + c| + |c| \\le 2.\\]", "This means that the largest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c|$ is at most $2.$", "To see if this bound is attainable, I try to construct an example of a quadratic function that satisfies the given inequality and has $|a| + |b| + |c| = 2.$", "I observe that if $|a| + |b| + |c| = 2,$ then either $|a| = 1$ and $|b| + |c| = 1,$ or $|b| = 1$ and $|a| + |c| = 1,$ or $|c| = 1$ and $|a| + |b| = 1.$", "In each case, I can choose the signs of $a, b, c$ such that the quadratic function is either always nonnegative or always nonpositive on $[0, 1].$", "For example, if $|a| = 1$ and $|b| + |c| = 1,$ I can take $a = 1, b = -1, c = 0,$ and then\n\\[|x^2 - x| \\le 1\\]for all $0 \\le x \\le 1.$", "Therefore, I conclude that the largest possible value of $|a| + |b| + |c|$ is $2,$ and it is achieved by several quadratic functions, such as $x^2 - x, -x^2 + x, x - 1, -x + 1.$", "# Answer\n\n2"], "pre_generated_answer": "2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.023805009235464514}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the given inequality is true for all $0 \\le x \\le 1,$ so it must be true in particular for the endpoints $x = 0$ and $x = 1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in $x = 0,$ I get $|c| \\le 1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in $x = 1,$ I get $|a + b + c| \\le 1.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $|a|, |b|, |c|$ are nonnegative, I can use the triangle inequality to get\n\\[|a| + |b| + |c| \\le |a + b + c| + |c| \\le 2.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "These two inequalities imply that $-1 \\le c \\le 1$ and $-1 - c \\le a + b \\le 1 - c.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Since $|c| \\le 1,$ I can rewrite the second inequality as $|a + b| \\le 1 + |c|.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Ideally, I want to maximize $|a| + |b| + |c|,$ so let me assume that $a,$ $b,$ $c$ are all positive. (If some of them are negative, I can just change their signs and get the same absolute value.)", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Adding these two inequalities, I get $|a| + |b| + 2|c| \\le 2.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 362582, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
