ed and add up to $180^\\circ.$", "One thing I notice is that $\\sin 20^\\circ = \\sin 160^\\circ,$ $\\sin 40^\\circ = \\sin 140^\\circ,$ and so on, by the property that $\\sin (\\pi - x) = \\sin x.$", "So I can pair up the terms and write the product as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\\\\n=&(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ)(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ)(\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ)(\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ)\\\\\n=&\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 180^\\circ\\right)^4\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere I used the identity $\\sin x \\sin y = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (x + y) - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (x - y)$ and simplified the terms.", "Now I can see that the answer is just $0^4,$ since $\\sin 180^\\circ = 0.$", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.028830536326917452}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks very symmetrical, so I wonder if there is some way to use the fact that the angles are equally spaced and add up to $180^\\circ.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One thing I notice is that $\\sin 20^\\circ = \\sin 160^\\circ,$ $\\sin 40^\\circ = \\sin 140^\\circ,$ and so on, by the property that $\\sin (\\pi - x) = \\sin x.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I can pair up the terms and write the product as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\\\\n=&(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ)(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ)(\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ)(\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ)\\\\\n=&\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 180^\\circ\\right)^4\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere I used the identity $\\sin x \\sin y = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (x + y) - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (x - y)$ and simplified the terms.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "So I can group the factors in pairs and write the expression as $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ.$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This means I can pair up the terms and write the product as $(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ)(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ)(\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ)(\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So the product can be simplified by grouping the corresponding terms: $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ = (\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ)(\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ)(\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ)(\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ).$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So I can pair up the terms and write the product as $(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ) (\\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ) (\\sin 60^\\circ \\sin 120^\\circ) (\\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ)$", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 221682, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "07f4ddad-9614-40d7-9b9a-056d5456ee77", "timestamp": "2022-11-13T02:12:26.230067", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f(x)=|x-p|+|x-15|+|x-p-15|,$ where $0 < p < 15.$ Determine the minimum value taken by $f(x)$ for $x$ in the interval $p \\leq x\\leq15.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Since $0 < p \\le x \\le 15,$ the absolute values simplify to \\[f(x) = (x-p) - (x-15) - (x-p-15) = -x+30.\\]The value of this expression is minimized when $x=15,$ giving $-15+30=\\boxed{15}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "15", "pre_generated_steps": ["This is a problem involving absolute values, which are often tricky to work with.", "One thing I can try is to split the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$ into subintervals where each absolute value expression is either positive or negative.", "For example, I know that $|x-p|$ is positive when $x \\geq p$ and negative when $x < p$.", "Similarly, $|x-15|$ is positive when $x \\leq 15$ and negative when $x > 15$.", "And $|x-p-15|$ is positive when $x \\geq p+15$ and negative when $x < p+15$.", "Since $0 < p < 15$, I can ignore the cases where $x > 15$ or $x < p$, because they are not in the given interval.", "So I only have two cases to consider: when $x$ is between $p$ and $p+15$, and when $x$ is between $p+15$ and $15$.", "In the first case, when $p \\leq x < p+15$, I have $|x-p|=x-p$, $|x-15|=15-x$, and $|x-p-15|=-(x-p-15)$.", "So the function becomes $f(x)=(x-p)+(15-x)-(x-p-15)=-2x+30$.", "In the second case, when $p+15 \\leq x \\leq 15$, I have $|x-p|=x-p$, $|x-15|=-(x-15)$, and $|x-p-15|=x-p-15$.", "So the function becomes $f(x)=(x-p)-(x-15)+(x-p-15)=2p-30$.", "Now I have two linear expressions for $f(x)$, depending on the value of $x$.", "I want to find the minimum value of $f(x)$, so I need to see where these expressions are smallest.", "For the first expression, $-2x+30$, I can see that it decreases as $x$ increases.", "So the minimum value will occur at the right endpoint of the interval, which is $x=p+15$.", "Plugging this in, I get $f(p+15)=-2(p+15)+30=-2p$.", "For the second expression, $2p-30$, I can see that it is constant, so it doesn't depend on $x$.", "It only depends on $p$, which is a fixed number between $0$ and $15$.", "So the minimum value will be the same for any $x$ in the interval $[p+15,15]$, and it is $f(x)=2p-30$.", "Now I have two candidates for the minimum value of $f(x)$: $-2p$ and $2p-30$.", "I need to compare them and see which one is smaller.", "I can rewrite $-2p$ as $2p-4p$, and then subtract $2p-30$ from both sides.", "This gives me $-4p-(-2p-30)=-2p+30$.", "I can divide both sides by $-2$, and reverse the inequality sign, to get $p-15 > -x+15$.", "Adding $15$ to both sides, I get $p > x$.", "This means that $-2p$ is smaller than $2p-30$ when $p > x$, or equivalently, when $x < p$.", "But this is impossible, because $x$ is always greater than or equal to $p$ in the given interval.", "So the minimum value of $f(x)$ cannot be $-2p$.", "Therefore, the minimum value of $f(x)$ must be $2p-30$, which occurs for any $x$ in the interval $[p+15,15]$.", "# Answer\n\n2p-30"], "pre_generated_answer": "2p-30", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 7.897915399869871e-17}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This is a problem involving absolute values, which are often tricky to work with.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One thing I can try is to split the interval $p \\leq x \\leq 15$ into subintervals where each absolute value expression is either positive or negative.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, I know that $|x-p|$ is positive when $x \\geq p$ and negative when $x < p$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "For example, if $x \\geq p + 15,$ then all three expressions inside the absolute values are positive, and $f(x) = x - p + x - 15 + x - p - 15 = 3x - 30 - 2p.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For example, when $x<p,$ I have $|x-p| = -(x-p),$ $|x-15| = -(x-15),$ and $|x-p-15| = -(x-p-15).$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "For example, if $x < p$, then $|x-p| = -(x-p),$ $|x-15| = -(x-15),$ and $|x-p-15| = -(x-p-15).$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let's start with $|x-p|.$ This will be positive if $x \\geq p,$ which is always true in the given interval, and negative if $x < p,$ which never happens in the given interval. So $|x-p| = x - p$ for all $x$ in $p \\leq x \\leq 15.$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 171917, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
