{"labeler": "69bed983-f10f-43ac-b2b8-1d7dcdaecc93", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:29:32.541526", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a + b \\ge 2 \\sqrt{ab},\\]so $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab.$\n\nAlso by AM-GM,\n\\[(a + 2c) + (b + 2c) \\ge 2 \\sqrt{(a + 2c)(b + 2c)},\\]so $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c).$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 &\\ge 4ab + 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c) \\\\\n&= 8ab + 8ac + 8bc + 16c^2 \\\\\n&= 8(ab + ac + bc + 2c^2).\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nab + ac + bc + 2c^2 &= \\frac{ab}{2} + \\frac{ab}{2} + ac + bc + 2c^2 \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot \\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot ac \\cdot bc \\cdot 2c^2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}Also by AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}}.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} &\\ge 8 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}}{abc} \\\\\n&= 100.\n\\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a = b = 2$ and $c = 1,$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{100}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "100", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $b$, so I wonder if I can use the AM-GM inequality to simplify it.", "AM-GM says that for any nonnegative numbers $x$ and $y$, the arithmetic mean $(x + y)/2$ is greater than or equal to the geometric mean $\\sqrt{xy}$, and equality holds only when $x = y$.", "Applying AM-GM to $a$ and $b$, I get $\\frac{a + b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ab}$, or equivalently, $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab$.", "Equality holds when $a = b$.", "Similarly, applying AM-GM to $a + b$ and $4c$, I get $\\frac{a + b + 4c}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{(a + b)(4c)}$, or equivalently, $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 16(a + b)c$.", "Equality holds when $a + b = 4c$.", "Multiplying these two inequalities, I get\n\\[(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2] \\ge 64abc.\\]", "This means that the expression I want to minimize is greater than or equal to $\\frac{64abc}{abc} = 64$, and equality holds when $a = b = 4c$.", "Therefore, the minimum value of the expression is 64, and it occurs when $a = b = 4c$.", "# Answer\n\n64"], "pre_generated_answer": "64", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.003813510261133503}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $b$, so I wonder if I can use the AM-GM inequality to simplify it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "AM-GM says that for any nonnegative numbers $x$ and $y$, the arithmetic mean $(x + y)/2$ is greater than or equal to the geometric mean $\\sqrt{xy}$, and equality holds only when $x = y$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Applying AM-GM to $a$ and $b$, I get $\\frac{a + b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ab}$, or equivalently, $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Equality holds when $a = b$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Similarly, applying AM-GM to $a + b$ and $4c$, I get $\\frac{a + b + 4c}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{(a + b)(4c)}$, or equivalently, $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 16(a + b)c$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Equality holds when $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Multiplying these two inequalities, I get\n\\[(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2] \\ge 64abc.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Combining these two inequalities, I get\n\\[ (a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4ab + 16(a + b)c = 4a(b + 4c) + 16bc.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now I can use these inequalities to bound the expression from below as follows:\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} \\ge \\frac{(a + b + c)[4ab + 16(a + b)c]}{abc}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Substituting these inequalities into the expression, I get\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} \\ge \\frac{4 \\sqrt{ab} + 4c}{\\sqrt{ab}c}(4ab + 16(a + b)c) = 64 + \\frac{256c^2}{\\sqrt{ab}}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Now I can use these inequalities to bound the expression from below.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 750211, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6c655923-0179-4f96-afbc-f52af3de908d", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:29:18.744261", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a + b \\ge 2 \\sqrt{ab},\\]so $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab.$\n\nAlso by AM-GM,\n\\[(a + 2c) + (b + 2c) \\ge 2 \\sqrt{(a + 2c)(b + 2c)},\\]so $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c).$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 &\\ge 4ab + 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c) \\\\\n&= 8ab + 8ac + 8bc + 16c^2 \\\\\n&= 8(ab + ac + bc + 2c^2).\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nab + ac + bc + 2c^2 &= \\frac{ab}{2} + \\frac{ab}{2} + ac + bc + 2c^2 \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot \\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot ac \\cdot bc \\cdot 2c^2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}Also by AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}}.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} &\\ge 8 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}}{abc} \\\\\n&= 100.\n\\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a = b = 2$ and $c = 1,$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{100}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "100", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks like it might involve some algebraic manipulation or calculus techniques, but I'm going to start by trying some intuition and guessing.", "If I want to minimize the expression, I need to make the numerator as small as possible and the denominator as large as possible, subject to the constraints that $a, b, c$ are positive.", "The numerator has two factors: $(a + b + c)$ and $[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]$.", "The first factor is the sum of the three variables, and the second factor is the sum of two squares.", "I notice that the second factor has a common term of $(a + b)^2$, so I can factor it out and rewrite the numerator as\n\\[(a + b + c)(a + b)^2(1 + (1 + 4c/(a + b))^2).\\]", "Now I can see that the second factor $(a + b)^2$ is also the square of the sum of two variables, and the third factor is a square plus one.", "I recall a useful inequality that says that for any positive numbers $x$ and $y$, $(x + y)^2 \\geq 4xy$, with equality if and only if $x = y$.", "This is called the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean (AM-GM) inequality.", "I can apply this inequality to the second factor $(a + b)^2$, and get\n\\[(a + b)^2 \\geq 4ab, \\]\nwith equality if and only if $a = b$.", "I can also apply this inequality to the third factor $(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2$, and get\n\\[(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2 \\geq 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (4c/(a + b)) = 16c/(a + b), \\]\nwith equality if and only if $1 = 4c/(a + b)$.", "Putting these two inequalities together, I get that the numerator of the expression is at least\n\\[(a + b + c)(4ab)(16c/(a + b)) = 64abc.\\]", "This lower bound is attained when $a = b$ and $1 = 4c/(a + b)$, or equivalently, when $a = b = 2c$.", "Now I turn my attention to the denominator $abc$.", "I want to make this as large as possible, given that $a, b, c$ are positive.", "I recall another useful inequality that says that for any positive numbers $x, y, z$, the arithmetic mean of $x, y, z$ is at least the geometric mean of $x, y, z$, or\n\\[\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz},\\]\nwith equality if and only if $x = y = z$.", "This is another form of the AM-GM inequality.", "I can apply this inequality to the denominator $abc$, and get\n\\[abc \\leq \\left(\\frac{a + b + c}{3}\\right)^3,\\]\nwith equality if and only if $a = b = c$.", "Combining this with the lower bound for the numerator, I get that the expression is at least\n\\[\\frac{64abc}{abc} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{a + b + c}{3}\\right)^3}{(a + b + c)^3} = \\frac{64}{27},\\]\nwith equality if and only if $a = b = c$.", "Therefore, the minimum value of the expression is $\\frac{64}{27}$, and it occurs when $a = b = c$.", "# Answer\n\n64/27"], "pre_generated_answer": "64/27", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 4.697199229335347e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks like it might involve some algebraic manipulation or calculus techniques, but I'm going to start by trying some intuition and guessing.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I want to minimize the expression, I need to make the numerator as small as possible and the denominator as large as possible, subject to the constraints that $a, b, c$ are positive.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The numerator has two factors: $(a + b + c)$ and $[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The first factor is the sum of the three variables, and the second factor is the sum of two squares.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the second factor has a common term of $(a + b)^2$, so I can factor it out and rewrite the numerator as\n\\[(a + b + c)(a + b)^2(1 + (1 + 4c/(a + b))^2).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "It seems likely that the numerator would be minimized when $a, b, c$ are as close to each other as possible, since that would balance the terms in both factors and avoid any large differences or squares.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I know that the sum of squares is minimized when the terms are equal, so I wonder if that might help me here.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the second factor has a common term of $(a + b)^2$, and the other term is $(a + b + 4c)^2$. This looks like a perfect square, since $(a + b + 4c)^2 = (a + b)^2 + 2(a + b)(4c) + (4c)^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the second factor has some symmetry in it: if I swap $a$ and $b$, the factor doesn't change, but if I swap either $a$ or $b$ with $c$, the factor increases.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 537819, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "9aa704c5-7f0a-4719-8cf3-931a83832a4b", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:27:25.521186", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a + b \\ge 2 \\sqrt{ab},\\]so $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab.$\n\nAlso by AM-GM,\n\\[(a + 2c) + (b + 2c) \\ge 2 \\sqrt{(a + 2c)(b + 2c)},\\]so $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c).$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 &\\ge 4ab + 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c) \\\\\n&= 8ab + 8ac + 8bc + 16c^2 \\\\\n&= 8(ab + ac + bc + 2c^2).\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nab + ac + bc + 2c^2 &= \\frac{ab}{2} + \\frac{ab}{2} + ac + bc + 2c^2 \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot \\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot ac \\cdot bc \\cdot 2c^2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}Also by AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}}.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} &\\ge 8 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}}{abc} \\\\\n&= 100.\n\\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a = b = 2$ and $c = 1,$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{100}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "100", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks complicated, but I notice that it has a lot of symmetry in the expression.", "I wonder if I can use that to simplify it somehow.", "First, I try to factor the numerator, since it has some common terms.", "I see that $(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2$ can be written as $(a + b + 2c)^2 + 4c(a + b)$.", "Then, I can factor out $(a + b + c)$ from the whole numerator and get\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)^2(a + b + 2c)^2 + 4c(a + b + c)(a + b)}{abc}.\\]", "This still looks messy, but I notice that the fraction has a degree of 6 in the numerator and a degree of 3 in the denominator.", "That means that if I divide both the numerator and the denominator by $(a + b + c)^3$, I will get a fraction with a degree of 0 in the numerator and a degree of -3 in the denominator.", "That means that the fraction will become a constant, independent of the values of $a, b,$ and $c$.", "To find the constant, I need to choose some convenient values for $a, b,$ and $c$ that make the fraction easy to evaluate.", "I can't choose zero, because that would make the denominator zero.", "I can't choose negative numbers, because the problem said they are positive.", "I can't choose 1, because that would make the denominator zero again.", "So, I choose $a = b = c = 2$, which gives me\n\\[\\frac{(2 + 2 + 2)^2(2 + 2 + 2 \\cdot 2)^2 + 4 \\cdot 2(2 + 2 + 2)(2 + 2)}{(2)(2)(2)(2 + 2 + 2)^3} = \\frac{216 + 192}{64} = \\frac{408}{64} = 6.375.\\]", "Since this fraction does not depend on $a, b,$ and $c$, it must be the same for any values of $a, b,$ and $c$.", "Therefore, it is the minimum value of the original expression.", "# Answer\n\n6.375"], "pre_generated_answer": "6.375", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 6.033356676999439e-07}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks complicated, but I notice that it has a lot of symmetry in the expression.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can use that to simplify it somehow.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "First, I try to factor the numerator, since it has some common terms.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I see that $(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2$ can be written as $(a + b + 2c)^2 + 4c(a + b)$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I see that $(a + b + 4c)^2 = (a + b)^2 + 8(a + b)c + 16c^2$, so I can rewrite the numerator as\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\n(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2] &= (a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b)^2 + 8(a + b)c + 16c^2] \\\\\n&= (a + b + c)[2(a + b)^2 + 8(a + b)c + 16c^2] \\\\\n&= 2(a + b + c)(a + b)[(a + b) + 4c] + 16c^2(a + b + c) \\\\\n&= 2(a + b + c)(a + b)(a + b + 4c) + 8c(a + b + 2c)(a + b + c)\n\\end{aligned}\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I see that $(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 = (a + b + 2c)^2 + (2c)^2 = (a + b + 2c + 2c)(a + b + 2c - 2c) = (a + b + 4c)(a + b)$", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I see that $(a + b)^2$ is a factor of both terms there, so I can write the numerator as\n\\[(a + b + c)(a + b)^2[1 + (1 + 4c/(a+b))^2].\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I see that $a + b$ is a common factor of both terms in the brackets, so I can pull it out.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 521868, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "cd63d345-bd43-4cbe-aa3f-3b2cd57f0a75", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:26:02.993753", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]Find the range of $f(x).$  All functions are in radians.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we claim that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\nNote that\n\\[\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\right) = \\cos (\\arccos x) = x.\\]Furthermore, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\arcsin x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ so $0 \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\le \\pi.$  Therefore,\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x = \\arccos x,\\]so $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\arccos x$ and $\\beta = \\arcsin x,$ so $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 \\\\\n&= \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\beta \\right)^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\beta^2 - \\pi \\beta + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\left( \\beta - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\beta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse trigonometric functions that take values from -1 to 1 and output angles from 0 to pi and from -pi/2 to pi/2, respectively.", "So, if I plug in -1 or 1 into f(x), I get\n\\[f(-1) = (\\arccos(-1))^2 + (\\arcsin(-1))^2 = \\pi^2 + \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\]\nand\n\\[f(1) = (\\arccos(1))^2 + (\\arcsin(1))^2 = 0^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{4}.\\]", "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), respectively, because both arccosine and arcsine are decreasing functions on their domains, so their squares are also decreasing.", "To confirm this, I can take the derivative of f(x) and check that it is always negative on the interval (-1,1):\n\\[f'(x) = 2\\arccos(x)\\cdot\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + 2\\arcsin(x)\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} = -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\left(\\arccos(x) - \\arcsin(x)\\right).\\]", "Since $\\arccos(x) - \\arcsin(x) = \\pi/2$ for all x in (-1,1), the derivative is always negative, which means f(x) is always decreasing.", "Therefore, the range of f(x) is the interval from $\\pi^2/4$ to $5\\pi^2/4$.", "# Answer\n\n\\left[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\right]"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\left[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\right]", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.024771194599947324}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse trigonometric functions that take values from -1 to 1 and output angles from 0 to pi and from -pi/2 to pi/2, respectively.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, if I plug in -1 or 1 into f(x), I get\n\\[f(-1) = (\\arccos(-1))^2 + (\\arcsin(-1))^2 = \\pi^2 + \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\]\nand\n\\[f(1) = (\\arccos(1))^2 + (\\arcsin(1))^2 = 0^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{4}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), respectively, because both arccosine and arcsine are decreasing functions on their domains, so their squares are also decreasing.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), because the arccosine and arcsine functions are decreasing and increasing, respectively, on the interval [-1, 1], and their squares are nonnegative.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the largest and smallest possible outputs of f(x), because the arccosine and arcsine functions are decreasing and increasing on the interval from -1 to 1, respectively. This means that as x increases from -1 to 1, one of the terms in f(x) decreases while the other one increases, so the sum of the squares remains between these two extreme values.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the maximum and minimum values of f(x), respectively, because as x varies from -1 to 1, the arccosine and arcsine functions decrease and increase, respectively, and their squares are always positive.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the endpoints of the range of f(x), but I need to check if f(x) attains every value between them, or if there are any gaps.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 834124, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6fe1408f-2ad0-4a74-8dab-4628da096e49", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:25:37.089193", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]Find the range of $f(x).$  All functions are in radians.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we claim that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\nNote that\n\\[\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\right) = \\cos (\\arccos x) = x.\\]Furthermore, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\arcsin x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ so $0 \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\le \\pi.$  Therefore,\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x = \\arccos x,\\]so $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\arccos x$ and $\\beta = \\arcsin x,$ so $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 \\\\\n&= \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\beta \\right)^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\beta^2 - \\pi \\beta + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\left( \\beta - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\beta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find the set of all possible values that $f(x)$ can take, given that $x$ is a real number.", "I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverses of the cosine and sine functions, respectively, and that they are defined only for $-1 \\le x \\le 1$.", "I also know that the cosine and sine functions are periodic, and that they have a maximum value of 1 and a minimum value of -1 on the interval $[0, \\pi]$.", "So, if I plug in the endpoints of the domain of $f(x)$, I get\n\\[f(-1) = (\\arccos(-1))^2 + (\\arcsin(-1))^2 = (\\pi)^2 + (-\\frac{\\pi}{2})^2 = \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4},\\]\nand\n\\[f(1) = (\\arccos(1))^2 + (\\arcsin(1))^2 = (0)^2 + (\\frac{\\pi}{2})^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{4}.\\]", "These are the maximum and minimum values of $f(x)$, respectively, because $f(x)$ is a sum of two non-negative functions that decrease and increase, respectively, as $x$ increases from -1 to 1.", "Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ is the interval $[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}]$.", "# Answer\n\n[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}]"], "pre_generated_answer": "[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}]", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.019819745256151874}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find the set of all possible values that $f(x)$ can take, given that $x$ is a real number.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverses of the cosine and sine functions, respectively, and that they are defined only for $-1 \\le x \\le 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also know that the cosine and sine functions are periodic, and that they have a maximum value of 1 and a minimum value of -1 on the interval $[0, \\pi]$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I also know that the arccosine function ranges from $0$ to $\\pi$, while the arcsine function ranges from $-\\pi/2$ to $\\pi/2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also know that the arccosine and arcsine functions return angles in the intervals $[0, \\pi]$ and $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$, respectively.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, the first thing I can say is that $f(x)$ is defined only for $-1 \\le x \\le 1$, and that it takes the form $f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2$ for such $x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also know that the arccosine and arcsine functions have ranges of $[0, \\pi]$ and $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$, respectively, and that they are both decreasing on $[0, 1]$ and increasing on $[-1, 0]$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 709667, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "32b4241c-8316-48f0-8bdc-30c3ba23ee96", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:24:52.939395", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "There are an infinite number of vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix}.$  Then\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix},\\]so\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 5b + 2c \\\\ -5a - c \\\\ -2a + b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]Comparing the components, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n5b + 2c &= 90, \\\\\n-5a - c &= 30, \\\\\n-2a + b &= 30.\n\\end{align*}From the second equation, $c = -5a - 30.$  From the third equation, $b = 2a + 30.$  We want to minimize the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v},$ which is equivalent to minimizing\n\\[a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = a^2 + (2a + 30)^2 + (-5a - 30)^2 = 30a^2 + 420a + 1800.\\]Completing the square, we get $30 (a + 7)^2 + 330,$ so the magnitude is minimized when $a = -7.$  Then $b = 16$ and $c = 5,$ so the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ we seek is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the cross product of two vectors is a vector that is perpendicular to both of them, so I can visualize the given equation as saying that $\\mathbf{v}$ lies on a plane that is orthogonal to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$, and the result of the cross product is a vector that points in the direction of $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$.", "I also know that the magnitude of the cross product is equal to the product of the magnitudes of the two vectors and the sine of the angle between them, so I can write\n\\[|\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}| \\cdot |\\mathbf{v}| \\cdot \\sin \\theta = |\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}|\\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$.", "Simplifying, I get\n\\[\\sqrt{30} \\cdot |\\mathbf{v}| \\cdot \\sin \\theta = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "I want to minimize $|\\mathbf{v}|$, so I need to maximize $\\sin \\theta$.", "The maximum value of $\\sin \\theta$ is 1, which occurs when $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.", "This means that $\\mathbf{v}$ must be parallel to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$, since it is orthogonal to $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ and has the same direction as the cross product.", "So I can write $\\mathbf{v} = k \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$ for some scalar $k$, and plug it into the equation\n\\[\\sqrt{30} \\cdot |k \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}| \\cdot \\sin 90^\\circ = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "Simplifying, I get\n\\[30 k \\sqrt{30} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "Solving for $k$, I get\n\\[k = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\]", "So the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that has the smallest magnitude is\n\\[\\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9} \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}\\]\n\n# Answer\n\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9} \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9} \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.016121762971095962}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the cross product of two vectors is a vector that is perpendicular to both of them, so I can visualize the given equation as saying that $\\mathbf{v}$ lies on a plane that is orthogonal to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$, and the result of the cross product is a vector that points in the direction of $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I also know that the magnitude of the cross product is equal to the product of the magnitudes of the two vectors and the sine of the angle between them, so I can write\n\\[|\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}| \\cdot |\\mathbf{v}| \\cdot \\sin \\theta = |\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}|\\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[\\sqrt{30} \\cdot |\\mathbf{v}| \\cdot \\sin \\theta = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I want to minimize $|\\mathbf{v}|$, so I need to maximize $\\sin \\theta$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The maximum value of $\\sin \\theta$ is 1, which occurs when $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $\\mathbf{v}$ must be parallel to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$, since it is orthogonal to $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ and has the same direction as the cross product.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I can write $\\mathbf{v} = k \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$ for some scalar $k$, and plug it into the equation\n\\[\\sqrt{30} \\cdot |k \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}| \\cdot \\sin 90^\\circ = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[30 k \\sqrt{30} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[|k| \\cdot 10 \\sqrt{30} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[k \\cdot \\sqrt{30} \\cdot 10 \\sqrt{3} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[\\sqrt{30} \\cdot |k| \\cdot 10 \\sqrt{3} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get\n\\[k \\sqrt{30} \\cdot \\sqrt{90^2 + 30^2 + 30^2} = 10 \\sqrt{27}\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 504187, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6bc09742-9848-409e-bac0-ad7f7aa7216c", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:24:09.126871", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a + b \\ge 2 \\sqrt{ab},\\]so $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab.$\n\nAlso by AM-GM,\n\\[(a + 2c) + (b + 2c) \\ge 2 \\sqrt{(a + 2c)(b + 2c)},\\]so $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c).$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 &\\ge 4ab + 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c) \\\\\n&= 8ab + 8ac + 8bc + 16c^2 \\\\\n&= 8(ab + ac + bc + 2c^2).\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nab + ac + bc + 2c^2 &= \\frac{ab}{2} + \\frac{ab}{2} + ac + bc + 2c^2 \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot \\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot ac \\cdot bc \\cdot 2c^2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}Also by AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}}.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} &\\ge 8 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}}{abc} \\\\\n&= 100.\n\\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a = b = 2$ and $c = 1,$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{100}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "100", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression has a lot of terms involving $a + b$, so I wonder if it would help to make a substitution.", "Let $x = a + b$.", "Then I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\frac{x(x + c)(x^2 + (x + 4c)^2)}{ac(x - a)}.\\]", "Now I see that the denominator is a bit messy, and it has a factor of $x - a$ that could be negative.", "I recall that the arithmetic mean-geometric mean inequality (AM-GM) says that for any nonnegative numbers $p$ and $q$, we have\n\\[\\frac{p + q}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{pq},\\]\nwith equality if and only if $p = q$.", "I wonder if I can apply this inequality to the denominator somehow.", "I notice that $ac(x - a) = (a\\sqrt{c})(\\sqrt{c}(x - a))$, and these are both nonnegative numbers.", "So by AM-GM, I have\n\\[\\frac{a\\sqrt{c} + \\sqrt{c}(x - a)}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ac(x - a)},\\]\nwith equality if and only if $a\\sqrt{c} = \\sqrt{c}(x - a)$, or $x = 2a$.", "This means that the denominator is minimized when $x = 2a$, and the minimum value is\n\\[\\frac{a\\sqrt{c} + \\sqrt{c}(x - a)}{2} = \\frac{a\\sqrt{c}}{2}.\\]", "So the original expression is maximized when $x = 2a$, and the maximum value is\n\\[\\frac{x(x + c)(x^2 + (x + 4c)^2)}{\\frac{a\\sqrt{c}}{2}} = \\frac{2x(x + c)(x^2 + (x + 4c)^2)}{a\\sqrt{c}}.\\]", "Now I want to find the minimum value of this expression, which is equivalent to finding the minimum value of the original expression.", "I notice that the expression is symmetric in $a$ and $c$, so I can assume without loss of generality that $a \\le c$.", "Then I have $x = a + b \\le a + c$, and $x^2 + (x + 4c)^2 \\ge (a + c)^2 + (a + c + 4c)^2 = 26c^2 + 10ac + a^2$.", "So the expression is at least\n\\[\\frac{2x(x + c)(26c^2 + 10ac + a^2)}{a\\sqrt{c}}.\\]", "I want to minimize this expression over the possible values of $x$ and $a$, subject to the constraint that $a \\le c$.", "I notice that the expression is increasing in both $x$ and $a$, so it will be minimized when $x$ and $a$ are as small as possible.", "Since $x = a + b$, the smallest possible value of $x$ is when $b = 0$, so $x = a$.", "Then the expression becomes\n\\[\\frac{2a(a + c)(26c^2 + 10ac + a^2)}{a\\sqrt{c}} = \\frac{2(a + c)(26c^2 + 10ac + a^2)}{\\sqrt{c}}.\\]", "This expression is still increasing in $a$, so it will be minimized when $a$ is as small as possible.", "Since $a$ is a positive real number, the smallest possible value of $a$ is when $a$ approaches zero.", "As $a$ approaches zero, the expression approaches\n\\[\\frac{2(0 + c)(26c^2 + 10(0)c + 0^2)}{\\sqrt{c}} = \\frac{52c^3}{\\sqrt{c}} = 52c^{5/2}.\\]", "Therefore, the minimum value of the original expression is $52c^{5/2}$.", "# Answer\n\n52c^{5/2}"], "pre_generated_answer": "52c^{5/2}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 5.7097530391727906e-15}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the expression has a lot of terms involving $a + b$, so I wonder if it would help to make a substitution.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Let $x = a + b$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\frac{x(x + c)(x^2 + (x + 4c)^2)}{ac(x - a)}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Then the expression becomes $\\frac{x(x + c)[x^2 + (x + 4c)^2]}{abc}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Then the expression becomes\n\\[\\frac{(x + c)[x^2 + (x + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Then the expression becomes\n\\[\\frac{x(x + c)[x^2 + (x + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Then I can rewrite the expression as\n\\[\\frac{x(x + c)[x^2 + (x + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 271361, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "bde20ef8-f6b3-4e84-b018-dc6aba6c80ee", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:23:54.946171", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]Find the range of $f(x).$  All functions are in radians.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we claim that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\nNote that\n\\[\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\right) = \\cos (\\arccos x) = x.\\]Furthermore, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\arcsin x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ so $0 \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\le \\pi.$  Therefore,\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x = \\arccos x,\\]so $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\arccos x$ and $\\beta = \\arcsin x,$ so $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 \\\\\n&= \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\beta \\right)^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\beta^2 - \\pi \\beta + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\left( \\beta - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\beta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse functions of the cosine and sine functions, respectively.", "This means that for any $x$ in the domain of arccosine or arcsine, I have $\\cos(\\arccos x) = x$ and $\\sin(\\arcsin x) = x.$", "Using these identities, I can rewrite $f(x)$ as\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 = (\\cos^{-1} x)^2 + (\\sin^{-1} x)^2 = \\cos^2(\\cos^{-1} x) + \\sin^2(\\sin^{-1} x).\\]", "Now, I recall the Pythagorean identity, which states that for any angle $\\theta$, I have $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1.$", "Applying this identity to $f(x)$, I get\n\\[f(x) = \\cos^2(\\cos^{-1} x) + \\sin^2(\\sin^{-1} x) = 1.\\]", "This means that $f(x)$ is a constant function, and its range is just the single value $1.$", "# Answer\n\n1"], "pre_generated_answer": "1", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 1.645863881616624e-05}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse functions of the cosine and sine functions, respectively.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that for any $x$ in the domain of arccosine or arcsine, I have $\\cos(\\arccos x) = x$ and $\\sin(\\arcsin x) = x.$", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Using these identities, I can rewrite $f(x)$ as\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 = (\\cos^{-1} x)^2 + (\\sin^{-1} x)^2 = \\cos^2(\\cos^{-1} x) + \\sin^2(\\sin^{-1} x).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I also know that the cosine and sine functions are periodic and bounded between $-1$ and $1$, so the domain of arccosine and arcsine is the closed interval $[-1,1].$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also know that the cosine and sine functions are periodic with a period of $2\\pi$, and they have a range of $[-1,1].$", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To find the range of $f(x),$ I want to know the minimum and maximum possible values of $f(x)$ as $x$ varies in the domain of both arccosine and arcsine.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, if I apply the cosine and sine functions to both sides of $f(x)$, I get\n\\[\\cos(f(x)) = \\cos((\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2) = \\cos((\\arccos x)^2)\\cos((\\arcsin x)^2) - \\sin((\\arccos x)^2)\\sin((\\arcsin x)^2).\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 470674, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "72614629-fdfb-40af-a21a-cd9c8cf0736a", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:22:00.693934", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $f(x)=\\left\\lfloor\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x\\right\\rfloor$ be a function that is defined for all values of $x$ in $[0,\\infty)$ such that $f(x)$ is a real number. How many distinct values exist in the range of $f(x)$?", "ground_truth_solution": "Since $-\\frac58$ is a negative number, $f(x)$ is only defined for integer values of $x$, and will alternate between positive and negative values. Additionally, $\\left|-\\frac58\\right|< 1$, so $|f(x)|$ will continually decrease and approach 0 as $x$ increases in the interval $x\\ge0$. Therefore, the largest positive value will occur at $x=0$, giving us the positive upper bound of $\\left\\lfloor\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^0\\right\\rfloor=1$. The negative value that is greatest in magnitude then occurs at the next integer value of $x$: $x=1$, giving us the negative lower bound of $\\left\\lfloor\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^1\\right\\rfloor=-1$. This tells us that $-1 \\le f(x) \\le 1$. Since the $f(x)$ must be an integer, the only possible distinct values contained in the range are -1, 0, and 1. This gives us a total of $\\boxed{3}$ values of $f(x)$ when $x\\ge0$.", "ground_truth_answer": "3", "pre_generated_steps": ["The notation $\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor$ means the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$, also known as the floor function.", "For example, $\\left\\lfloor 3.7 \\right\\rfloor = 3$ and $\\left\\lfloor -1.2 \\right\\rfloor = -2$.", "To find the range of $f(x)$, I need to consider how the expression $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ behaves as $x$ varies from $0$ to $\\infty$.", "When $x=0$, any nonzero number raised to the zero power is $1$.", "So $f(0) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^0 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1 \\right\\rfloor = 1$.", "When $x$ is a positive integer, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ alternates between positive and negative values, depending on whether $x$ is even or odd.", "For example, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{64}$ and $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^3 = -\\frac{125}{512}$.", "The magnitude of these values decreases as $x$ increases, since $\\left|\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x\\right| = \\left(\\frac58\\right)^x$ and $\\frac58$ is less than $1$.", "When $x$ is a positive integer, $f(x)$ is the floor of $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$.", "This means that $f(x)$ is either equal to $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ if it is already an integer, or one less than the next integer above $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ if it is not.", "For example, $f(2) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^2 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25}{64} \\right\\rfloor = 0$ and $f(3) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^3 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor -\\frac{125}{512} \\right\\rfloor = -1$.", "As $x$ increases, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, but they never go below $-1$.", "This is because $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ approaches $0$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, and the floor of any positive number is at least $0$ and the floor of any negative number is at least $-1$.", "When $x$ is not an integer, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ is not a real number, so $f(x)$ is not defined.", "This is because raising a negative number to a non-integer power involves taking roots of negative numbers, which are complex numbers.", "For example, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^{\\frac12} = \\sqrt{-\\frac58}$, which is not a real number.", "Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ consists of the integer values that $f(x)$ takes when $x$ is a positive integer or zero.", "These values are $1, 0, -1$.", "There are $3$ distinct values in the range of $f(x)$.", "# Answer\n\n3"], "pre_generated_answer": "3", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0004051991641027882}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "The notation $\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor$ means the greatest integer less than or equal to $x$, also known as the floor function.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, $\\left\\lfloor 3.7 \\right\\rfloor = 3$ and $\\left\\lfloor -1.2 \\right\\rfloor = -2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the range of $f(x)$, I need to consider how the expression $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ behaves as $x$ varies from $0$ to $\\infty$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "When $x=0$, any nonzero number raised to the zero power is $1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So $f(0) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^0 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1 \\right\\rfloor = 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "When $x$ is a positive integer, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ alternates between positive and negative values, depending on whether $x$ is even or odd.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{64}$ and $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^3 = -\\frac{125}{512}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The magnitude of these values decreases as $x$ increases, since $\\left|\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x\\right| = \\left(\\frac58\\right)^x$ and $\\frac58$ is less than $1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "When $x$ is a positive integer, $f(x)$ is the floor of $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $f(x)$ is either equal to $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ if it is already an integer, or one less than the next integer above $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ if it is not.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, $f(2) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^2 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{25}{64} \\right\\rfloor = 0$ and $f(3) = \\left\\lfloor \\left(-\\frac58\\right)^3 \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor -\\frac{125}{512} \\right\\rfloor = -1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "As $x$ increases, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, but they never go below $-1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is because $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ approaches $0$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, and the floor of any positive number is at least $0$ and the floor of any negative number is at least $-1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "When $x$ is not an integer, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^x$ is not a real number, so $f(x)$ is not defined.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is because raising a negative number to a non-integer power involves taking roots of negative numbers, which are complex numbers.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "For example, $\\left(-\\frac58\\right)^{\\frac12} = \\sqrt{-\\frac58}$, which is not a real number.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, the range of $f(x)$ consists of the integer values that $f(x)$ takes when $x$ is a positive integer or zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "These values are $1, 0, -1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "There are $3$ distinct values in the range of $f(x)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n3", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 1006310, "finish_reason": "solution"}}
{"labeler": "f8eeff50-2b57-4d80-a977-e0f8af62018e", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:20:46.651905", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be positive real numbers.  Find the minimum value of\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc}.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By AM-GM,\n\\[a + b \\ge 2 \\sqrt{ab},\\]so $(a + b)^2 \\ge 4ab.$\n\nAlso by AM-GM,\n\\[(a + 2c) + (b + 2c) \\ge 2 \\sqrt{(a + 2c)(b + 2c)},\\]so $(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c).$\n\nHence,\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2 &\\ge 4ab + 4(a + 2c)(b + 2c) \\\\\n&= 8ab + 8ac + 8bc + 16c^2 \\\\\n&= 8(ab + ac + bc + 2c^2).\n\\end{align*}By AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\nab + ac + bc + 2c^2 &= \\frac{ab}{2} + \\frac{ab}{2} + ac + bc + 2c^2 \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot \\frac{ab}{2} \\cdot ac \\cdot bc \\cdot 2c^2} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}Also by AM-GM,\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{a}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac{b}{2} + c \\\\\n&\\ge 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{a}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot \\frac{b}{2} \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}}.\n\\end{align*}Hence,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} &\\ge 8 \\cdot \\frac{5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^2 b^2 c}{16}} \\cdot 5 \\sqrt[5]{\\frac{a^3 b^3 c^4}{2}}}{abc} \\\\\n&= 100.\n\\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a = b = 2$ and $c = 1,$ so the minimum value is $\\boxed{100}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "100", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a complicated algebraic expression, so I wonder if there is some symmetry or factorization that can simplify it.", "I notice that the numerator has a common factor of $(a + b)$, so I can pull that out and write\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} = \\frac{(a + b)^2(a + b + c)[1 + (1 + 4c/(a + b))^2]}{abc}.\\]", "This looks a bit better, but I still have to deal with the term $(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2$.", "I wonder if there is a way to make this term smaller by choosing $a, b,$ and $c$ appropriately.", "I recall that the arithmetic mean of two numbers is always greater than or equal to their geometric mean, that is, $(x + y)/2 \\ge \\sqrt{xy}$ for any nonnegative $x$ and $y$.", "If I apply this to $x = a + b$ and $y = 4c$, I get\n\\[\\frac{a + b + 4c}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{(a + b)(4c)}.\\]", "Squaring both sides, I get\n\\[(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + b)(4c) = 16(a + b)c.\\]", "Dividing by $(a + b)^2$, I get\n\\[\\left(1 + \\frac{4c}{a + b}\\right)^2 \\ge \\frac{16c}{a + b}.\\]", "This means that the term $(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2$ is always greater than or equal to $16c/(a + b)$, and the equality holds when $a + b = 4c$ or when $a = b = c = 0$.", "But since $a, b,$ and $c$ are positive, the equality only holds when $a + b = 4c$.", "Therefore, to minimize the expression, I want to choose $a, b,$ and $c$ so that $a + b = 4c$.", "In that case, the expression becomes\n\\[\\frac{(a + b)^2(a + b + c)[1 + (1 + 4c/(a + b))^2]}{abc} = \\frac{(4c)^2(5c)[1 + (2)^2]}{abc} = \\frac{80c^3[5]}{abc} = 400.\\]", "So the minimum value of the expression is 400, and it occurs when $a + b = 4c$.", "# Answer\n\n400"], "pre_generated_answer": "400", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.04002461599293835}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a complicated algebraic expression, so I wonder if there is some symmetry or factorization that can simplify it.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the numerator has a common factor of $(a + b)$, so I can pull that out and write\n\\[\\frac{(a + b + c)[(a + b)^2 + (a + b + 4c)^2]}{abc} = \\frac{(a + b)^2(a + b + c)[1 + (1 + 4c/(a + b))^2]}{abc}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This looks a bit better, but I still have to deal with the term $(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if there is a way to make this term smaller by choosing $a, b,$ and $c$ appropriately.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that the arithmetic mean of two numbers is always greater than or equal to their geometric mean, that is, $(x + y)/2 \\ge \\sqrt{xy}$ for any nonnegative $x$ and $y$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I apply this to $x = a + b$ and $y = 4c$, I get\n\\[\\frac{a + b + 4c}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{(a + b)(4c)}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Squaring both sides, I get\n\\[(a + b + 4c)^2 \\ge 4(a + b)(4c) = 16(a + b)c.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Dividing by $(a + b)^2$, I get\n\\[\\left(1 + \\frac{4c}{a + b}\\right)^2 \\ge \\frac{16c}{a + b}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the term $(1 + 4c/(a + b))^2$ is always greater than or equal to $16c/(a + b)$, and the equality holds when $a + b = 4c$ or when $a = b = c = 0$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But since $a, b,$ and $c$ are positive, the equality only holds when $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Therefore, to minimize the expression, I want to choose $a, b,$ and $c$ so that $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "So, to minimize the original expression, I should try to make $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This suggests that the expression I want to minimize can only take its minimum value when $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, to minimize the expression, I should try to make $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "So, to minimize the original expression, I should choose $a, b,$ and $c$ so that $a + b = 4c$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 180933, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "6c655923-0179-4f96-afbc-f52af3de908d", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:20:04.993782", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]Find the range of $f(x).$  All functions are in radians.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we claim that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\nNote that\n\\[\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\right) = \\cos (\\arccos x) = x.\\]Furthermore, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\arcsin x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ so $0 \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\le \\pi.$  Therefore,\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x = \\arccos x,\\]so $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\arccos x$ and $\\beta = \\arcsin x,$ so $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 \\\\\n&= \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\beta \\right)^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\beta^2 - \\pi \\beta + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\left( \\beta - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\beta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse trigonometric functions that take values from -1 to 1 and output angles from 0 to pi and from -pi/2 to pi/2, respectively.", "So, if I plug in -1 or 1 into f(x), I get\n\\[f(-1) = (\\arccos(-1))^2 + (\\arcsin(-1))^2 = \\pi^2 + \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\]\nand\n\\[f(1) = (\\arccos(1))^2 + (\\arcsin(1))^2 = 0^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{4}.\\]", "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), respectively, because both arccosine and arcsine are decreasing functions on their domains, so their squares are also decreasing.", "To confirm this, I can take the derivative of f(x) and check that it is always negative on the interval (-1,1):\n\\[f'(x) = 2\\arccos(x)\\cdot\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} + 2\\arcsin(x)\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}} = -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\left(\\arccos(x) - \\arcsin(x)\\right).\\]", "Since $\\arccos(x) - \\arcsin(x) = \\pi/2$ for all x in (-1,1), the derivative is always negative, which means f(x) is always decreasing.", "Therefore, the range of f(x) is the interval from $\\pi^2/4$ to $5\\pi^2/4$.", "# Answer\n\n\\left[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\right]"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\left[\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\right]", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.024771194599947324}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the arccosine and arcsine functions are inverse trigonometric functions that take values from -1 to 1 and output angles from 0 to pi and from -pi/2 to pi/2, respectively.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So, if I plug in -1 or 1 into f(x), I get\n\\[f(-1) = (\\arccos(-1))^2 + (\\arcsin(-1))^2 = \\pi^2 + \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{5\\pi^2}{4}\\]\nand\n\\[f(1) = (\\arccos(1))^2 + (\\arcsin(1))^2 = 0^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{4}.\\]", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), respectively, because both arccosine and arcsine are decreasing functions on their domains, so their squares are also decreasing.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "These are the maximum and minimum values of f(x), respectively, because both arccosine and arcsine are decreasing functions on the interval [-1, 1], so their squares are also decreasing functions.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the maximum and minimum values of f(x), because the arccosine and arcsine functions are decreasing and increasing, respectively, on the interval [-1, 1], and their squares are nonnegative.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the maximum and minimum values of f(x), since both arccosine and arcsine are strictly decreasing and increasing functions, respectively, on their domains, and their squares are nonnegative.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "These are the minimum and maximum values of f(x), because I can use the identity\n\\[\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\]\nto rewrite f(x) as\n\\[f(x) = \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x\\right)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 476799, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "9aa704c5-7f0a-4719-8cf3-931a83832a4b", "timestamp": "2022-11-12T21:18:40.003577", "generation": 6, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]Find the range of $f(x).$  All functions are in radians.", "ground_truth_solution": "First, we claim that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for all $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\nNote that\n\\[\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\right) = \\cos (\\arccos x) = x.\\]Furthermore, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\arcsin x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ so $0 \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x \\le \\pi.$  Therefore,\n\\[\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x = \\arccos x,\\]so $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\arccos x$ and $\\beta = \\arcsin x,$ so $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nf(x) &= (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 \\\\\n&= \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\beta \\right)^2 + \\beta^2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\beta^2 - \\pi \\beta + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} \\\\\n&= 2 \\left( \\beta - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)^2 + \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\n\\end{align*}Since $-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\le \\beta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},$ the range of $f(x)$ is $\\boxed{\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\left[ \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{5 \\pi^2}{4} \\right]", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the domain of both arccos and arcsin is the interval [-1, 1], so I can start by assuming that x is in that interval.", "Next, I recall that arccos and arcsin are inverse functions, meaning that arccos(arcsin(x)) = x and arcsin(arccos(x)) = x for any x in [-1, 1].", "I can use this fact to simplify f(x) by applying the Pythagorean identity: sin^2(t) + cos^2(t) = 1.", "If I let t = arccos x, then I get\n\n\\[f(x) = (\\arccos x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2 = t^2 + (\\arcsin(\\cos t))^2 = t^2 + (\\pi/2 - t)^2\\]\n\nby the complementary angle identity for arccos and arcsin.", "Now, I can expand the square and simplify:\n\n\\[f(x) = t^2 + (\\pi/2 - t)^2 = t^2 + \\pi^2/4 - \\pi t + t^2 = 2t^2 - \\pi t + \\pi^2/4.\\]", "This is a quadratic function in t, which has a minimum value at the vertex.", "To find the vertex, I use the formula t = -b/2a, where a = 2 and b = -\\pi.", "This gives me t = \\pi/4.", "To find the minimum value of f(x), I plug in t = \\pi/4 and simplify:\n\n\\[f(x) = 2(\\pi/4)^2 - \\pi (\\pi/4) + \\pi^2/4 = \\pi^2/8 - \\pi^2/4 + \\pi^2/4 = \\pi^2/8.\\]", "This means that f(x) is always greater than or equal to \\pi^2/8 for any x in [-1, 1].", "To find the maximum value of f(x), I need to check the endpoints of the domain, x = -1 and x = 1.", "If x = -1, then t = arccos(-1) = \\pi, and\n\n\\[f(-1) = 2(\\pi)^2 - \\pi (\\pi) + \\pi^2/4 = \\pi^2 - \\pi^2 + \\pi^2/4 = \\pi^2/4.\\]", "If x = 1, then t = arccos(1) = 0, and\n\n\\[f(1) = 2(0)^2 - \\pi (0) + \\pi^2/4 = 0 - 0 + \\pi^2/4 = \\pi^2/4.\\]", "This means that f(x) is always less than or equal to \\pi^2/4 for any x in [-1, 1].", "Therefore, the range of f(x) is the interval [\\pi^2/8, \\pi^2/4].", "# Answer\n\n[\\pi^2/8, \\pi^2/4]"], "pre_generated_answer": "[\\pi^2/8, \\pi^2/4]", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.01273772602524107}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the domain of both arccos and arcsin is the interval [-1, 1], so I can start by assuming that x is in that interval.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Next, I recall that arccos and arcsin are inverse functions, meaning that arccos(arcsin(x)) = x and arcsin(arccos(x)) = x for any x in [-1, 1].", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Next, I recall the identity cos^2(theta) + sin^2(theta) = 1, which is true for any angle theta.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also know that arccos and arcsin are inverse functions, so for any x in [-1, 1], arccos x and arcsin x are complementary angles, meaning that their sum is pi/2 radians.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Next, I recall that arccos and arcsin are complementary angles, meaning that their sum is $\\pi/2$ for any x in their domain. That is, $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\pi/2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I also recall that the relationship between arccos and arcsin is that $\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ for any x in [-1, 1]. This is because the cosine and sine functions are complementary to each other on the unit circle.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 325417, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
